:"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Introduction: Orthostatic intolerance (OI) is a barrier for early mobilization and same-day discharge after total hip arthroplasty (THA), with an estimated 30 % of patients affected within the first 6 h after surgery. Since repeated mobilization is known to be valuable in non-surgical OI conditions, we aimed to evaluate the feasibility of an early standardized repeated mobilization procedure as treatment of postoperative OI after THA. Methods: This study was a single-arm, two-center hypothesis-generating feasibility study in patients undergoing THA. At first mobilization 4 h after surgery patients were categorized as having orthostatic tolerance (OT) or OI. Patients with OI underwent an hourly standardized repeated mobilization procedure until achieving OT or reaching 8 h post-surgery. Results: Of 84 patients screened for OI, 25 (30 %) had OI 4 h postoperatively. Four patients left the study before achieving OT. Of the 21 completing the study per protocol, 16 patients achieved OT at 5 h and the remaining 5 achieved OT at 6 h postoperatively. Discussion: Early repeated mobilization in patients with postoperative OI was feasible and potentially valuable after THA. This hypothesis-generating study calls for future large-scale studies in surgical patients with OI.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Frandsen, Mikkel Nicklas","Mehlsen, Jesper","Varnum, Claus","Lindberg-Larsen, Martin","Foss, Nicolai Bang","Kehlet, Henrik"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-812263"],"filters":{"added":"2025-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Frandsen, Mikkel Nicklas"],"keyword":["Enhanced recovery after surgery","Fast track surgery","Total hip arthroplasty"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"feasibility of repeated early mobilization in orthostatic intolerance after total hip arthroplasty","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijotn.2025.101187","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-812263","identifier":[{"id":"105003932735","type":"scopus"},{"id":"40319814","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["sdu-4620d0c2-5739-4b3b-a01e-7f985d5191ca"],"keyword":["Enhanced recovery after surgery","Fast track surgery","Postoperative mobilization","Postoperative orthostatic intolerance","Total hip arthroplasty"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-E27B2B9ACD0730BDB0AE09D103DFCDB4"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijotn.2025.101187","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/289636303/Open_Access_Version.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"capital region of denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CAP","l3nl":"Capital Region of Denmark","l3nn":"Capital Region of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Capital Region of Denmark","namevar":["Rigshospitalet"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CAP","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Regional Health Research","SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Regional Health Research, IRS - Lillebælt Hospital, Research Unit of Orthopaedics (Vejle)"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Mikkel Nick