24,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["39887674"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1111/aas.14578","issn":["00015172"],"issue":"3","journal":"Acta Anaesthesiologica Scandinavica","paperid":"e14578","publisher":"Blackwell Munksgaard","status":"p","title":"Acta Anaesthesiologica Scandinavica","volume":"69"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85216650191"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Miscellaneous","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","Central Denmark Region","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Miscellaneous","North Denmark Region","Other Hospitals","Other Research","Region Zealand","Region of Southern Denmark","Universities","University of Copenhagen","University of Southern Denmark","capio privathospital"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-05d84a4f-dd81-4eb8-bf1f-4cc4824e5104","ku-7cf01493-e179-4b5e-b23e-813a53320dfd","sdu-925b5685-305a-4bd6-bb63-2ab6a0fe008b"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University","KU University of Copenhagen","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"The adverse effects with ibuprofen after major orthopedic surgeries: A protocol for the PERISAFE randomized clinical trial","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S03-086.04-792763","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Purpose: To improve the understanding of what influences the motivation of children and adolescents diagnosed with cancer to engage in exercise during the first six months of treatment. Materials and Methods: Qualitative design using semistructured interviews with children (6–17 years) diagnosed with cancer (n = 12) and their parents (n = 12). A deductive thematic analysis based on self-determination theory was applied. Results: Three predefined themes described different aspects of motivation for exercise during treatment. Amotivation: Treatment-related illness and fatigue causing amotivation was described as a dominant barrier. Exercise driven by negative reinforcements facilitated short-term exercise engagement but was perceived as amotivation. Controlled Regulation: Exercise regulated by exercise professionals could facilitate and introject positive experiences with exercise (i.e., ameliorated side effects) and create confidence in physical capabilities. Autonomous Self-Regulation: An autonomy-supportive approach using cocreation and age-appropriate and treatment-regulated exercise, facilitated trust, and confidentiality with exercise professionals. Conclusion: Motivation for exercise is a dynamic interplay that can be facilitated or negatively affected by treatment, parents, peers, and external regulation. Exercise interventions should use an individual and autonomy-supportive approach, encompassing treatment-related daily variations of physical capacity. Externally regulated motivation can facilitate exercise on a short-term basis when children are inactive or hesitant to engage in exercise.","author_count":9,"contributors":["Schmidt-Andersen, P.","Boensvang, N. N.","Pouplier, A.","Lykkedegn, S.","Hasle, Henrik","Müller, K.","Christensen, J.","Fridh, M. K.","Larsen, H. B."],"cuid":["S03-099.96-813528"],"filters":{"added":"2025-08","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":3,"corresponding_author":["Schmidt-Andersen, P."],"keyword":["Motivation","Pediatric exercise oncology","Self determination theory"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-SDR"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-SDR","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"exploring motivation for engaging in exercise during the first six months of childhood cancer treatment a qualitative study","src":["au","ku","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1155/ecc/8305173","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S03-099.96-813528","identifier":[{"id":"001495301200001","type":"wos"},{"id":"105006682447","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-0227dbef-efc3-4211-82b9-8664fdd5eef7","ku-99e9ecb5-44c0-456d-849c-7c1b7feb7b1a","sdu-0c1e6667-9b0b-4704-a854-f10178910455"],"keyword":["motivation","pediatric exercise oncology","self-determination theory"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-5669B35D2843B1ACA9DCE43E0D97CDFE"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/454856607/European_Journal_of_Cancer_Care_-_2025_-_Schmidt-Andersen_-_Exploring_Motivation_for_Engaging_in_Exercise_During_the_First.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1155/ecc/8305173","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/291182361/e001975.full.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"capital region of denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CAP","l3nl":"Capital Region of Denmark","l3nn":"Capital Region of Denmark"