{"abstract":"Background Globally, the increase in antimicrobial resistance is of great concern. In Denmark, the pig sector is accountable for the majority of antimicrobial usage in animals. As new-born piglets are at risk of developing infectious omphalitis, and many pigs are treated by antimicrobials within the first days of life, an early and accurate diagnosis of the disease is imperative to maintain animal welfare and reduce the antimicrobial usage. The aim of the present study was to compare histopathological and microbiological findings in piglets clinically diagnosed with and without omphalitis during the first three days after birth. Results A total of 98 case piglets with omphalitis and 98 control piglets without omphalitis, based on clinical examinations during the first three days of life, were included. Of the 196 piglets, 79 (38 cases and 41 controls) presented histopathological omphalitis. Post mortem, the volume of the umbilicus was significantly enlarged in cases compared to controls (p = 0.01). Except for a group of bacteria unidentified by matrix-assisted laser desorption ionisation time-of-flight (MALDI-TOF) (p = 0.02), no apparent association was found between histopathologically diagnosed omphalitis and the prevalence of specific pathogens (p > 0.05). The frequency of histopathologically diagnosed omphalitis tended to increase with increasing age in both cases and controls. The frequency of piglets with both histopathological omphalitis and arthritis/synovitis was significantly different among cases and controls (p = 0.05). This was due to all controls, and none of the cases, with arthritis/synovitis presented histopathological omphalitis. Conclusion The clinical differentiation between omphalitis cases and controls did not correspond to the histological diagnosis of omphalitis in zero-to-three days old piglets. An inaccurate clinical diagnosis complicates prudent use of antimicrobials in pig herds. In addition, animal welfare may be hampered in infected piglets due to lack of treatment and in healthy piglets due to the antimicrobial effect on the gut microbiome.","author_count":13,"contributors":["Blirup-Plum, Sophie Amalie","Jensen, Henrik Elvang","Hartmann, Katrine Top","Nielsen, Søren Saxmose","Pankoke, Karen","Hansen, Mette Sif","Pedersen, Ken Steen","Larsen, Inge","Nielsen, Jens Peter","Olsen, John Elmerdahl","Kudirkiene, Egle","Braunstein, Thomas Hartig","Barington, Kristiane"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-800109"],"filters":{"added":"2025-04","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-PR","DI-U"],"level3":{"PR":["DI-PR-ALL","DI-PR-OTH"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-PR-OTH","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"can infectious omphalitis in piglets be clinically diagnosed during the first three days of life","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/431112738/Can_infectious_omphalitis_in_piglets_be_clinically_diagnosed_during_the_first_three_days_of_life.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-800109","identifier":[{"id":"85218199568","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-9a65de03-4865-4bbd-92f8-ca650a604efa"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-61B36B40767DFD0D372B745EC70E82AE"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"rem","url":"https://bmcvetres.biomedcentral.com/articles/10.1186/s12917-025-04507-3","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/431112738/Can_infectious_omphalitis_in_piglets_be_clinically_diagnosed_during_the_first_three_days_of_life.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Veterinary and Animal Sciences, Pathobiological Sciences"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"other companies","l2":"DI-PR","l2n":"Private Research","l3":"DI-PR-OTH","l3nl":"Other Companies","l3nn":"Other Companies","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Other Companies","namevar":["Ø-Vet A/S"],"nora_code":"DI-PR-OTH","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Sophie Amalie","key":"blirupplumsophieamaliepau","last":"Blirup-Plum","locid":"54f7ba11-2529-4cf7-adb1-139dfa0e4b53","name":"Blirup-Plum, Sophie Amalie","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Henrik Elvang","key":"jensenhenrikelvangpau","last":"Jensen","locid":"20245e41-1eb4-4cfa-a2d6-e6851e101300","name":"Jensen, Henrik Elvang","orcid":"0000-0001-7277-7829","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Katrine Top","key":"hartmannkatrinetoppau","last":"Hartmann","locid":"24872909-edcd-41d3-ac0e-6d89e6fef67b","name":"Hartmann, Katrine Top","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Søren Saxmose","key":"nielsensørensaxmosepau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"8d00f12a-fe80-48ba-96c2-6769b91281e7","name":"Nielsen, Søren Saxmose","orcid":"0000-0003-2417-0787","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Karen","key":"pankokekarenpau","last":"Pankoke","locid":"c59c44ce-f168-448f-82d3-b444274b4fee","name":"Pankoke, Karen","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Mette Sif","key":"hansenmettesifpau","last":"Hansen","locid":"cf95e878-fe91-4715-b9f7-05fc972de6c6","name":"Hansen, Mette Sif","orcid":"0000-0002-8132-8475","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","first":"Ken Steen","key":"pedersenkensteenpau","last":"Pedersen","locid":"e7e2a10f-e368-4635-9481-f23e58c80aa8","name":"Pedersen, Ken Steen","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Inge","key":"larseningepau","last":"Larsen","locid":"82b09bf9-73d5-4c71-a3c1-01e66b0e9ebe","name":"Larsen, Inge","orcid":"0000-0003-4575-2241","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Jens Peter","key":"nielsenjenspeterpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"e550301c-f89e-4a8b-bf34-e6fc3b76ff37","name":"Nielsen, Jens Peter","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"John Elmerdahl","key":"olsenjohnelmerdahlpau","last":"Olsen","locid":"d7f792e1-ac2c-4d67-8887-3615a3a98edb","name":"Olsen, John Elmerdahl","orcid":"0000-0001-6225-6587","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Egle","key":"kudirkieneeglepau","last":"Kudirkiene","locid":"49238462-eac4-44c3-964c-3f16918f3782","name":"Kudirkiene, Egle","rank":11,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Thomas Hartig","key":"braunsteinthomashartigpau","last":"Braunstein","locid":"bcf3512f-c187-470a-9589-e975d7685151","name":"Braunstein, Thomas Hartig","rank":12,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Kristiane","key":"baringtonkristianepau","last":"Barington","locid":"0913e657-6538-4dea-a32f-5f97b7acc33c","name":"Barington, Kristiane","rank":13,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"issn":["17466148"],"journal":"BMC Veterinary Research","paperid":"58","status":"p","title":"BMC Veterinary Research","title_org":"B M C Veterinary Research","volume":"21"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85218199568"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Private Research","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-PR","DI-PR-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Other Companies","Private Research","Universities","University of Copenhagen","ø-vet a/s"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-9a65de03-4865-4bbd-92f8-ca650a604efa"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Can infectious omphalitis in piglets be clinically diagnosed during the first three days of life?","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-800109","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Background Cardiogenic shock complicating myocardial infarction (AMI-CS) is charactirized by reduced cardiac output (CO), poor end-organ perfusion, and high mortality. Current medical therapies have not significantly improved survival rates. Lactate, though often considered a toxic byproduct of anaerobic metabolism, serves as an oxygen efficient alternative, readily oxidizable substrate for the myocardium. Lactate infusion has been shown to improve CO and left ventricular ejection fraction (LVEF) in acute heart failure patients and in experimental septic shock models while the effects of treatment with lactate in AMI-CS remains unexplored. This study aimed to elucidate the cardiovascular effects of lactate infusion in a porcine model of AMI-CS. Methods In a randomized, assessor-blinded design, we studied 20 female Danish Landrace pigs (60 kg). AMI-CS was induced by left main coronary artery microsphere injections. Predefined criteria for AMI-CS were a 30% reduction in CO or mixed venous saturation (SvO2). Pigs were randomized to receive either intravenous lactate infusion or a matched control saline for 180 minutes (n=10 per group). Hemodynamic measurements were obtained by right and left heart catheterisation. The primary endpoint was CO through 180 minutes of lactate infusion compared with control. Results Compared with control, lactate infusion increased arterial lactate levels increased by 5.1 mmol/L (95% CI 4.4 to 5.8; P<0.001) (Fig. 1). CO increased by 0.7 L/min (95% CI 0.4 to 1.0; P<0.001), driven by increased stroke volume of 7 mL (95% CI 3 to 11; P=0.003), while heart rate was unchanged (P=0.71) (Fig. 2). SvO2 increased by 7 %-points (95% CI 2 to 12; P=0.018) while the venous-to-arterial CO2 gap (P(v-a)CO2 decreased (P=0.004), indicating improved tissue perfusion. Pulmonary vascular resistance was reduced (P=0.031), while systemic vascular resistance remained unaltered (P=0.275). Systemic and pulmonary blood pressures and rate-pressure-product showed no significant differences between the two treatments. LV end-systolic elastance (contractility), was higher during lactate infusion (P=0.048), along with dP/dt(max) (P=0.041) and LVEF (P=0.009). While arterial elastance (afterload) was not significantly reduced (P=0.121), ventriculo-arterial coupling improved significantly by -0.67 (95% CI -1.13 to -0.21; P=0.012). Cardiac mechano-energetics were significantly improved with increased cardiac efficiency (P=0.003) while pressure-volume area was unchanged. Finally, lactate infusion resulted in increased diuresis (P=0.016) and alkalosis (P<0.001). Conclusions Lactate infusion significantly enhanced CO and peripheral tissue perfusion in a porcine model of AMI-CS. This was driven by increased stroke volume and enhanced LV contractility, without changes in heart rate or systemic blood pressure. Further studies are warranted to explore the long-term effects of lactate infusion and its potential integration with existing therapies for AMI-CS.","author_count":9,"contributors":["Hørsdal, Oskar Kjærgaard","Ellegaard, Mark Stoltenberg","Larsen, Alexander Møller","Guldbrandsen, Halvor Østerby","Moeslund, Niels","Wiggers, Henrik","Nielsen, Bent Roni Ranghøj","Gopalasingam, Nigopan","Berg-Hansen, Kristoffer"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-807342"],"event":{"end":"2025-03-15","place":"Firenze, it","start":"2025-03-14","title":"European Society of Cardiology: Acute CardioVascular Care Congress 2025","year":"2025"},"filters":{"added":"2025-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"pop","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"und","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"cardiovascular effects of lactate infusion in cardiogenic shock complicating acute myocardial infarction","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-807342","ids":["au-b7cd36b0-44aa-440c-8e83-053a411170a3"],"lang":"English","level":"Popular","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-3F175014AE5122BB210BEBE247427977"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - The Department of Cardiological Medicine B"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Oskar Kjærgaard","key":"hørsdalpau","last":"Hørsdal","locid":"7d245c7f-d337-4968-8e56-600f752ff6b5","name":"Hørsdal, Oskar Kjærgaard","orcid":"0000-0003-1009-3768","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Mark Stoltenberg","key":"ellegaardpau","last":"Ellegaard","locid":"45800d23-564a-47e1-9aed-170913fe4a98","name":"Ellegaard, Mark Stoltenberg","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Alexander Møller","key":"larsenpau","last":"Larsen","locid":"c597eab5-5b08-4c91-ae6f-d25e64c92d74","name":"Larsen, Alexander Møller","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Halvor Østerby","key":"guldbrandsenpau","last":"Guldbrandsen","locid":"5943a4f4-a0e1-4337-94a1-15a2b8faaf9f","name":"Guldbrandsen, Halvor Østerby","orcid":"0000-0003-0820-8247","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Niels","key":"moeslundpau","last":"Moeslund","locid":"1ca116ea-4d1c-4846-8f52-757e058b826c","name":"Moeslund, Niels","orcid":"0000-0003-0827-5263","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Henrik","key":"wiggerspau","last":"Wiggers","locid":"aef6a13e-8c07-474c-a4f6-bc35236a6eda","name":"Wiggers, Henrik","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Bent Roni Ranghøj","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"896f4a21-d29c-4ad9-b0ef-4e7f8616c234","name":"Nielsen, Bent Roni Ranghøj","orcid":"0000-0001-9228-3869","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Nigopan","key":"gopalasingampau","last":"Gopalasingam","locid":"eec36c5a-ad89-4943-aea3-e5b049111380","name":"Gopalasingam, Nigopan","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Kristoffer","key":"berghansenpau","last":"Berg-Hansen","locid":"5532f364-aeaa-4065-aa68-179f0bd03637","name":"Berg-Hansen, Kristoffer","orcid":"0000-0002-5110-0656","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1093/ehjacc/zuaf044.160","issn":["20488726","20488734"],"issn_org":["20488726"],"issue":"Supplement 1","journal":"European Heart Journal: Acute Cardiovascular Care","publisher":"Oxford University Press","status":"p","title":"European Heart Journal: Acute Cardiovascular Care","volume":"14"},"review":"Undetermined","search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-b7cd36b0-44aa-440c-8e83-053a411170a3"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Cardiovascular effects of lactate infusion in cardiogenic shock complicating acute myocardial infarction","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-807342","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Metabolic magnetic resonance spectroscopic imaging using hyperpolarized contrast agents offers a non-invasive approach to monitoring real-time in vivo energy metabolism. The technique involves hyperpolarizing a contrast agent in a polarizer, administering it to a living system, and then imaging its distribution and metabolites using a magnetic resonance scanner. Over the past two decades, the method has transitioned from in vitro studies to clinical research, with an increasing focus on clinical applications. Here, we present a hybrid system that adapts a clinical magnetic resonance scanner for pre-clinical rodent experiments. The hybrid system includes (1) a customizable, 3D-printable animal cradle setup and (2) optimized imaging strategies, including coil configurations, metabolic contrast agent administration, and proton imaging acquisition. The system enables 13C dynamic imaging, which we illustrate with detection of hyperpolarized [1–13C]pyruvate and its metabolites in the mouse brain. We detail the experimental procedure, provide practical guidance, and showcase the capabilities of the system with example data from mouse brain imaging. This hybrid setup bridges the gap between clinical and pre-clinical research, enabling iterative testing of equipment, imaging sequences, and hypotheses across phantoms, in vivo rodent models and clinical settings. By facilitating a smoother translation, both forward and reverse, between pre-clinical and clinical applications, this approach enhances the potential for advancing metabolic imaging research.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Christensen, Ditte Bentsen","Skre, Ingeborg S.","Ardenkjær-Larsen, Jan Henrik","Mishkovsky, Mor","Lerche, Mathilde Hauge"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-808359"],"filters":{"added":"2025-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Lerche, Mathilde Hauge"],"country":["Switzerland"],"glorg":["All