,"first":"Anne","key":"blangypau","last":"Blangy","name":"Blangy, Anne","rank":21,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"15 17","first":"Patrick","key":"caswellpau","last":"Caswell","name":"Caswell, Patrick","rank":22,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 9 4 19","first":"Massiullah","key":"shafaqzadahpau","last":"Shafaq-Zadah","name":"Shafaq-Zadah, Massiullah","rank":23,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 28 5","first":"Satyajit","key":"mayorpau","last":"Mayor","name":"Mayor, Satyajit","rank":24,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"14 20","first":"Roberto","key":"weigertpau","last":"Weigert","name":"Weigert, Roberto","rank":25,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 9 4 19","first":"Christian","key":"wunderpau","last":"Wunder","name":"Wunder, Christian","rank":26,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 9 4 19","first":"Ludger","key":"johannespau","last":"Johannes","name":"Johannes, Ludger","rank":27,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["39984654"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1038/s41556-025-01616-x","issn":["14657392","14764679"],"issn_org":["14657392"],"issue":"3","journal":"Nature Cell Biology","pages":"449-463","publisher":"Nature Research","status":"p","title":"Nature Cell Biology","volume":"27"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85218711814"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-0e298d26-928f-4e68-b738-8c659f9f6250","sdu-5eb32fef-7404-4583-95c4-c1f7f0e7c1b5"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Growth factor-triggered de-sialylation controls glycolipid-lectin-driven endocytosis","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-100.00-804420","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"PURPOSE: The aim was to assess the feasibility of a randomized controlled exercise intervention, including physical assessments, in children and adolescents during the first 6 months of cancer treatment. MATERIALS AND METHODS: A sample of children and adolescents (n = 84, 6‒17.9 years) from an ongoing trial (INTERACT: NCT04706676) was randomly assigned to an integrative neuromuscular training (INT) intervention or active control intervention during treatment. The following inter-related feasibility domains were assessed: availability, acceptance, and attrition. Further, we assessed adherence to INT and physical assessments. Adverse events related to exercise and physical assessments were also reported. RESULTS: We found feasible rates within the availability and attrition domains. While the INT group demonstrated feasible group-level adherence rates, individual adherence to prescribed intervention demands was suboptimal. Physical assessments after 6 months of cancer treatment showed feasible rates. CONCLUSION: This study offers insights into the feasibility of an early-initiated INT intervention designed for children and adolescents undergoing cancer treatment. To ensure an optimal frequency of exercise in future studies, a flexible approach to hospital-based INT and a structured strategy for home-based exercise should be considered. Future trials should prioritize outcomes to minimize the length and timing of assessment.","author_count":11,"contributors":["Schmidt-Andersen, Peter","Pouplier, Anna","Faigenbaum, Avery D","Beth, Christina Kirkeby","Olsen, Clara Cæcilie","Lykkedegn, Sine","Hasle, Henrik","Müller, Klaus","Larsen, Hanne Baekgaard","Fridh, Martin","Christensen, Jan"],"cuid":["S03-093.12-793233"],"filters":{"added":"2025-02","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":3,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Fridh, Martin"],"country":["United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","College of New Jersey","The College of New Jersey","The College of New Jersey, Department of Biology, Ewing, USA"],"keyword":["Adolescent","Child","Exercise","Exercise Therapy methods","Exercise intervention","Feasibility Studies","Female","Follow Up Studies","Humans","Male","Neoplasms therapy","Patient Compliance","Pediatric exercise oncology","Physical activity","Prognosis","physical assessment"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-SDR"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-SDR","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"evaluating feasibility of an exercise intervention including physical assessment during the first 000000000006 months of cancer treatment in children and adolescents in a randomized controlled trial","src":["au","ku","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85213972197","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S03-093.12-793233","identifier":[{"id":"85213972197","type":"scopus"},{"id":"39745099","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["au-4b417a2a-edfb-4737-bfd5-0f3cd910dcf6","ku-8e56970f-db4e-420d-a668-98a26a952e69","sdu-eb2290b9-ee0c-4a9a-8b27-2333de90311e"],"keyword":["Adolescent","Child","Exercise","Exercise Therapy/methods","Feasibility Studies","Female","Follow-Up Studies","Humans","Male","Neoplasms/therapy","Patient Compliance","Prognosis","exercise intervention","pediatric exercise oncology","physical activity","physical assessment"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-6-4D80FBAF62420140D4F9A20905EE7883"],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"capital region of denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CAP","l3nl":"Capital Region o