{"abstract":"Hyperpolarized carbon-13 labeled compounds are increasingly being used in medical MR imaging (MRI) and MR imaging (MRI) and spectroscopy (MRS) research, due to its ability to monitor tissue and cell metabolism in real-time. Although radiological biomarkers are increasingly being considered as clinical indicators, biopsies are still considered the gold standard for a large variety of indications. Bioreactor systems can play an important role in biopsy examinations because of their ability to provide a physiochemical environment that is conducive for therapeutic response monitoring ex vivo. We demonstrate here a proof-of-concept bioreactor and microcoil receive array setup that allows for ex vivo preservation and metabolic NMR spectroscopy on up to three biopsy samples simultaneously, creating an easy-to-use and robust way to simultaneously run multisample carbon-13 hyperpolarization experiments. Experiments using hyperpolarized [1-13C]pyruvate on ML-1 leukemic cells in the bioreactor setup were performed and the kinetic pyruvate-to-lactate rate constants ((Formula presented.)) extracted. The coefficient of variation of the experimentally found (Formula presented.) s for five repeated experiments was (Formula presented.). With this statistical power, treatment effects of 30%–40% change in lactate production could be easily differentiable with only a few hyperpolarization dissolutions on this setup. Furthermore, longitudinal experiments showed preservation of ML-1 cells in the bioreactor setup for at least 6 h. Rat brain tissue slices were also seen to be preserved within the bioreactor for at least 1 h. This validation serves as the basis for further optimization and upscaling of the setup, which undoubtedly has huge potential in high-throughput studies with various biomarkers and tissue types.","author_count":8,"contributors":["Christensen, Nichlas Vous","Holm, Rikke","Sanchez, Juan D","Søvsø Szocska Hansen, Esben","Lerche, Mathilde H","Ardenkjaer-Larsen, Jan Henrik","Laustsen, Christoffer","Bertelsen, Lotte Bonde"],"cuid":["S02-097.07-724439"],"filters":{"added":"2024-05","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Bertelsen, Lotte Bonde"],"country":["Germany"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","JD Coils"],"keyword":["Animals","Biomarkers","Bioreactor","Bioreactors","Carbon 13","Carbon isotopes","Ex vivo metabolism","Hyperpolarization","Lactic Acid metabolism","Leukemia","Metabolic Flux Analysis","Microcoil","Pyruvic Acid metabolism","Rats"],"level":"sci","mra":["med","sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-DTU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-DTU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"a continuous flow bioreactor system for high throughput hyperpolarized metabolic flux analysis","src":["au","dtu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-DTU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85183198633","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S02-097.07-724439","identifier":[{"id":"85183198633","type":"scopus"},{"id":"38279190","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["au-02f7101e-57de-4e07-b8d4-e6973123b5f7","dtu-858afde7-3ff0-4fb1-b1c7-0efea928b6d2"],"keyword":["Animals","Biomarkers","Bioreactors","Carbon Isotopes","Lactic Acid/metabolism","Metabolic Flux Analysis","Pyruvic Acid/metabolism","Rats","bioreactor","carbon-13","ex vivo metabolism","hyperpolarization","leukemia","microcoil"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science","Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-73C219D63A3BC348D3393CDCFCBD8B15"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - The MR Research Centre"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"de","key":"jd coils","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"JD Coils","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, Department of Health Technology","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Health Technology, Magnetic Resonance","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Health Technology, Magnetic Resonance, Hyperpolarization & Metabolism","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Health Technology, Magnetic Resonance, Metabolic Brain Magnetic Resonance"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Nichlas Vous","key":"christensenpau","last":"Christensen","locid":"58401e4e-edd2-47f5-904c-bed76b6b18af","name":"Christensen, Nichlas Vous","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Rikke","key":"holmpau","last":"Holm","locid":"66d6492e-d6ff-4018-b732-5d88d85d91cd","name":"Holm, Rikke","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Juan D.","key":"sanchezpau","last":"Sanchez","name":"Sanchez, Juan D.","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Esben S S","key":"hansenpau","last":"Hansen","locid":"2ea5821e-bbda-4f7e-a18a-0b9eb3b993f0","name":"Søvsø Szocska Hansen, Esben","name_org":"Hansen, Esben S S","orcid":"0000-0001-5512-9870","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Mathilde H.","key":"lerchepau","last":"Lerche","locid":"bf55c4cb-8ebc-4c00-a107-f90c312e6e76","name":"Lerche, Mathilde Hauge","name_org":"Lerche, Mathilde H.","orcid":"0000-0002-5899-2781","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jan Henrik","key":"ardenkjærlarsenpau","last":"Ardenkjær-Larsen","locid":"ae9519c9-1b64-4426-b89f-4c36d08c1836","name":"Ardenkjær-Larsen, Jan Henrik","orcid":"0000-0001-6167-6926","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Christoffer","key":"laustsenpau","last":"Laustsen","locid":"ba5f7084-cbbb-4c1a-9fb6-85cce410d52a","name":"Laustsen, Christoffer","orcid":"0000-0002-0317-2911","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Lotte Bonde","key":"bertelsenpau","last":"Bertelsen","locid":"17c3813d-f5fd-4a91-9ca6-79a0a1de421d","name":"Bertelsen, Lotte Bonde","orcid":"0000-0002-2491-3184","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["38279190"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1002/nbm.5107","issn":["09523480","10991492"],"issn_org":["10991492"],"issue":"5","journal":"NMR in Biomedicine","paperid":"e5107","publisher":"John Wiley and Sons Ltd","status":"p","title":"NMR in Biomedicine","volume":"37"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85183198633"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Technical University of Denmark","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-02f7101e-57de-4e07-b8d4-e6973123b5f7","dtu-858afde7-3ff0-4fb1-b1c7-0efea928b6d2"],"src":["AU Aarhus University","DTU Technical University of Denmark"],"title":"A continuous flow bioreactor system for high-throughput hyperpolarized metabolic flux analysis","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-097.07-724439","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"The accuracy of multi-frequency (MF) bioelectrical impedance analysis (BIA) to estimate low muscle mass in older hospitalized patients remains unclear. This study aimed to describe the ability of MF-BIA to identify low muscle mass as proposed by The Global Leadership Initiative on Malnutrition (GLIM) and The European Working Group on Sarcopenia in Older People (EWGSOP-2) and examine the association between muscle mass, dehydration, malnutrition, and poor appetite in older hospitalized patients. In this prospective exploratory cohort study, low muscle mass was estimated with MF-BIA against dual-energy X-ray absorptiometry (DXA) in 42 older hospitalized adults (≥65 years). The primary variable for muscle mass was appendicular skeletal muscle mass (ASM), and secondary variables were appendicular skeletal muscle mass index (ASMI) and fat-free mass index (FFMI). Cut-off values for low muscle mass were based on recommendations by GLIM and EWGSOP-2. MF-BIA was evaluated against DXA on the ability to estimate absolute values of muscle mass by mean bias, limits of agreement (LOA), and accuracy (5% and 10% levels). Agreement between MF-BIA and DXA to identify low muscle mass was evaluated with sensitivity, specificity, negative predictive value (NPV), and positive predictive value (PPV). The association between muscle mass, dehydration, malnutrition, and poor appetite was visually examined with boxplots. MF-BIA overestimated absolute values of ASM with a mean bias of 0.63 kg (CI: -0.20:1.46, LOA: -4.61:5.87). Agreement between MF-BIA and DXA measures of ASM showed a sensitivity of 86%, specificity of 94%, PPV of 75% and NPV of 97%. Boxplots indicate that ASM is lower in patients with malnutrition. This was not observed in patients with poor appetite. We observed a tendency toward higher ASM in patients with dehydration. Estimation of absolute ASM values with MF-BIA should be interpreted with caution, but MF-BIA might identify low muscle mass in older hospitalized patients.","author_count":13,"contributors":["Nielsen, Rikke Lundsgaard","Andersen, Aino Leegaard","Kallemose, Thomas","Damgaard, Morten","Bornæs, Olivia","Juul-Larsen, Helle Gybel","Strejby Christensen, Louise Westberg","Jawad, Baker Nawfal","Andersen, Ove","Rasmussen, Henrik Højgaard","Munk, Tina","Lund, Trine Meldgaard","Houlind, Morten Baltzer"],"cuid":["S02-100.00-715825"],"filters":{"added":"2024-02","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"corresponding_author":["Nielsen, Rikke Lundsgaard"],"keyword":["BIA","DXA","Malnutrition","Muscle mass","Older patients","Sarcopenia"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-NDR"],"OT":["Copenhagen University Hospital – The Hospital Pharmacy"],"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-NDR","DI-U-AAU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"evaluation of multi frequency bioelectrical impedance analysis against dual energy x ray absorptiometry for estimation of low muscle mass in older hospitalized patients","src":["aau","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY 4.0","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/jcm13010196","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["Funding for our study was received from the Capital Region’s strategic funds, the Capital Region’s fund for transitional research, and the Danish Regions. Morten Baltzer Houlind is supported by the BRIDGE—Translational Excellence Program (bridge.ku.dk) at the Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, University of Copenhagen, funded by the Novo Nordisk Foundation (grant No. NNF20SA0064340). These funders had no role in the design of this study and will not have any role during its execution, analyses, interpretation of the data, or decision to submit