Gromov, Kirill","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 3","corresponding":null,"first":"Mikkel R.","key":"andersenpau","last":"Andersen","name":"Andersen, Mikkel R.","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 5","corresponding":null,"first":"Manuel","key":"biederpau","last":"Bieder","name":"Bieder, Manuel","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 3","corresponding":null,"first":"Søren","key":"overgaardpau","last":"Overgaard","name":"Overgaard, Søren","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Claus","key":"varnumpau","last":"Varnum","name":"Varnum, Claus","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["39653131"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.arth.2024.12.005","issn":["08835403","15328406"],"issn_org":["08835403"],"journal":"Journal of Arthroplasty","publisher":"Elsevier","status":"e","title":"Journal of Arthroplasty"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85213940450"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Miscellaneous","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Miscellaneous","Other Research","Region Zealand","Region of Southern Denmark","Universities"],"PST":["E-Published (ahead of print)"]},"sid":["aau-559df6ca-a277-473e-bce0-1d5fb902706a"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University"],"title":"Discharge Destination of Patients in Fast-Track Primary Hip and Knee Arthroplasty: Results From a Prospective Danish Cohort","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-785864","year":"2024","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"BACKGROUND & AIMS: The inflammatory bowel diseases (IBDs), ulcerative colitis (UC) and Crohn's disease (CD), are heterogenous diseases ranging from mild to severe. We aimed to describe the prevalence and prognosis of mild IBD in an unselected population-based patient cohort. METHODS: We identified all individuals diagnosed with IBD during 1997 to 2020 in North Denmark (n = 4607). Patients with mild disease, based on treatment history within the first year, were followed for progression to moderate-severe disease, based on a composite outcome of immunomodulators, biologic therapies, IBD-related hospitalization, and/or surgery. We used time-to-event analysis to calculate probabilities of progression based on IBD subtype, age, sex, and duration of mild IBD. RESULTS: Among 2315 individuals with initial mild IBD, 24.5% with UC (n = 474) and 46% with CD (n = 174) progressed to moderate-severe disease during follow-up. Of the total IBD population, 52.3% of patients with UC and 86.8% with CD progressed during follow-up. Individuals <18 years at diagnosis were the most likely to progress. The 10-year probability of progressing to moderate-severe UC was 26% after 1 year with mild disease, 19% after 5 years, and 12% after 10 years. The probability of progressing to moderate-severe CD was 53% after 1 year with mild disease, 34% after 5 years, and 33% after 10 years. CONCLUSIONS: Approximately one-fourth of individuals with mild UC within 1 year after diagnosis and one-half of those with mild CD progressed to moderate-severe disease over time. Young age at diagnosis increased the probability of progression, whereas increasing duration of mild disease decreased the probability.","author_count":10,"contributors":["Jacobsen, Henrik Albæk","Karachalia Sandri, Anastasia","Ebert, Anthony C","Allin, Kristine H.","Ananthakrishnan, Ashwin N","Agrawal, Manasi","Ungaro, Ryan C","Colombel, Jean-Frederic","Larsen, Lone","Jess, Tine"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-786343"],"filters":{"added":"2025-02","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Jacobsen, Henrik Albæk"],"country":["United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Icahn School of Medicine at Mount Sinai","Massachusetts General Hospital"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"prevalence and prognosis of mild inflammatory bowel disease a population based cohort study 000000001997 000000002020","src":["aau"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY 4.0","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cgh.2024.10.021","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-786343","identifier":[{"id":"105004441441","type":"scopus"},{"id":"39675406","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["aau-cf30dbdc-9de0-4366-8d7c-060cb08e6625"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-B46B9BF3C32342C3DEF0998A1FE464FC"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY 4.0","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cgh.2024.10.021","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Faculty of Medicine","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Medicine, Aalborg University Hospital","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Medicine, Aalborg University Hospital, Department of Gastroenterology and Hepatology","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine, Center for Molecular Prediction of Inflammatory Bowel D