:"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Rene Sjogren","key":"hendriksenpau","last":"Hendriksen","locid":"d0695f89-50d1-4626-b4b2-0ea8773ccb3c","name":"Hendriksen, Rene S.","name_org":"Hendriksen, Rene Sjogren","orcid":"0000-0003-2934-8214","rank":77,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Frank Moller","key":"aarestruppau","last":"Aarestrup","locid":"8a1eb030-2c37-4557-bce1-7bc6d5c75aaa","name":"Aarestrup, Frank Møller","name_org":"Aarestrup, Frank Moller","orcid":"0000-0002-7116-2723","rank":78,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["37717051"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1038/s41597-023-02502-7","issn":["20524463"],"issue":"1","journal":"Scientific Data","paperid":"628","publisher":"Nature Publishing Group","status":"p","title":"Scientific Data","volume":"10"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85171410753"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Technical University of Denmark","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-c94b6525-c42e-4cdb-b3f7-61b625759408","ku-07f297a4-4feb-4c2f-9983-d7a1868534c7"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Whole genomes from bacteria collected at diagnostic units around the world 2020","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-100.00-742212","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"BACKGROUND: Night work has been associated with coronary heart disease. The present study examined exposure-response relations between quantitative night work characteristics and coronary heart disease (angina pectoris or myocardial infarction) with the aim to contribute to evidence-based recommendations for low-risk night work schedules. METHODS: We followed 100 149 night workers (80% women) and 153 882 day workers (78% women), all health care workers in Denmark with day by day payroll information on night shifts from 2007 to 2015. We analysed data with Poisson regression stratified by sex and adjusted for age, calendar year, diabetes, family history of cardiovascular disease, educational level, occupation, indicators for obesity, hypercholesterolaemia, and hypertension. RESULTS: Female and male night workers worked on average 1.7 and 1.8 night shifts per month for an average duration of less than 4 years. During follow-up, 1198 night and 2128 day workers were hospitalized with first-time coronary heart disease. When compared with day workers, the overall incidence rate ratios for female and male night workers were 1.06 [95% confidence interval (CI): 0.97, 1.17] and 1.22 (95% CI 1.07, 1.39). Highest risks were observed in top exposure categories for several night work characteristics. However, no consistent exposure-response relations by number of monthly night shifts, cumulative night shifts, years with rotating night shifts, years with any night shift and consecutive night shifts were observed among the night workers of either sex. CONCLUSIONS: This study of a population with low exposure to night work does not indicate that reducing extent of monthly night shifts, cumulative night shifts, years with rotating night shifts, years with any night shift and consecutive night shifts would reduce the risk of coronary heart disease.","author_count":12,"contributors":["Vestergaard, Jesper Medom","Dalbøge, Annett","Bonde, Jens Peter Ellekilde","Garde, Anne He