sponding":null,"first":"Thomas","key":"wittersehpau","last":"Witterseh","name":"Witterseh, Thomas","rank":11,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"issue":"2021:04","pages":"95","place":"Kbh.","publisher":"Institut for Byggeri, By og Miljø (BUILD), Aalborg Universitet","repno":"BUILD Rapport, 2021:04","series":"BUILD Rapport","status":"p"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All GTS Institutes","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-GTS","DI-GTS-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","GTS Institutes","Technical University of Denmark","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-82a96324-7178-452a-8299-b89106494f9b"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University"],"title":"IK-kompas Etageboliger – værktøj til holistisk vurdering af indeklima : Dokumentationsrapport","type":"Report","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-088078","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Introduction: No reliable predictive blood-based biomarkers are available for determining survival from pancreatic cancer. This combined explorative and validation study examines promoter hypermethylation of Secreted frizzled-related protein 1 (phSFRP1) in plasma-derived cell-free DNA as a predictive marker for survival and gemcitabine effectiveness in patients with stage IV pancreatic adenocarcinoma. Materials and Methods: This work consists of an explorative study and a validation study. Patients were included prospectively. Blood samples were drawn before diagnostic workup and treatment. We conducted methylation-specific polymerase chain reaction analysis of the promoter region of the SFRP1 gene, based on bisulfite treatment. Survival was analyzed with log-rank test and Cox proportional hazard regression. The adjusted model included the variables age>65, WHO Performance Status, and gender. Results: The explorative study included 40 patients. Patients not receiving chemotherapy (n=15) had a mOS of 2.0 months. In gemcitabine treated patients (n=25), phSFRP1 patients had a significantly shorter median overall survival of 4.4 months compared to 11.3 months in unmethylated patients. This difference was significant in adjusted cox-regression with a HR of 4.8 (95% CI; 1.5-15.3). The validation study included 58 patients who received gemcitabine. Patients with phSFRP1 had a shorter median overall survival (3·2 months) than the unmethylated group (6.3 months). This difference was significant in adjusted cox-regression with a HR of 3.5 (95% CI; 1.8-6.7). Conlusions: In both the explorative and the validation study, phSFRP1 was associated with poorer survival in stage IV pancreatic cancer patients receiving gemcitabine treatment. This may indicate that SFRP1-positive tumors are more aggressive and less sensitive to gemcitabine treatment than tumors without SFRP1 hypermethylation. This knowledge may facilitate tailored treatment of patients with stage IV pancreatic adenocarcinoma.","author_count":8,"contributors":["Stubbe, Benjamin","Henriksen, Stine Dam","Madsen, Poul Henning","Larsen, Anders Christian","Krarup, Henrik","Pedersen, Inge Søkilde","Johansen, Martin Berg","Thorlacius-Ussing, Ole"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-117701"],"event":{"end":"2021-08-27","place":"Odense, dk","start":"2021-08-26","title":"Danske Kræftforskningsdage 2021","year":"2021"},"filters":{"added":"2022-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Biomarker","Blood based","Cancer","DNA methylation","Epigenetics","Pancreatic cancer","Personalized therapy","Prognostic","Survival"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"sfrp000000000001 promoter hypermethylation as a predictor of survival and gemcitabine efficiency in patients with stage iv pancreatic adenocarcinoma","src":["aau"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU"],"type":"dco","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY 4.0","type":"loc","url":"https://vbn.aau.dk/ws/files/468225790/SFRP1_promoter_hypermethylation_as_a_prognostic_marker.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-117701","ids":["aau-e8acd2a6-30e7-4091-9c06-a308c5f24bda"],"keyword":["DNA Methylation","biomarker","blood-based","cancer","epigenetics","pancreatic cancer","personalized therapy","prognostic","survival"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Local-1-1-A2BFC00ADEC87549BDEC020513092F6B"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY 4.0","type":"loc","url":"https://vbn.aau.dk/ws/files/468225790/SFRP1_promoter_hypermethylation_as_a_prognostic_marker.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Faculty of Medicine","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Medicine, Aalborg University Hospital","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Medicine, Aalborg University Hospital, Clinical Cancer Research Center","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Medicine, Aalborg University Hospital, Department of Gastrointestinal Surgery","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Medicine, Aalborg University Hospital, Klinik Kirurgi og Kræftbehandling"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Benjamin","key":"stubbepau","last":"Stubbe","locid":"2cc4af0d-5f4a-4bbf-8465-ee12c2c896cb","name":"Stubbe, Benjamin","orcid":"0000-0002-2727-5915","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Stine Dam","key":"henriksenpau","last":"Henriksen","locid":"59571ea6-b5de-44f6-ab36-c829af2fdba3","name":"Henriksen, Stine Dam","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Poul Henning","key":"madsenpau","last":"Madsen","locid":"867371b3-f1df-40b0-95d1-412a29ad2287","name":"Madsen, Poul Henning","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Anders Christian","key":"larsenpau","last":"Larsen","locid":"a1634007-e037-478b-aee3-b9915c8cafa5","name":"Larsen, Anders Christian","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Henrik Bygum","key":"kraruppau","last":"Krarup","locid":"72b4ebce-12cf-4f92-bb97-c9a588b06d57","name":"Krarup, Henrik","name_org":"Krarup, Henrik Bygum","orcid":"0000-0001-5294-2428","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Inge Søkilde","key":"pedersenpau","last":"Pedersen","locid":"a3b6feb6-0d43-4dd7-870c-e6e493108acd","name":"Pedersen, Inge Søkilde","orcid":"0000-0002-9902-8040","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Martin Nygård","key":"johansenpau","last":"Johansen","locid":"bbd320a7-9c17-4062-a6d9-76a1bbed24bf","name":"Johansen, Martin Berg","name_org":"Johansen, Martin Nygård","orcid":"0000-0001-9790-0985","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Ole","key":"thorlaciusussingpau","last":"Thorlacius-Ussing","locid":"00effb83-33c5-4f0d-97af-5107bd146c4b","name":"Thorlacius-Ussing, Ole","orcid":"0000-0003-4963-1551","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-e8acd2a6-30e7-4091-9c06-a308c5f24bda"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University"],"title":"SFRP1 Promoter Hypermethylation as a Predictor of Survival and Gemcitabine Efficiency in Patients with Stage IV Pancreatic Adenocarcinoma","type":"Conference Poster","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-117701","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Eating disorders and schizophrenia are both moderately to highly heritable and share significant genetic risk despite distinct diagnostic criteria. Large-scale family studies on the co-aggregation of these disorders are lacking. Thus, we aimed to estimate the co-occurrence and familial co-aggregation of these disorders within the entire Swedish and Danish population. The proband cohort consisted of individuals born in Sweden (1977-2003) and Denmark (1984-2006) and still residing in their respective country at age six (NSweden = 2,535,191, NDenmark = 1,382,367). Probands were linked to their biological parents, siblings, grandparents, uncles/aunts, and cousins. Diagnoses for anorexia nervosa (AN) and other eating disorders (OED: bulimia nervosa, binge-eating disorder, and eating disorder not otherwise specified) for probands and schizophrenia diagnoses for both probands and relatives were obtained. The likelihood of having schizophrenia in those with AN or OED and their relatives was compared with individuals without eating disorder diagnoses and their relatives. Probands with AN or OED were more likely to have schizophrenia than probands without these disorders. All relatives of probands with AN or OED (except parents and uncles/aunts of probands with AN) were at increased risk of schizophrenia. In general, the magnitude of odds ratios attenuated with decreasing genetic relatedness. These results suggest familial liability contributes to the association between eating disorders and schizophrenia. Clinicians should be mindful of this comorbid and co-aggregation pattern as it may influence case conceptualization and treatment decisions.","author_count":10,"contributors":["Zhang, Ruyue","Larsen, Janne Tidselbak","Kuja-Halkola, Ralf","Thornton, Laura","Yao, Shuyang","Larsson, Henrik","Lichtenstein, Paul","Petersen, Liselotte","Bulik, Cynthia M","Bergen, Sarah E"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-167594"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Bergen, Sarah E"],"country":["Sweden","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Karolinska Institutet","University of North Carolina","Örebro University"],"keyword":["Anorexia nervosa","Bipolar disorder","Cohort","Genetic correlations","Linkage","Morbidity","Paternal Age","Population"],"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-NP","DI-U"],"level3":{"NP":["DI-NP-ALL","DI-NP-LUND"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-NP-LUND","DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"familial co aggregation of schizophrenia and eating disorders in sweden and denmark","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85085096104","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-167594","identifier":[{"id":"000530988300001","type":"wos"},{"id":"85085096104","type":"scopus"},{"id":"32382133","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["au-43270dac-c48b-440f-acaa-206bb5fbfe2a"],"keyword":["ANOREXIA-NERVOSA","BIPOLAR DISORDER","CHROMOSOME 10P","COHORT","GENETIC CORRELATIONS","LINKAGE","MORBIDITY","PATERNAL AGE","POPULATION"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-694368C24C185EB05A4D26C4F912A0FD"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Social Science (SOC)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"se","key":"karolinska institutet","loc":["Sweden","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Karolinska Institutet","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Public Health, Department of Public H