Biophysics Letters","volume":"50"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Private Research","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-PR","DI-PR-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Other Companies","Private Research","Technical University of Denmark","Universities","fida biosystems aps"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-44fe35aa-e844-4be4-b00c-f8827535efad"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark"],"title":"High throughput determination of kinetic and thermodynamic parameters of biomolecular liquid-liquid phase separation [Capflex]","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-325099","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"While the assessment of hearing aid use has traditionally relied on subjective self-reported measures, smartphone-connected hearing aids enable objective data logging from a large number of users. Objective data logging allows to overcome the inaccuracy of self-reported measures. Moreover, data logging enables assessing hearing aid use with a greater temporal resolution and longitudinally, making it possible to investigate hourly patterns of use and to account for the day-to-day variability. This study aims to explore patterns of hearing aid use throughout the day and assess whether clusters of users with similar use patterns can be identified. We did so by analyzing objective hearing aid use data logged from 15,905 real-world users over a 4-month period. Firstly, we investigated the daily amount of hearing aid use and its within-user and between-user variability. We found that users, on average, used the hearing aids for 10.01 h/day, exhibiting a substantial between-user (SD = 2.76 h) and within-user (SD = 3.88 h) variability. Secondly, we examined hearing aid use hourly patterns by clustering 453,612 logged days into typical days of hearing aid use. We identified three typical days of hearing aid use: full day (44% of days), afternoon (27%), and sporadic evening (26%) day of hearing aid use. Thirdly, we explored the usage patterns of the hearing aid users by clustering the users based on the proportion of time spent in each of the typical days of hearing aid use. We found three distinct user groups, each characterized by a predominant (i.e., experienced ~60% of the time) typical day of hearing aid use. Notably, the largest user group (49%) of users predominantly had full days of hearing aid use. Finally, we validated the user clustering by training a supervised classification ensemble to predict the cluster to which each user belonged. The high accuracy achieved by the supervised classifier ensemble (~86%) indicated valid user clustering and showed that such a classifier can be successfully used to group new hearing aid users in the future. This study provides a deeper insight into the adoption of hearing care treatments and paves the way for more personalized solutions.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Pasta, Alessandro","Szatmari, Tiberiu-Ioan","Christensen, Jeppe Høy","Jensen, Kasper Juul","Pontoppidan, Niels Henrik","Sun, Kang","Larsen, Jakob Eg"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-335196"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Pasta, Alessandro"],"keyword":["Data logging","Ensemble classification","Hearing aids","Personalization","User clustering"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-PR","DI-U"],"level3":{"PR":["DI-PR-ALL","DI-PR-OTH","DI-PR-OTI"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU"]},"level3all":["DI-PR-OTH","DI-PR-OTI","DI-U-DTU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"clustering users based on hearing aid use an exploratory analysis of real world data","src":["dtu"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fdgth.2021.725130","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-335196","identifier":[{"id":"85129783694","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC8521852","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"34713197","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["dtu-669b39ec-241b-4ed8-b1fc-5ae20f0fd9f9"],"keyword":["Data logging","Ensemble classification","Hearing aid use amount,","Hearing aid use patterns","Hearing aids","Personalization","User clustering"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-F8B665360A9B4B54CBD275603489A275"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3389/fdgth.2021.725130","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/256795726/fdgth_03_725130.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, Department of Applied Mathematics and Computer Science","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Applied Mathematics and Computer Science, Cognitive Systems"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"oticon","l2":"DI-PR","l2n":"Private Research","l3":"DI-PR-OTI","l3nl":"Oticon A/S","l3nn":"Oticon","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Oticon","namevar":["Oticon Danmark AS"],"nora_code":"DI-PR-OTI","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"dk","key":"other companies","l2":"DI-PR","l2n":"Private Research","l3":"DI-PR-OTH","l3nl":"Other Companies","l3nn":"Other Companies","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Other Companies","namevar":["Demant A/S"],"nora_code":"DI-PR-OTH","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Alessandro","key":"pastapau","last":"Pasta","locid":"f53ef5d5-4a76-451c-8109-212c835a8f52","name":"Pasta, Alessandro","orcid":"0000-0002-9920-6695","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Tiberiu-Ioan","key":"szatmaripau","last":"Szatmari","locid":"18a19e5f-a6a9-4ea3-b1a6-eecccdd48352","name":"Szatmari, Tiberiu-Ioan","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Jeppe Høy","key":"christensenpau","last":"Christensen","name":"Christensen, Jeppe Høy","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Kasper Juul","key":"jensenpau","last":"Jensen","name":"Jensen, Kasper Juul","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Niels Henrik","key":"pontoppidanpau","last":"Pontoppidan","name":"Pontoppidan, Niels Henrik","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Kang","key":"sunpau","last":"Sun","name":"Sun, Kang","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jakob Eg","key":"larsenpau","last":"Larsen","locid":"691c8ae7-9a21-4b98-9b91-5e2d9806a4b9","name":"Larsen, Jakob Eg","orcid":"0000-0002-7461-9901","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["34713197"],"pub":{"doi":"10.3389/fdgth.2021.725130","issn":["2673253X"],"issn_org":["2673253x"],"journal":"Frontiers in Digital Health","paperid":"725130","publisher":"Frontiers Media SA","publisher_org":"Frontiers Media S.A.","status":"p","title":"Frontiers in Digital Health","volume":"3"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85129783694"],"sdg":["3. Good Health and Well-being","All UN SDG Classification"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Private Research","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-PR","DI-PR-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Other Companies","Oticon A/S","Private Research","Technical University of Denmark","Universities","demant a/s"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-669b39ec-241b-4ed8-b1fc-5ae20f0fd9f9"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark"],"title":"Clustering Users Based on Hearing Aid Use: An Exploratory Analysis of Real-World Data","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-335196","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Drastic sensitivity enhancement of dynamic nuclear polarization is becoming an increasingly critical methodology to monitor real-time metabolic and physiological information in chemistry, biochemistry, and biomedicine. However, the limited number of available hyperpolarized 13C probes, which can effectively interrogate crucial metabolic activities, remains one of the major bottlenecks in this growing field. Here, we demonstrate [1-13C] N-acetyl cysteine (NAC) as a novel probe for hyperpolarized 13C MRI to monitor glutathione redox chemistry, which plays a central part of metabolic chemistry and strongly influences various therapies. NAC forms a disulfide bond in the presence of reduced glutathione, which generates a spectroscopically detectable product that is separated from the main peak by a 1.5 ppm shift. In vivo hyperpolarized MRI in mice revealed that NAC was broadly distributed throughout the body including the brain. Its biochemical transformation in two human pancreatic tumor cells in vitro and as xenografts differed depending on the individual cellular biochemical profile and microenvironment in vivo. Hyperpolarized NAC can be a promising non-invasive biomarker to monitor in vivo redox status and can be potentially translatable to clinical diagnosis.","author_count":19,"contributors":["Yamamoto, Kazutoshi","Opina, Ana","Sail, Deepak","Blackman, Burchelle","Saito, Keita","Brender, Jeffrey R.","Malinowski, Ronja Maja","Seki, Tomohiro","Oshima, Nobu","Crooks, Daniel R.","Kishimoto, Shun","Saida, Yu","Otowa, Yasunori","Choyke, Peter L.","Ardenkjær-Larsen, Jan Henrik","Mitchell, James B.","Linehan, W. Marston","Swenson, Rolf E.","Krishna, Murali C."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-357093"],"filters":{"added":"2022-03","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"country":["United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","National Institutes of Health"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"real time insight into in vivo redox status utilizing hyperpolarized 000000000001 000000000013c n acetyl cysteine","src":["dtu"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-021-90921-0","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-357093","identifier":[{"id":"000663770500001","type":"wos"},{"id":"85107516081","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC8190077","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"34108512","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["dtu-afd7542e-f5df-442f-8dee-624cfcda3060"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-507642DE89F1BE727BE2BADCD50EDBD0"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-021-90921-0","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/265190748/616ba26baeeadf1763bef860.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"us","key":"national institutes of health","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"National Institutes of Health","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, Centers, Center for Hyperpolarization in Magnetic Resonance","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Health Technology","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Health Technology, Magnetic Resonance","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Health Technology, Magn