st":"Alexander K.","key":"buellpau","last":"Buell","locid":"72009c5b-9a1f-40ed-a109-1ef9270c2d7c","name":"Buell, Alexander K.","orcid":"0000-0003-1161-3622","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["34911929"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1038/s41467-021-27433-y","issn":["20411723"],"journal":"Nature Communications","paperid":"7289","publisher":"Nature Research","status":"p","title":"Nature Communications","volume":"12"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85121391953"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Private Research","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-PR","DI-PR-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Other Companies","Private Research","Technical University of Denmark","Universities","University of Copenhagen","fida biosystems aps","fida biosystems aps (biz)"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-67fd420f-60a0-4070-aa4f-9f7c68b8e918","ku-049deea5-403e-48ea-a206-ef73fcc4f227"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Capillary flow experiments for thermodynamic and kinetic characterization of protein liquid-liquid phase separation","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-100.00-034976","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Growing evidence supports that pharmacological application of growth differentiation factor 15 (GDF15) suppresses appetite but also promotes sickness-like behaviors in rodents via GDNF family receptor α-like (GFRAL)-dependent mechanisms. Conversely, the endogenous regulation of GDF15 and its physiological effects on energy homeostasis and behavior remain elusive. Here we show, in four independent human studies that prolonged endurance exercise increases circulating GDF15 to levels otherwise only observed in pathophysiological conditions. This exercise-induced increase can be recapitulated in mice and is accompanied by increased Gdf15 expression in the liver, skeletal muscle, and heart muscle. However, whereas pharmacological GDF15 inhibits appetite and suppresses voluntary running activity via GFRAL, the physiological induction of GDF15 by exercise does not. In summary, exercise-induced circulating GDF15 correlates with the duration of endurance exercise. Yet, higher GDF15 levels after exercise are not sufficient to evoke canonical pharmacological GDF15 effects on appetite or responsible for diminishing exercise motivation.","author_count":25,"contributors":["Klein, Anders Bue","Nicolaisen, Trine Sand","Ørtenblad, Niels","Gejl, Kasper Degn","Jensen, Rasmus","Fritzen, Andreas Mæchel","Larsen, Emil L","Karstoft, Kristian","Poulsen, Henrik Enghusen","Morville, Thomas","Sahl, Ronni Eg","Helge, Jørn Wulff","Lund, Jens","Falk, Sarah","Lyngbæk, Mark","Ellingsgaard, Helga","Pedersen, Bente Klarlund","Lu, Wei","Finan, Brian","Jørgensen, Sebastian B","Seeley, Randy J","Kleinert, Maximilian","Kiens, Bente","Richter, Erik A.","Clemmensen, Christoffer"],"cuid":["S03-100.00-698285"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":3,"continent":["America, North","Europe, EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Clemmensen, Christoffer"],"country":["Germany","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Helmholtz Zentrum München - German Research Center for Environmental Health","Novo Nordisk Research Center Indianapolis","Technical University of Munich","University of Michigan","University of Michigan, Ann Arbor"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-PR","DI-U","DI-UC"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"PR":["DI-PR-ALL","DI-PR-NOVO"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"UC":["DI-UC-ALL","DI-UC-UCS"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-PR-NOVO","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU","DI-UC-UCS"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"pharmacological but not physiological gdf000000000015 suppresses feeding and the motivation to exercise","src":["ku","sdu","ucsyd"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU","DI-UC-UCS"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-021-21309-x","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["We thank Charlotte Svendsen, Anne Lund and Camilla Lund for experimental assistance. The study was supported by Independent Research Fund Denmark (0134-00254B), Lundbeckfonden (R164-2013-16132), the Ministry of Culture Denmark (FPK.2017-0013, TKIF2009-052), the Augustinus Foundation (17-4211), the Danish Diabetes Academy, Funded by the Novo Nordisk Foundation, Independent Research Fund Denmark, TrygFonden and Fonden til Lægevidenskabens Fremme—AP Møller Fonden. R.J.S. received funding from the NIH (P0I DKK117821 and R01 DK119188). M.K. is supported by a grant from the Novo Nordisk Foundation (NNF19OC0055192). E.A.R. is supported by the Novo Nordisk Foundation grants NNF17OC0027274 and NNF18OC0034072. B.K. is supported by research project grants from the Novo Nordisk Foundation (19OC0057404) and the Danish Council of Independent Research—Medical Sciences. C.C. is supported by research grants from the Lundbeck Foundation (Fellowship R238-2016-2859) and the Novo Nordisk Foundation (grant number NNF17OC0026114). A.M.F. and J.L. are supported by a research grant from the Danish Diabetes Academy (grant number NNF17SA0031406), which is funded by the Novo Nordisk Foundation. Novo Nordisk Foundation Center for Basic Metabolic Research is an independent Research Center, based at the University of Copenhagen, Denmark, and partially funded by an unconditional donation from the Novo Nordisk Foundation (www.cbmr.ku.dk) (Grant number NNF18CC0034900). Graphical icons (mice and exercise cartoons) were created using BioRender.com. S.B.J., W.L., and B.F. are working for Novo Nordisk A/S, a pharmaceutical company producing and selling medicine for the treatment of chronic diseases including diabetes and obesity. E.L.L. and H.E.P. have received an unrestricted research grant from Boehringer Ingelheim for an unrelated investigator-initiated study. R.J.S. has received research support from Zafgen, Novo Nordisk, Ionis, AstraZeneca, and Pfizer. R.J.S. has served on scientific advisory boards for Novo Nordisk, Sanofi, Scohia, Ionis, Kintai Therapeutics, and GuidePoint Consultants. R.J.S. is a stakeholder of Zafgen and Redesign Health. The other authors have declared that no competing interests exist."],"id":"S03-100.00-698285","identifier":[{"id":"000626747000021","type":"wos"},{"id":"85101481595","type":"scopus"},{"id":"33589633","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC7884842","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["ku-ae58f7a6-4bbf-4741-ac83-4a5608702be2","sdu-bfcc8b0f-5e4d-4bbc-b7ae-5d45f972ddf7","ucsyd-60026de2-df70-4958-9189-78043803e3c9"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-6-29F9BB37ACAC5F73EEA4808FC6D46FBD"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/179523009/s41467_021_21309_x.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-021-21309-x","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc",