{"abstract":"Jacket structures installed in shallow water less than 50 m for offshore wind turbines are frequently exposed to steep and even breaking waves, which may result in severe damage of the structure. However, the models for calculating wave loads were primarily developed for monopile structures, and may not be suitable for jacket structures. The aim of this paper is to improve the understanding of extreme wave loads on jacket structures exposed to steep and breaking waves, based on a combination of numerical modeling and large scale experiments. The experiments with a 1:8 scaled jacket structure exposed to controlled regular waves were conducted in the WaveSlam project. In this study, the measured wave surface elevations and water particle velocities from the experiments are reproduced using a fully nonlinear potential flow solver with a fairly good agreement. Furthermore, the validity of the Morison equation applied to jacket structures is investigated. A reasonably good agreement is achieved for the steep waves without occurrence of breaking. In terms of the breaking waves, the calculated hydrodynamic forcing is examined by subdividing it into a quasi-static and an impulsive slamming force component. The quasi-static force is well reproduced by the Morison equation given the fully nonlinear wave kinematics. Furthermore, eleven degrees of parameterization of the slamming force history are statistically analyzed for a better understanding of the slamming force characteristics. Significant variabilities are presented in these parameters, as a result of the inherent uncertainties in the wave breaking process. In the end, practical implications of the proposed slamming force description to load assessment of a realistic offshore wind jacket structure are discussed.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Wang, Shaofeng","Larsen, Torben Juul","Bredmose, Henrik"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-337893"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Wang, Shaofeng"],"keyword":["Jacket structure"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"experimental and numerical investigation of a jacket structure subject to steep and breaking regular waves","src":["dtu"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/222419074/WangEtAl_ExperimentalJacket_MarineStructures_postprint.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-337893","identifier":[{"id":"85081044348","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["dtu-6fa5779e-90f3-4cc6-9814-4c3fd47e201d"],"keyword":["Jacket structure","Numerical and experimental investigation","Slamming force","Steep and breaking waves"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-0D477EF1FABD4B5773927744CA46B77B"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/222419074/WangEtAl_ExperimentalJacket_MarineStructures_postprint.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Blocked","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2020","yearSub":"2020"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, Department of Wind Energy"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Shaofeng","key":"wangpau","last":"Wang","locid":"17f49d6a-2ba8-4205-88f0-ec4c7246de62","name":"Wang, Shaofeng","orcid":"0000-0003-3113-0468","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Torben Juul","key":"larsenpau","last":"Larsen","name":"Larsen, Torben Juul","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Henrik","key":"bredmosepau","last":"Bredmose","locid":"a46a2dd1-d040-4240-8dfb-bdb22c646a47","name":"Bredmose, Henrik","orcid":"0000-0001-6961-0753","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.marstruc.2020.102744","issn":["09518339","18734170"],"issn_org":["09518339"],"journal":"Marine Structures","paperid":"102744","publisher":"Elsevier","status":"p","title":"Marine Structures","volume":"72"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85081044348"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Technical University of Denmark","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-6fa5779e-90f3-4cc6-9814-4c3fd47e201d"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark"],"title":"Experimental and numerical investigation of a jacket structure subject to steep and breaking regular waves","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-337893","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"Flexible coil arrays are increasingly popular in magnetic resonance imaging (MRI), due to their superior anatomical fitting and patient comfort. Several coil concepts have been proposed, using self-resonant structures for the implementation of the detecting coils. In this work, we show that flexible coil arrays can be implemented in a simpler way, using standard flexible copper wire, as long as the decoupling between elements is kept high. The design approach proposed here can be extended to any frequency, and an example of a 7-channel array for 13 C at 3T (32.1 MHz) is shown, where an SNR increase is obtained for a human head phantom, compared to state-of-the-art traditional coils.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Sanchez, Juan Diego","Wang, Wenjun","Hansen, Rie Beck","Zhurbenko, Vitaliy","Ardenkjær-Larsen, Jan Henrik"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-359455"],"event":{"end":"2020-03-20","place":"Copenhagen, dk","start":"2020-03-15","title":"14<sup>th</sup> European Conference on Antennas and Propagation","year":"2020"},"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Flexible Antennas","MRI","Mutual coupling"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"enhanced low frequency mri using flexible shape arrays made of standard wire","src":["dtu"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85088655960","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-359455","identifier":[{"id":"85088655960","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["dtu-b7a43d29-ea94-49fd-a470-3dff8604fd36"],"keyword":["Flexible Antennas","MRI","Mutual Coupling"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-7D5EF0C7C0910CB8229C9F4DAA332CE8"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, Centers, Center for Hyperpolarization in Magnetic Resonance","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Health Technology","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Health Technology, Magnetic Resonance"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Juan D.","key":"sanchezherediapau","last":"Sanchez-Heredia","locid":"2d24121b-877e-4bfe-a434-9c7c3e7a34e0","name":"Sanchez, Juan Diego","name_org":"Sanchez-Heredia, Juan D.","orcid":"0000-0002-8201-4136","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Wenjun","key":"wangpau","last":"Wang","locid":"21310cb6-dd90-4add-b43a-5ada0e7d9da9","name":"Wang, Wenjun","orcid":"0000-0001-7781-9856","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Rie Beck","key":"olinpau","last":"Olin","locid":"8214cd7c-cdb4-4678-b17b-bbd3f5d87ece","name":"Hansen, Rie Beck","name_org":"Olin, Rie Beck","orcid":"0000-0003-1718-8196","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Vitaliy","key":"zhurbenkopau","last":"Zhurbenko","locid":"8449f428-fe8f-4ade-ad94-2e052869c1f4","name":"Zhurbenko, Vitaliy","orcid":"0000-0002-1515-733X","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jan H.","key":"ardenkjærlarsenpau","last":"Ardenkjær-Larsen","locid":"ae9519c9-1b64-4426-b89f-4c36d08c1836","name":"Ardenkjær-Larsen, Jan Henrik","name_org":"Ardenkjær-Larsen, Jan H.","orcid":"0000-0001-6167-6926","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.23919/eucap48036.2020.9135812","isbn":["9781728137124"],"publisher":"IEEE","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of 2020 14th European Conference on Antennas and Propagation"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85088655960"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Technical University of Denmark","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-b7a43d29-ea94-49fd-a470-3dff8604fd36"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark"],"title":"Enhanced Low Frequency MRI using Flexible Shape Arrays Made of Standard Wire","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-359455","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"The transglycosylation abilities of β-galactosidases were investigated using hyperpolarized [U-13C,U-2H]glucose as an acceptor and o-nitrophenyl β-galactopyranoside as a donor. Several products were readily observable, and at least in the case when O3 acted as an acceptor, the enzymes showed a clear selectivity toward the β-anomer of glucose. Additionally, it was possible to determine the relative hydrolysis rates of the formed transglycosylation products, providing information on the selectivity as well. Using this method, the transglycosylation abilities of the enzymes could be studied at a very high temporal resolution as well as with high sensitivity, and due to the relative ease of the setup, this method could be more generally applied to investigate glycosidases.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Kjeldsen, Christian","Ardenkjær-Larsen, Jan Henrik","Duus, Jens Øllgaard"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-363724"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Duus, Jens Øllgaard"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"unexpected anomeric acceptor preference observed using ddnp nmr for transglycosylation studies of β galactosidases","src":["dtu"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/221634658/beta_Galactosidase_postprint.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-363724","identifier":[{"id":"85089615632","type":"scopus"},{"id":"32686402","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["dtu-c6141153-63d4-4e52-a135-7a5f2f78653c"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-B28F8842F93E7A69451FB82D66831298"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/221634658/beta_Galactosidase_postprint.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2020","yearSub":"2020"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, Centers, Center for Hyperpolarization in Magnetic Resonance","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Chemistry","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Health Technology, Magnetic Resonance"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Christian","key":"kjeldsenpau","last":"Kjeldsen","locid":"00f20e6e-d673-49c4-8036-36889d60410d","name":"Kjeldsen, Christian","orcid":"0000-0002-0976-6622","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jan Henrik","key":"ardenkjærlarsenpau","last":"Ardenkjær-Larsen","locid":"ae9519c9-1b64-4426-b89f-4c36d08c1836","name":"Ardenkjær-Larsen, Jan Henrik","orcid":"0000-0001-6167-6926","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jens Øllgaard","key":"duuspau","last":"Duus","locid":"4317dfee-5351-4137-a6e2-c82bd6a3d0bf","name":"Duus, Jens Øllgaard","orcid":"0000-0003-3625-1250","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["32686402"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1021/acs.biochem.0c00390","issn":["00062960","15204995"],"issn_org":["00062960"],"issue":"31","journal":"Biochemistry","pages":"2903-2908","publisher":"American Chemical Society","status":"p","title":"Biochemistry","volume":"59"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85089615632"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Technical University of Denmark","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-c6141153-63d4-4e52-a135-7a5f2f78653c"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark"],"title":"Unexpected Anomeric Acceptor Preference Observed Using dDNP NMR for Transglycosylation Studies of β-Galactosidases","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-363724","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"In recent years, hyperpolarization of water protons via dissolution Dynamic Nuclear Polarization (dDNP) has attracted increasing interest in the magnetic resonance community. Hyperpolarized water may provide an alternative to Gd-based contrast agents for angiographic and perfusion Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) examinations, and it may report on chemical and biochemical reactions and proton exchange while perfoming Nuclear Magnetic Resonance (NMR) investigations. However, hyperpolarizing water protons is challenging. The main reason is the presence of radicals, required to create the hyperpolarized nuclear spin state. Indeed, the radicals will also be the main source of relaxation during the dissolution and transfer to the NMR or MRI system. In this work, we report water magnetizations otherwise requiring a field of 10,000 T at room temperature on a sample of pure water, by employing dDNP via UV-generated, labile radicals. We demonstrate the potential of our methodology by acquiring a 15N spectrum from natural abundance urea with a single scan, after spontaneous magnetization transfer from water protons to nitrogen nuclei.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Pinon, Arthur César","Capozzi, Andrea","Ardenkjær-Larsen, Jan Henrik"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-373394"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Ardenkjær-Larsen, Jan Henrik"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"hyperpolarized water through dissolution dynamic nuclear polarization with uv generated radicals","src":["dtu"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s42004-020-0301-6","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-373394","identifier":[{"id":"000533897200001","type":"wos"},{"id":"85084296121","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["dtu-e63c2137-7dc5-4fba-a58e-e98b03e79cc8"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-9C7215D6F0A415980BE57CD62909BACB"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/214118932/s42004_020_0301_6.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s42004-020-0301-6","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2020","yearSub":"2020"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, Centers, Center for Hyperpolarization in Magnetic Resonance","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Health Technology","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Health Technology, Magnetic Resonance"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Arthur C.","key":"pinonpau","last":"Pinon","locid":"646f6efc-6714-4f43-bbc4-3f1f7b4fe28f","name":"Pinon, Arthur César","name_org":"Pinon, Arthur C.","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Andrea","key":"capozzipau","last":"Capozzi","locid":"b0cf62b2-059b-44b4-bcf7-c10bdbeda7b9","name":"Capozzi, Andrea","orcid":"0000-0002-2306-9049","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jan Henrik","key":"ardenkjærlarsenpau","last":"Ardenkjær-Larsen","locid":"ae9519c9-1b64-4426-b89f-4c36d08c1836","name":"Ardenkjær-Larsen, Jan Henrik","orcid":"0000-0001-6167-6926","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1038/s42004-020-0301-6","issn":["23993669"],"issue":"1","journal":"Communications Chemistry","paperid":"57","publisher":"Springer Nature","status":"p","title":"Communications Chemistry","volume":"3"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85084296121"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Technical University of Denmark","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-e63c2137-7dc5-4fba-a58e-e98b03e79cc8"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark"],"title":"Hyperpolarized water through dissolution dynamic nuclear polarization with UV-generated radicals","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-373394","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"With the rise of the field of systems biology, metabolomic data have been integrated with the data for other -omic sciences, and these gigantic collections of correlated data have with the ever improving computing power, been data mined to locate biomarkers and motifs.[1] In this project the metabolic fingerprint of four prostate cancer cell lines, with different levels of aggression were analyzed. Metabolic data were obtained by incubating the cells with 13C6-d7 isotope labeled glucose, then quenching the metabolism, removing the cell debris and hyperpolarizing the metabolite extracts with dissolution Dynamic Nuclear Polarization (dDNP). By integrating the peaks of the resulting NMR spectra, a collection of metabolic data was obtained without the need for identification of specific compounds. On this data, data mining was applied, with the aim to identify biomarkers of cancer and to classify the aggressiveness of the cancer. The illustrations below show examples of obtained NMR spectra for the different cell types (on the left) and Principal Components-Discriminant Function Analysis (PCDFA) results from the four prostate cancer cell types and a breast cancer cell line, in red, (on the right). The PC-DFA is clearly able to separate the cell types, with the most aggressive clustering together (blue and green). As dDNP MNR have been shown to be quantitative and reproducible,[2] it could be an important tool in the future for cancer diagnostics.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Frahm, Anne Birk","Lundsgaard, E.","Matei, A.","Jensen, Pernille Rose","Karlsson, Magnus","Ardenkjær-Larsen, Jan Henrik","Lerche, Mathilde Hauge"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-373985"],"event":{"end":"2018-09-05","place":"Southampton , gb","start":"2018-09-02","title":"International Conference on Nuclear Hyperpolarization 2018","year":"2018"},"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"analysis of ddnp nmr metabolic data from cancer cells","src":["dtu"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/151065309/frahm_anne_dissdnpapps_bio_poster_only_2018_05_01.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-373985","ids":["dtu-e81e6ed7-8c56-4b15-a458-2a60d36a8d73"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-E5684AA1E53501407705E84205AFAFE0"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/151065309/frahm_anne_dissdnpapps_bio_poster_only_2018_05_01.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, Centers, Center for Hyperpolarization in Magnetic Resonance","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Electrical Engineering","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Electrical Engineering, Center for Magnetic Resonance"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Anne Birk","key":"frahmpau","last":"Frahm","locid":"e1c57a75-5e33-408f-b05d-077262dafeb4","name":"Frahm, Anne Birk","orcid":"0000-0001-6909-9369","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"E.","key":"lundsgaardpau","last":"Lundsgaard","name":"Lundsgaard, E.","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"A.","key":"mateipau","last":"Matei","name":"Matei, A.","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Pernille Rose","key":"jensenpau","last":"Jensen","locid":"945cf0b9-5a16-4e82-a927-c9c42c2467b2","name":"Jensen, Pernille Rose","orcid":"0000-0003-4359-848X","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Magnus","key":"karlssonpau","last":"Karlsson","locid":"b36d1ae3-33f2-4b98-9112-fbe3e155be82","name":"Karlsson, Magnus","orcid":"0000-0001-7738-1700","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jan Henrik","key":"ardenkjærlarsenpau","last":"Ardenkjær-Larsen","locid":"ae9519c9-1b64-4426-b89f-4c36d08c1836","name":"Ardenkjær-Larsen, Jan Henrik","orcid":"0000-0001-6167-6926","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Mathilde Hauge","key":"lerchepau","last":"Lerche","locid":"bf55c4cb-8ebc-4c00-a107-f90c312e6e76","name":"Lerche, Mathilde Hauge","orcid":"0000-0002-5899-2781","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","sdg":["3. Good Health and Well-being","All UN SDG Classification"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Technical University of Denmark","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-e81e6ed7-8c56-4b15-a458-2a60d36a8d73"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark"],"title":"Analysis of dDNP NMR metabolic data from cancer cells","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-373985","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"Mutual inductive coupling between elements of an MRI detector array greatly complicates the process of impedance matching of the array. This problem is even more severe in flexible arrays, where coupling matrix will change with the shape of the array. To overcome this problem a low noise amplifier can be integrated in each element with a network providing simultaneous noise matching and power mismatching for decoupling. This work describes the design of such impedance transforming networks that allow minimizing the influence of the inductive coupling and, at the same time, to ensure minimum noise Figure of the connected low noise preamplifier. Different circuit topologies are analyzed. Their performance with regard to bandwidth and noise Figure are compared. The networks ensure minimum noise Figure and decoupling level to about 28 dB. The presented analysis would be useful for MRI and antenna array designs where element coupling presents a practical problem.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Wang, Wenjun","Zhurbenko, Vitaliy","Sanchez, Juan Diego","Ardenkjær-Larsen, Jan Henrik"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-378935"],"event":{"end":"2020-03-20","place":"Copenhagen, dk","start":"2020-03-15","title":"14<sup>th</sup> European Conference on Antennas and Propagation","year":"2020"},"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Zhurbenko, Vitaliy"],"keyword":["Array","Imaging","Impedance matching","Low noise amplifiers","Magnetic resonance","Matching networks","Optimal noise matching"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"matching and decoupling networks for receive only mri arrays","src":["dtu"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85088639670","version":"Other Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["ACKNOWLEDGMENT This work was supported in part by the Danish National Research Foundation under grant DNRF 124."],"id":"S01-000.00-378935","identifier":[{"id":"85088639670","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["dtu-f8888b3e-63bb-42d0-be98-8f5637882760"],"keyword":["Array","Imaging","Impedance matching","Low noise amplifiers","Magnetic resonance","Matching bandwidths","Matching networks","Optimal noise matching"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-6B4D16327078CE05B8DC2E0B5A6B1D94"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, Centers, Center for Hyperpolarization in Magnetic Resonance","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Electrical Engineering","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Electrical Engineering, Electromagnetic Systems","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Health Technology, Magnetic Resonance"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Wenjun","key":"wangpau","last":"Wang","locid":"21310cb6-dd90-4add-b43a-5ada0e7d9da9","name":"Wang, Wenjun","orcid":"0000-0001-7781-9856","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Vitaliy","key":"zhurbenkopau","last":"Zhurbenko","locid":"8449f428-fe8f-4ade-ad94-2e052869c1f4","name":"Zhurbenko, Vitaliy","orcid":"0000-0002-1515-733X","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Juan Diego","key":"sanchezherediapau","last":"Sanchez-Heredia","locid":"2d24121b-877e-4bfe-a434-9c7c3e7a34e0","name":"Sanchez, Juan Diego","name_org":"Sanchez-Heredia, Juan Diego","orcid":"0000-0002-8201-4136","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jan Henrik","key":"ardenkjærlarsenpau","last":"Ardenkjær-Larsen","locid":"ae9519c9-1b64-4426-b89f-4c36d08c1836","name":"Ardenkjær-Larsen, Jan Henrik","orcid":"0000-0001-6167-6926","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.23919/eucap48036.2020.9135676","eisbn":["9788831299008"],"publisher":"IEEE","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of 14<sup>th</sup> European Conference on Antennas and Propagation"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85088639670"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Technical University of Denmark","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-f8888b3e-63bb-42d0-be98-8f5637882760"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark"],"title":"Matching and decoupling networks for receive-only MRI arrays","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-378935","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"The chapter presents the dominant discourse in Danish foreign policy. The dominant dis­course articulates the EU as essential and the key platform for Danish foreign policy, while NATO and the United States are also articulated as crucial if mainly in the field of security. The articulation of an activism that breaks with the strategic passivity in the past is the background for Denmark’s participation in conflicts in Syria and Iraq. The UN and in particular Nordic cooperation are not attributed the same value as the EU and NA­TO/the United States. However, particularly from the Foreign and Security Policy Strate­gy 2019–20, tendencies towards an even stronger Danish emphasis on the EU, multilater­alism, international rules, and on issue areas such as security in the neighbourhood, im­migration, the Arctic, and trade are identified. The chapter raises the question of whether theensembleof these tendencies will challenge or reinforce the EU’s and NATO’s central roles","author_count":1,"contributors":["Larsen, Henrik"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-409479"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Activism","Denmark","Discourse","EU","Faculty of Social Sciences","Foreign policy","NATO"],"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"foreign policy new directions in a changing world order","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dba","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-409479","ids":["ku-1adc2784-b0cf-410a-8805-24b75f79025b"],"keyword":["Denmark","Discourse","EU","Faculty of Social Sciences","NATO","activism","foreign policy"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-E44E980811C7F2E164B8B96EE2BDB447"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Social Sciences, Department of Political Science","Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Social Sciences, Department of Political Science, Centre for European Politics","Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Social Sciences, Department of Political Science, International Relations"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Henrik","key":"larsenpau","last":"Larsen","locid":"36bbf99a-f2ff-4406-b1ca-f9cb7be18f17","name":"Larsen, Henrik","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1093/oxfordhb/9780198833598.013.27","pages":"470-487","place":"Oxford","publisher":"Oxford University Press","status":"p","title":"The Oxford Handbook of Danish Politics"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-1adc2784-b0cf-410a-8805-24b75f79025b"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Foreign Policy : New Directions in a Changing World Order?","type":"Book Chapter","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-409479","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"Den måde, man bruger de forskellige begreber på, når man analyserer EU, betyder meget for, hvad man kommer frem til. Et godt eksempel er udenrigspolitikken, hvor forskere i 27 år har begået en afgørende misforståelse","author_count":1,"contributors":["Larsen, Henrik"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-453116"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"level":"pop","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"und","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"er eu en normativ magt eller et permanent flop","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"doi","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","text":"Undefined","type":"url","url":"https://videnskab.dk/kultur-samfund/er-eu-en-normativ-magt-eller-et-permanent-flop","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-453116","ids":["ku-63cfee99-c5e4-487f-a0e0-d90460aa76ea"],"lang":"Danish","level":"Popular","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Local-1-1-963A8DC84066482DC311CCEB570BDE30"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Social Sciences, Department of Political Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Henrik","key":"larsenpau","last":"Larsen","locid":"36bbf99a-f2ff-4406-b1ca-f9cb7be18f17","name":"Larsen, Henrik","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"X"},"review":"Undetermined","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Undefined"]},"sid":["ku-63cfee99-c5e4-487f-a0e0-d90460aa76ea"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Er EU en normativ magt eller et permanent flop?","type":"Other-Internet Publication","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-453116","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"The initial drug release from in situ forming implants is affected by factors such as the physicochemical properties of the active pharmaceutical ingredient, the type of the excipients utilized, and the surrounding environment. The feasibility of UV-vis imaging for characterization of the initial behavior of poly(Dplactide-co-glycolide) (PLGA)/1-methyl-2-pyrrolidinone (NMP) in situ forming implants was investigated. The in vitro release of leuprolide acetate (LA) and implant formation in real time were monitored using dual-wavelength imaging at 280 and 525 nm, respectively, in matrices based on agarose gel and hyaluronic acid (HA) solution emulating the subcutaneous matrix. Three hours upon injection of the pre-formulation, approximately 15% of the total amount of LA administered was found in the agarose gel, while 5% was released from the implant into the HA solution. Concurrently, more extensive swelling of the implants in the HA solution as compared to implants in the agarose gel was observed. Transport of both LA and the solvent NMP was investigated using UV-vis imaging in a small-scale cell where the geometry of the formulation was controlled, showing a linear correlation between drug release and solvent escape. Light microscopy showed that the microstructures of the resulting implants in agarose gel and HA solution were different, which may be attributed to the different solvent exchange rates. UV imaging was also used to examine the interaction of LA with the release medium by characterizing the diffusion of LA in agarose gel, HA solution, and phosphate buffered saline. The reduced LA diffusivity in HA solution as compared to agarose gel and the LA distribution coefficient in the agarose gel-HA system indicated the presence of interactions between LA and HA Our findings show that the external environment affects the solvent exchange kinetics for in situ forming implants in vitro, resulting in different types of initial release behavior. UV-vis imaging in combination with biorelevant matrices may offer an interesting approach in the development of in situ forming implant delivery systems.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Li, Zhuoxuan","Mu, Huiling","Larsen, Susan Weng","Jensen, Henrik","Østergaard, Jesper"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-454884"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Delivery","Diffusion","Drug release","In vitro release","Injectable","Lactide","MODEL","PLGA","SYSTEM","UV Vis imaging","VIVO PERFORMANCE"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"initial leuprolide acetate release from poly d l lactide co glycolide in situ forming implants as studied by ultraviolet visible imaging","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-454884","identifier":[{"id":"000599194800010","type":"wos"},{"id":"33164519","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85096614091","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-66e4a71a-bd5d-4e5f-b4aa-0158f2fefcd7"],"keyword":["DELIVERY","DIFFUSION","DRUG-RELEASE","HYDROGEL MATRIX MIMICKING","LACTIDE","MODEL","PHASE INVERSION DYNAMICS","PLGA","SYSTEM","UV-vis imaging","VITRO RELEASE","VIVO PERFORMANCE","biorelevant matrix","in situ forming implant","in vitro release","injectable"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-B4F11264A1325BE40F4A4F7B5F706337"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2020","yearSub":"2020"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Pharmacy, Pharmaceutical Physical and Analytical Chemistry"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Zhuoxuan","key":"lipau","last":"Li","locid":"1b9299d7-bca7-499b-8e06-734921531509","name":"Li, Zhuoxuan","orcid":"0000-0002-9380-888X","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Huiling","key":"mupau","last":"Mu","locid":"f112da1b-f1ab-4891-bf6a-3c799d71d4cc","name":"Mu, Huiling","orcid":"0000-0001-9246-3931","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Susan Weng","key":"larsenpau","last":"Larsen","locid":"f6980004-978f-4940-b71f-de426f2a084a","name":"Larsen, Susan Weng","orcid":"0000-0002-3818-7275","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Henrik","key":"jensenpau","last":"Jensen","locid":"88cd8faf-69c6-46de-a355-03f93d52eeb1","name":"Jensen, Henrik","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Jesper","key":"ostergaardpau","last":"Ostergaard","locid":"718ffee9-f57d-4060-9440-4ade85c29600","name":"Østergaard, Jesper","name_org":"Ostergaard, Jesper","orcid":"0000-0003-3448-4237","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["33164519"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1021/acs.molpharmaceut.0c00625","issn":["15438384","15438392"],"issn_org":["15438384"],"issue":"12","journal":"Molecular Pharmaceutics","pages":"4522-4532","status":"p","title":"Molecular Pharmaceutics","volume":"17"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85096614091"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-66e4a71a-bd5d-4e5f-b4aa-0158f2fefcd7"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Initial Leuprolide Acetate Release from Poly(D,L-lactide-co-glycolide) in Situ Forming Implants as Studied by Ultraviolet-Visible Imaging","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-454884","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"Protein N-glycosylation is an essential and highly conserved posttranslational modification found in all eukaryotic cells. Yeast, plants and mammalian cells, however, produce N-glycans with distinct structural features. These species-specific features not only pose challenges in selecting host cells for production of recombinant therapeutics for human medical use but also provide opportunities to explore and utilize species-specific glycosylation in design of vaccines. Here, we used reverse cross-species engineering to stably introduce plant core alpha 3fucose (alpha 3Fuc) and beta 2xylose (beta 2Xyl) N-glycosylation epitopes in the mammalian Chinese hamster ovary (CHO) cell line. We used directed knockin of plant core fucosylation and xylosylation genes (AtFucTA/AtFucTB and AtXylT) and targeted knockout of endogenous genes for core fucosylation (fut8) and elongation (B4galt1), for establishing CHO cells with plant N-glycosylation capacities. The engineering was evaluated through coexpression of two human therapeutic N-glycoproteins, erythropoietin (EPO) and an immunoglobulin G (IgG) antibody. Full conversion to the plant-type alpha 3Fuc/beta 2Xyl bi-antennary agalactosylated N-glycosylation (G0FX) was demonstrated for the IgG1 produced in CHO cells. These results demonstrate that N-glycosylation in mammalian cells is amenable for extensive cross-kingdom engineering and that engineered CHO cells may be used to produce glycoproteins with plant glycosylation.","author_count":8,"contributors":["Larsen, Joachim Steen","Karlsson, Richard","Tian, Weihua","Schulz, Morten Alder","Matthes, Annemarie","Clausen, Henrik","Petersen, Bent Larsen","Yang, Zhang"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-454976"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Biosynthesis","CHO Cells","Chinese hamster ovary cells","Fucose","Glycans","Lewis","Monoclonal antibodies","O glycosylation","Xylose","core fucose"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"engineering mammalian cells to produce plant specific n glycosylation on proteins","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-454976","identifier":[{"id":"000582710300004","type":"wos"},{"id":"85088609996","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-670db0c2-7abb-4c23-a2a9-37b3f4399fd6"],"keyword":["ALPHA-4-FUCOSYL-TRANSFERASE","BIOSYNTHESIS","CHO-CELLS","Chinese hamster ovary cells","FUCOSE","GLYCANS","IGG-FC","LEWIS","MONOCLONAL-ANTIBODIES","O-GLYCOSYLATION","XYLOSE","core fucose","core xylose","cross-species glycoengineering","plant-type N-glycosylation"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-31D3CA0224CCCB2FE97DB6DC718A4033"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2020","yearSub":"2020"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Cellular and Molecular Medicine, Glycomics Program","Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Plant and Environmental Sciences, Section for Plant Glycobiology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Joachim Steen","key":"larsenpau","last":"Larsen","locid":"5159dc60-aef3-42a1-9aab-a58d7b4fc91b","name":"Larsen, Joachim Steen","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Richard Torbjorn Gustav","key":"karlssonpau","last":"Karlsson","locid":"744db30f-e1bc-48d0-980c-019b474e9b53","name":"Karlsson, Richard","name_org":"Karlsson, Richard Torbjorn Gustav","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Weihua","key":"tianpau","last":"Tian","locid":"9c94707a-e04c-4c99-b4b6-3adce8a13103","name":"Tian, Weihua","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Morten Alder","key":"schulzpau","last":"Schulz","locid":"2bc935b8-6d66-4e6c-872a-4d5b41a96c04","name":"Schulz, Morten Alder","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Annemarie","key":"matthespau","last":"Matthes","locid":"267a6151-5e70-4915-93e7-0d7b6dc51ede","name":"Matthes, Annemarie","orcid":"0000-0001-8326-4842","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Henrik","key":"clausenpau","last":"Clausen","locid":"a7882c09-1275-4857-8b5b-7e3efc1d088d","name":"Clausen, Henrik","orcid":"0000-0002-0915-5055","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Bent L","key":"petersenpau","last":"Petersen","locid":"0d3fa717-88a9-444e-8096-3163ca10ec75","name":"Petersen, Bent Larsen","name_org":"Petersen, Bent L","orcid":"0000-0002-2004-9077","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Zhang","key":"yangpau","last":"Yang","locid":"69786fb3-302a-41d6-b1f4-a3565265efc7","name":"Yang, Zhang","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1093/glycob/cwaa009","issn":["09596658","14602423"],"issn_org":["09596658"],"issue":"8","journal":"Glycobiology","pages":"528-538","status":"p","title":"Glycobiology","volume":"30"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85088609996"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-670db0c2-7abb-4c23-a2a9-37b3f4399fd6"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Engineering mammalian cells to produce plant-specific N-glycosylation on proteins","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-454976","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"Protein glycosylation plays essential roles in protein structure, stability, and activity such as cell adhesion. The cadherin superfamily of adhesion molecules carry O-linked mannose glycans at conserved sites and it was recently demonstrated that the transmembrane and tetratricopeptide repeat-containing proteins 1-4 (TMTC1-4) gene products contribute to the addition of these O-linked mannoses. Here, biochemical, cell biological, and organismal analysis was used to determine that TMTC3 supports the O-mannosylation of E-cadherin, cellular adhesion, and embryonic gastrulation. Using genetically engineered cells lacking all four TMTC genes, overexpression of TMTC3 rescued O-linked glycosylation of E-cadherin and cell adherence. The knockdown of the Tmtcs in Xenopus laevis embryos caused a delay in gastrulation that was rescued by the addition of human TMTC3. Mutations in TMTC3 have been linked to neuronal cell migration diseases including Cobblestone lissencephaly. Analysis of TMTC3 mutations associated with Cobblestone lissencephaly found that three of the variants exhibit reduced stability and missence mutations were unable to complement TMTC3 rescue of gastrulation in Xenopus embryo development. Our study demonstrates that TMTC3 regulates O-linked glycosylation and cadherin-mediated adherence, providing insight into its effect on cellular adherence and migration, as well the basis of TMTC3-associated Cobblestone lissencephaly.","author_count":10,"contributors":["Graham, Jill B.","Sunryd, Johan C.","Mathavan, Ketan","Weir, Emma","Larsen, Ida Signe Bohse","Halim, Adnan","Clausen, Henrik","Cousin, Hélène","Alfandari, Dominque","Hebert, Daniel N."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-478780"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"country":["United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Massachusetts Amherst"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"endoplasmic reticulum transmembrane protein tmtc000000000003 contributes to o mannosylation of e cadherin cellular adherence and embryonic gastrulation","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-SA","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1091/mbc.E19-07-0408","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-478780","identifier":[{"id":"PMC7001481","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"85082144581","type":"scopus"},{"id":"31851597","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-8ec09252-0218-446d-91f0-65f503f3bdf6"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-D54291299B8F1816A0A460C80C29DDBF"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-SA","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1091/mbc.E19-07-0408","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-SA","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/290595329/Endoplasmic_reticulum_transmembrane_protein.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2020","yearSub":"2020"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"us","key":"university of massachusetts amherst","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"University of Massachusetts Amherst","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Cellular and Molecular Medicine, Glycomics Program"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Jill B.","key":"grahampau","last":"Graham","name":"Graham, Jill B.","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Johan C.","key":"sunrydpau","last":"Sunryd","name":"Sunryd, Johan C.","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Ketan","key":"mathavanpau","last":"Mathavan","name":"Mathavan, Ketan","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Emma","key":"weirpau","last":"Weir","name":"Weir, Emma","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Ida Signe Bohse","key":"larsenpau","last":"Larsen","locid":"f2869bf8-54a4-49ae-9db2-434d2714b362","name":"Larsen, Ida Signe Bohse","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Adnan","key":"halimpau","last":"Halim","locid":"65f08640-6203-4f49-ad7e-f95cb9c070b8","name":"Halim, Adnan","orcid":"0000-0003-1629-187X","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Henrik","key":"clausenpau","last":"Clausen","locid":"a7882c09-1275-4857-8b5b-7e3efc1d088d","name":"Clausen, Henrik","orcid":"0000-0002-0915-5055","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Hélène","key":"cousinpau","last":"Cousin","name":"Cousin, Hélène","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Dominque","key":"alfandaripau","last":"Alfandari","name":"Alfandari, Dominque","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Daniel N.","key":"hebertpau","last":"Hebert","name":"Hebert, Daniel N.","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["31851597"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1091/mbc.e19-07-0408","issn":["10591524","19394586"],"issn_org":["10591524"],"issue":"3","journal":"Molecular Biology of the Cell","pages":"167-183","status":"p","title":"Molecular Biology of the Cell","volume":"31"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85082144581"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-8ec09252-0218-446d-91f0-65f503f3bdf6"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Endoplasmic reticulum transmembrane protein TMTC3 contributes to O-mannosylation of E-cadherin, cellular adherence, and embryonic gastrulation","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-478780","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"Acute and adaptive changes in systemic markers of oxidatively generated nucleic acid modifications (i.e., 8-oxo-7,8-dihydro-2ʹ-deoxyguanosine (8-oxodG) and 8-oxo-7,8-dihydroguanosine (8-oxoGuo)) as well as inflammatory cytokines (i.e., C-reactive protein, interleukin-6, interleukin-10, and tumour necrosis factor alpha), a liver hormone (i.e., fibroblast growth factor 21 (FGF21)), and bone metabolism markers (sclerostin, osteocalcin, C-terminal telopeptide, and N-terminal propeptide of type 1 procollagen) were investigated following a marathon in 20 study participants. Immediate changes were observed in inflammatory cytokines, FGF21, and bone metabolism markers following the marathon. In contrast, no immediate changes in urinary excretion of 8-oxodG and 8-oxoGuo were evident. Four days after the marathon, decreased urinary excretion of 8-oxodG (-2.9 (95% CI -4.8;-1.1) nmol/24 h, P < 0.01) and 8-oxoGuo (-5.8 (95% CI -10.3;-1.3) nmol/24 h, P = 0.02) was observed. The excretion rate of 8-oxodG remained decreased 7 days after the marathon compared to baseline (-2.3 (95%CI -4.3;-0.4) nmol/24 h, P = 0.02), whereas the excretion rate of 8-oxoGuo was normalized. In conclusion marathon participation immediately induced a considerable inflammatory response, but did not increase excretion rates of oxidatively generated nucleic acid modifications. In fact, a delayed decrease in oxidatively generated nucleic acid modifications was observed suggesting adaptive antioxidative effects following exercise. Abbreviations: 8-oxodG: 8-oxo-7,8-dihydro-2ʹ-deoxyguanosine; 8-oxoGuo: 8-oxo-7,8-dihydroguanosine; CI: confidence interval; CTX: C-terminal telopeptide of type 1 collagen; DXA: dual-energy X-ray absorptiometry; ELISA: enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay; FGF21: Fibroblast growth factor 21; h: hour; hsCRP: high sensitivity C-reactive protein; IL: interleukin; IQR: interquartile range; MS: mass spectrometry: P1NP: N-terminal propeptide of type 1 procollagen; TNFα: tumour necrosis factor alpha; UPLC: ultra-performance liquid chromatography.","author_count":14,"contributors":["Larsen, Emil List","Poulsen, Henrik Enghusen","Michaelsen, Cristina","Kjær, Laura Kofoed","Lyngbæk, Mark","Andersen, Emilie Skytte","Petersen-Bønding, Christina","Lemoine, Clara","Gillum, Matthew P.","Jørgensen, Niklas Rye","Ploug, Thorkil","Vilsbøll, Tina","Knop, Filip K.","Karstoft, Kristian"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-499940"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Bone metabolism","Exercise","Fibroblast growth factor 21","Inflammation","Oxidative stress"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"differential time responses in inflammatory and oxidative stress markers after a marathon an observational study","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-499940","identifier":[{"id":"85087130837","type":"scopus"},{"id":"32530734","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-b259afc8-375b-4827-8487-d5a52130d68c"],"keyword":["Bone metabolism","exercise","fibroblast growth factor 21","inflammation","oxidative stress"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-A385452A42B662C9EDCFD9402F633541"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2020","yearSub":"2020"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Clinical Pharmacology","Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","first":"Emil List","key":"larsenpau","last":"Larsen","name":"Larsen, Emil List","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Henrik Enghusen","key":"poulsenpau","last":"Poulsen","locid":"8fb54acd-3a26-48f0-9111-b8e5535a0431","name":"Poulsen, Henrik Enghusen","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Cristina","key":"michaelsenpau","last":"Michaelsen","name":"Michaelsen, Cristina","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Laura Kofoed","key":"kjærpau","last":"Kjær","name":"Kjær, Laura Kofoed","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Mark","key":"lyngbækpau","last":"Lyngbæk","name":"Lyngbæk, Mark","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Emilie Skytte","key":"andersenpau","last":"Andersen","locid":"914faedd-d3d2-44d2-8e5e-e00c4067fb07","name":"Andersen, Emilie Skytte","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Christina","key":"petersenbøndingpau","last":"Petersen-Bønding","name":"Petersen-Bønding, Christina","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Clara","key":"lemoinepau","last":"Lemoine","name":"Lemoine, Clara","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Matthew","key":"gillumpau","last":"Gillum","locid":"65c5e8eb-5634-45db-952e-d28bf525bd32","name":"Gillum, Matthew P.","name_org":"Gillum, Matthew","orcid":"0000-0003-4893-012X","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Niklas Rye","key":"jørgensenpau","last":"Jørgensen","locid":"3dc68a95-e26c-4b82-a4f0-7548313f8045","name":"Jørgensen, Niklas Rye","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Thorkil","key":"plougpau","last":"Ploug","locid":"ab66e714-8f43-47de-847c-d3d274315d2d","name":"Ploug, Thorkil","orcid":"0000-0002-5375-6595","rank":11,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Tina","key":"vilsbøllpau","last":"Vilsbøll","locid":"76773372-af8c-45a0-9447-a509cbf6d466","name":"Vilsbøll, Tina","orcid":"0000-0002-0456-6787","rank":12,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Filip Krag","key":"knoppau","last":"Knop","locid":"ce6eaad9-59f8-4ecf-8a1e-388a74734f6a","name":"Knop, Filip K.","name_org":"Knop, Filip Krag","rank":13,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Kristian","key":"karstoftpau","last":"Karstoft","name":"Karstoft, Kristian","rank":14,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["32530734"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1080/02640414.2020.1770918","issn":["02640414","1466447X"],"issn_org":["02640414"],"issue":"18","journal":"Journal of Sports Sciences","pages":"2080-2091","status":"p","title":"Journal of Sports Sciences","volume":"38"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85087130837"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-b259afc8-375b-4827-8487-d5a52130d68c"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Differential time responses in inflammatory and oxidative stress markers after a marathon : An observational study","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-499940","year":"2020","yearsub":"2020"}
{"abstract":"Sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitors (SGLT2i) improve hard renal outcomes in type 2 diabetes. This is possibly explained by the fact that SGLT2i normalize the measured glomerular filtration rate (mGFR) by increasing renal vascular resistance, as was shown in young people with type 1 diabetes and glomerular hyperfiltration. Therefore, we compared the renal hemodynamic effects of dapagliflozin with gliclazide in type 2 diabetes. The mGFR and effective renal plasma flow were assessed using inulin and para-aminohippurate clearances in the fasted state, during clamped euglycemia (5 mmol/L) and during clamped hyperglycemia (15 mmol/L). Filtration fraction and renal vascular resistance were calculated. Additionally, factors known to modulate renal hemodynamics were measured. In 44 people with type 2 diabetes on metformin monotherapy (Hemoglobin A1c 7.4%, mGFR 113 mL/min), dapagliflozin versus gliclazide reduced mGFR by 5, 10, and 12 mL/min in the consecutive phases while both agents similarly improved Hemoglobin A1c (-0.48% vs -0.65%). Dapagliflozin also reduced filtration fraction without increasing renal vascular resistance, and increased urinary adenosine and prostaglandin concentrations. Gliclazide did not consistently alter renal hemodynamic parameters. Thus, beyond glucose control, SGLT2i reduce mGFR and filtration fraction in type 2 diabetes. The fact that renal vascular resistance was not increased by dapagliflozin suggests that this is due to post-glomerular vasodilation rather than pre-glomerular vasoconstriction.","author_count":17,"contributors":["van Bommel, Erik J.M.","Muskiet, Marcel H.A.","van Baar, Michaël J.B.","Tonneijck, Lennart","Smits, Mark M.","Emanuel, Anna L.","Bozovic, Andrea","Danser, A. H.Jan","Geurts, Frank","Hoorn, Ewout J.","Touw, Daan J.","Larsen, Emil L.","Poulsen, Henrik Enghusen","Kramer, Mark H.H.","Nieuwdorp, Max","Joles, Jaap A.","van Raalte, Daniël H."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-539611"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsr