he Performativity of Concepts in the Study of European Foreign Policy\", paper presented at the 2019 Biennial Conference, 9-11 May, 2019, Denver, Colorado, Conference panel 1J, EU Actorness revisited.","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-433475","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"Tumor eradication may be greatly improved by targeting cancer stem cells (CSCs), as they exhibit resistance to conventional therapy. To gain insight into the unique biology of CSCs, we developed patient-derived xenograft tumors (PDXs) from ER− breast cancers from which we isolated mammospheres that are enriched for CSCs. Comparative global proteomic analysis was performed on patient tumor tissues and corresponding PDXs and mammospheres. Mammospheres exhibited increased expression of proteins associated with de novo cholesterol synthesis. The clinical relevance of increased cholesterol biosynthesis was verified in a large breast cancer cohort showing correlation with shorter relapse-free survival. RNAi and chemical inhibition of the cholesterol biosynthesis pathway reduced mammosphere formation, which could be rescued by a downstream metabolite. Our findings identify the cholesterol biosynthesis pathway as central for CSC propagation and a potential therapeutic target, as well as providing a mechanistic explanation for the therapeutic benefit of statins in breast cancer. Ehmsen et al. identify cholesterol biosynthesis as essential for breast cancer stem cell propagation. Increased expression of multiple cholesterol biosynthesis proteins is observed in mammospheres, and the expression levels of these proteins correlate with the outcome of basal-like breast cancer patients. Inhibition of the cholesterol biosynthesis pathway reduces mammosphere formation.","author_count":9,"contributors":["Ehmsen, Sidse","Pedersen, Martin Haar","Wang, Guisong","Terp, Mikkel Green","Arslanagic, Amina","Hood, Brian L.","Conrads, Thomas P.","Leth-Larsen, Rikke","Ditzel, Henrik"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-617465"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Ditzel, Henrik"],"country":["United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Inova Center for Personalized Health","Walter Reed National Military Medical Center"],"keyword":["Breast cancer stem cells","Mammospheres"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"increased cholesterol biosynthesis is a key characteristic of breast cancer stem cells influencing patient outcome","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.celrep.2019.05.104","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["We would like to thank Martin Bak, Anne Marie Bak Jylling, Lisbet Mortensen, and Ole Nielsen at the Department of Pathology, Odense University Hospital, for providing us with patient tumor tissues and excellent technical assistance with the immunocytochemical and immunohistochemical stainings; Anette Rasmussen for excellent technical assistance; and M. Kat Occhipinti for editorial assistance. This work was supported by the Danish Cancer Society (to H.J.D.), the Region of Southern Denmark Research Council (to R.L.-L.), A Race Against Breast Cancer (to H.J.D.), the Odense University Hospital Research Council (to H.J.D.), and the Academy of Geriatric Cancer Research (AgeCare) (to H.J.D.)."],"id":"S01-000.00-617465","identifier":[{"id":"31242424","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85067498397","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["sdu-9a9db11f-638c-4f76-965f-6614d3384055"],"keyword":["PDX tumors","breast cancer stem cells","cholesterol biosynthesis pathway","mammospheres","proteome profiling"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-A190F7A549A195E2A223766EAE2223D4"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/151669346/Increased_Cholesterol_Biosynthesis_Is_a_Key_Characteristic_of_Breast_Cancer_Stem_Cells_Influencing_Patient_Outcome.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.celrep.2019.05.104","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2019","yearSub":"2019"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Clinical Research, KI, OUH, Research unit of Oncology (Odense)","SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Molecular Medicine, Department of Cancer and Inflammation Research","SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Molecular Medicine, Ditzel group"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"us","key":"walter reed national military medical center","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Walter Reed National Military Medical Center","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"us","key":"inova center for personalized health","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Inova Center for Personalized Health","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Sidse","key":"ehmsenpau","last":"Ehmsen","locid":"c839634d-08df-43e0-9e69-c8abc04d56cc","name":"Ehmsen, Sidse","orcid":"0000-0002-4807-6325","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Martin H.","key":"pedersenpau","last":"Pedersen","locid":"05ea4510-d97c-4e20-952b-315077d40968","name":"Pedersen, Martin Haar","name_org":"Pedersen, Martin H.","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Guisong","key":"wangpau","last":"Wang","name":"Wang, Guisong","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Mikkel G.","key":"terppau","last":"Terp","locid":"31863af3-7d3a-48d5-ae0a-e76e938a23fb","name":"Terp, Mikkel Green","name_org":"Terp, Mikkel G.","orcid":"0000-0002-4468-6900","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Amina","key":"arslanagicpau","last":"Arslanagic","locid":"5ed51e77-ccc7-40a4-b2a4-40cd54c76d8d","name":"Arslanagic, Amina","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Brian L.","key":"hoodpau","last":"Hood","name":"Hood, Brian L.","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 3","corresponding":null,"first":"Thomas P.","key":"conradspau","last":"Conrads","name":"Conrads, Thomas P.","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Rikke","key":"lethlarsenpau","last":"Leth-Larsen","locid":"66fd89b9-b81d-4e74-bf02-9a0ea313ee3e","name":"Leth-Larsen, Rikke","orcid":"0000-0002-1612-4089","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Henrik J.","key":"ditzelpau","last":"Ditzel","locid":"f10637d7-ca3b-4956-a007-100f66737838","name":"Ditzel, Henrik","name_org":"Ditzel, Henrik J.","orcid":"0000-0003-3927-5135","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["31242424"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.celrep.2019.05.104","issn":["22111247"],"issue":"13","journal":"Cell Reports","publisher":"Cell Press","status":"p","title":"Cell Reports","volume":"27"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85067498397"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-9a9db11f-638c-4f76-965f-6614d3384055"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Increased Cholesterol Biosynthesis Is a Key Characteristic of Breast Cancer Stem Cells Influencing Patient Outcome","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-617465","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"Background: Although tumor necrosis factor (TNF) inhibitors are used to treat chronic inflammatory diseases, there is little information about how long-term inhibition of TNF affects the homeostatic functions that TNF maintains in the intact CNS. Materials and methods: To assess whether developmental TNF deficiency causes alterations in the naïve CNS, we estimated the number of proliferating cells, microglia, and neurons in the developing neocortex of E13.5, P7 and adult TNF knock out (TNF −/−) mice and wildtype (WT) littermates. We also measured changes in gene and protein expression and monoamine levels in adult WT and TNF −/− mice. To evaluate long-term effects of TNF inhibitors, we treated healthy adult C57BL/6 mice with either saline, the selective soluble TNF inhibitor XPro1595, or the nonselective TNF inhibitor etanercept. We estimated changes in cell number and protein expression after two months of treatment. We assessed the effects of TNF deficiency on cognition by testing adult WT and TNF −/− mice and mice treated with saline, XPro1595, or etanercept with specific behavioral tasks. Results: TNF deficiency decreased the number of proliferating cells and microglia and increased the number of neurons. At the same time, TNF deficiency decreased the expression of WNT signaling-related proteins, specifically Collagen Triple Helix Repeat Containing 1 (CTHRC1) and Frizzled receptor 6 (FZD6). In contrast to XPro1595, long-term inhibition of TNF with etanercept in adult C57BL/6 mice decreased the number of BrdU + cells in the granule cell layer of the dentate gyrus. Etanercept, but not XPro1595, also impaired spatial learning and memory in the Barnes maze memory test. Conclusion: TNF deficiency impacts the organization of neurogenic zones and alters the cell composition in brain. Long-term inhibition of TNF with the nonselective TNF inhibitor etanercept, but not the soluble TNF inhibitor XPro1595, decreases neurogenesis in the adult mouse hippocampus and impairs learning and memory after two months of treatment.","author_count":20,"contributors":["Yli-Karjanmaa, Minna Liisa Kyllikki","Larsen, Kathrine Solevad","Fenger, Christina","Kristensen, Lotte Kellemann","Martin, Nellie Anne","Jensen, Peter Toft","Breton, Alexandre","Nathanson, Lubov","Nielsen, Pernille Vinther","Lund, Minna Christiansen","Lindeman Carlsen, Stephanie","Gramsbergen, Jan Bert","Finsen, Bente","Stubbe, Jane","Frich, Lars Henrik","Stolp, Helen","Brambilla, Roberta","Anthony, Daniel C.","Meyer, Morten","Lambertsen, Kate Lykke"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-637332"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["United Kingdom","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Nova Southeastern University","Royal Veterinary College","University of Miami","University of Oxford"],"keyword":["Cytokines","Neurogenesis","TNF inhibitors"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"tnf deficiency causes alterations in the spatial organization of neurogenic zones and alters the number of microglia and neurons in the cerebral cortex","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bbi.2019.08.195","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-637332","identifier":[{"id":"85072208556","type":"scopus"},{"id":"31505254","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["sdu-dfe0815d-2658-49b9-b831-b199150ba675"],"keyword":["Cytokines","Neurogenesis","TNF inhibitors"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-70853774586125B01F13181B51E4A350"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearc