{"abstract":"This white paper discusses prospects for advancing hyperpolarization technology to better understand cancer metabolism, identify current obstacles to HP (hyperpolarized) 13C magnetic resonance imaging's (MRI's) widespread clinical use, and provide recommendations for overcoming them. Since the publication of the first NIH white paper on hyperpolarized 13C MRI in 2011, preclinical studies involving [1-13C]pyruvate as well a number of other 13C labeled metabolic substrates have demonstrated this technology's capacity to provide unique metabolic information. A dose-ranging study of HP [1-13C]pyruvate in patients with prostate cancer established safety and feasibility of this technique. Additional studies are ongoing in prostate, brain, breast, liver, cervical, and ovarian cancer. Technology for generating and delivering hyperpolarized agents has evolved, and new MR data acquisition sequences and improved MRI hardware have been developed. It will be important to continue investigation and development of existing and new probes in animal models. Improved polarization technology, efficient radiofrequency coils, and reliable pulse sequences are all important objectives to enable exploration of the technology in healthy control subjects and patient populations. It will be critical to determine how HP 13C MRI might fill existing needs in current clinical research and practice, and complement existing metabolic imaging modalities. Financial sponsorship and integration of academia, industry, and government efforts will be important factors in translating the technology for clinical research in oncology. This white paper is intended to provide recommendations with this goal in mind.","author_count":24,"contributors":["Kurhanewicz, John","Vigneron, Daniel B","Ardenkjaer-Larsen, Jan Henrik","Bankson, James A","Brindle, Kevin","Cunningham, Charles H","Gallagher, Ferdia A","Keshari, Kayvan R","Kjaer, Andreas","Laustsen, Christoffer","Mankoff, David A","Merritt, Matthew E","Nelson, Sarah J","Pauly, John M","Lee, Philips","Ronen, Sabrina","Tyler, Damian J","Rajan, Sunder S","Spielman, Daniel M","Wald, Lawrence","Zhang, Xiaoliang","Malloy, Craig R","Rizi, Rahim"],"cuid":["S03-087.38-013673"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":3,"continent":["America, North","Asia, South","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Kurhanewicz, John"],"country":["Canada","Singapore","United Kingdom","United States"],"glorg":["Agency for Science, Technology and Research","Agency for Science, Technology and Research, Singapore","All Global Affiliations","Harvard University","MD Anderson Medical Center","Memorial Sloan-Kettering Cancer Center","Samuel Lunenfeld Research Institute, Mount Sinai Hospital, Toronto","Stanford University","University of California","University of California at Berkeley","University of California at San Francisco","University of Cambridge","University of Florida","University of Oxford","University of Pennsylvania","University of Texas MD Anderson Cancer Center","University of Texas Southwestern Medical Center","University of Texas at Dallas","University of Toronto"],"keyword":["Animals","Breast cancer","Carbon isotopes","Disease Models Animal","Dynamic nuclear polarization","F 18 FDG PET","Humans","Imaging biomarkers","In vivo","LACTATE PRODUCTION","Magnetic Resonance Imaging methods","Neoplasms diagnosis","Prostate cancer","Reproducibility of Results","Translational Medical Research","Tumor metabolism","metabolic response"],"level":"sci","mra":["med","sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"OT":["Unspecified organisation"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"hyperpolarized sup 000000000013 sup c mri path to clinical translation in oncology","src":["au","dtu","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"djr","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.neo.2018.09.006","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["This report was initiated through discussions with representatives of the NCI Cancer Imaging Program and other NIH extramural programs (National Institute of Diabetes and Digestive and Kidney Diseases; National Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute; National Institute of Biomedical Imaging and Bioengineering; and NIH Research Infrastructure Programs). The initial draft was reviewed and edited by over 60 scientists, followed by extensive discussions at a workshop at the ISMRM meeting in May 2017 with more than 52 scientists from academia, industry, and federal agencies from the United States and other countries. The authors appreciate the assistance from NIH extramural program staff Drs. Huiming Zhang, Maren Laughlin, Guoying Liu, Larry Clarke, Paula Jacobs, Janet Eary, and Lalitha Shankar during the development of this report and for organizing the workshop. The authors would also like to thank other contributors, namely, Drs. Michael Boss and Karl Stupic from NIST, Dr. Murali C Krishna from NCI intramural, and Dr. Matthew G. Vander Heiden from MIT."],"id":"S03-087.38-013673","identifier":[{"id":"000454509400001","type":"wos"},{"id":"30472500","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC6260457","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"85056925374","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-0ed92f15-d9a0-4e1e-91fa-0dd1c1fa1d30","dtu-be5c4989-f56f-431f-a37c-ce5001f3c8e6","ku-8e7d643a-c762-43e6-a176-3d81ad5d1c2f"],"keyword":["Animals","BREAST-CANCER","C-13-PYRUVATE MRI","Carbon Isotopes","DYNAMIC NUCLEAR-POLARIZATION","Disease Models, Animal","F-18-FDG PET","Humans","IMAGING BIOMARKERS","IN-VIVO","LACTATE PRODUCTION","METABOLIC-RESPONSE","Magnetic Resonance Imaging/methods","Neoplasms/diagnosis","PROSTATE-CANCER","Reproducibility of Results","TUMOR METABOLISM","Translational Medical Research"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science","Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-6-D5B80ED7FF8BEB31744AB71E9F138AC4"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/238535979/OA_Hyperpolarized_13C_MRI.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.neo.2018.09.006","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/161121867/1_s2.0_S1476558618304056_main.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2019","yearSub":"2019"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"us","key":"university of california","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"University of California","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"us","key":"university of california at san francisco","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"University of California at San Francisco","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, Centers, Center for Hyperpolarization in Magnetic Resonance","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Electrical Engineering","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Electrical Engineering, Center for Magnetic Resonance"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"us","key":"university of texas md anderson cancer center","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"University of Texas MD Anderson Cancer Center","used":1},{"affno":"5","country":"us","key":"md anderson medical center","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"MD Anderson Medical Center","us