td.","status":"p","title":"Climate and Development","volume":"11"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85053518154"],"sdg":["13. Climate Action","15. Life on Land","2. Zero Hunger","All UN SDG Classification"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Private Research","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-PR","DI-PR-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Other Companies","Private Research","Technical University of Denmark","Universities","University of Copenhagen","international woodland company a/s","the international woodland company a/s (biz)"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-2f22d387-71f4-45de-bd23-7445ffebc7c9","ku-e4984236-ac6a-4c41-b8f7-d1814eea0fdc"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Empirically based analysis of households coping with unexpected shocks in the central Himalayas","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-099.75-033629","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"Background/objectives: Individuals with high pre-treatment bacterial Prevotella-to-Bacteroides (P/B) ratio have been reported to lose more body weight on diets high in fiber than subjects with a low P/B ratio. Therefore, the aim of the present study was to examine potential differences in dietary weight loss responses between participants with low and high P/B. Subjects/methods: Eighty overweight participants were randomized (52 completed) to a 500 kcal/d energy deficit diet with a macronutrient composition of 30 energy percentage (E%) fat, 52 E% carbohydrate and 18 E% protein either high (â‰ˆ1500 mg calcium/day) or low ( â‰¤ 600 mg calcium/day) in dairy products for 24 weeks. Body weight, body fat, and dietary intake (by 7-day dietary records) were determined. Individuals were dichotomized according to their pre-treatment P/B ratio derived from 16S rRNA gene sequencing of collected fecal samples to test the potential modification of dietary effects using linear mixed models. Results: Independent of the randomized diets, individuals with high P/B lost 3.8 kg (95%CI, 1.8,5.8; P <0.001) more body weight and 3.8 kg (95% CI, 1.1, 6.5; P = 0.005) more body fat compared to individuals with low P/B. After adjustment for multiple covariates, individuals with high P/B ratio lost 8.3 kg (95% CI, 5.8;10.9, P <0.001) more body weight when consuming above compared to below 30 g fiber/10MJ whereas this weight loss was 3.2 kg (95% CI, 0.8;5.5, P = 0.008) among individuals with low P/B ratio [Mean difference: 5.1 kg (95% CI, 1.7;8.6, P = 0.003)]. Partial correlation coefficients between fiber intake and weight change was 0.90 (P <0.001) among individuals with high P/B ratio and 0.25 (P = 0.29) among individuals with low P/B ratio. Conclusions: Individuals with high P/B lost more body weight and body fat compared to individuals with low P/B, confirming that individuals with a high P/B are more susceptible to weight loss on a diet rich in fiber.","author_count":10,"contributors":["Hjorth, Mads F.","Blædel, Trine","Bendtsen, Line Q.","Lorenzen, Janne K.","Holm, Jacob B.","Kiilerich, Pia","Roager, Henrik Munch","Kristiansen, Karsten","Larsen, Lesli H.","Astrup, Arne"],"cuid":["S02-099.92-037100"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["Asia, East"],"country":["China"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","BGI"],"keyword":["Faculty of Science","Obesity","Randomised controlled trials"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"i prevotella i to i bacteroides i ratio predicts body weight and fat loss success on 000000000024 week diets varying in macronutrient composition and dietary fiber results from a post hoc analysis","src":["dtu","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/248805586/Prevotella_to_Bacteroides_ratio_predicts_body_weight_and_fat_loss_success_on_24_week_diets_varying_in_macronutrient_composition_and_dietary_fiber_results_from_a_post_hoc_analysis_version_of_record_.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["Funding The study was supported by the Danish Council for Strategic Research and the Danish Dairy Research foundation. The present stratified analysis was supported by a grant from Gelesis Inc. Conflict of interest MFH and AA are co-inventers on a pending provisional patent application on the use of biomarkers for prediction of weight loss responses. The work reported in this manuscript was funded by grants from Gelesis Inc. AA is a consultant for Gelesis Inc. concerning scientific advice unrelated to the current paper. The remaining authors declare that they have no conflict of interest."],"id":"S02-099.92-037100","identifier":[{"id":"PMC6331389","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"85047111941","type":"scopus"},{"id":"29777234","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["dtu-c7c2ba21-777d-4806-98c2-1df48a406c15","ku-b251af79-fa1c-4cbc-be59-5c0472bbcc3b"],"keyword":["Faculty of Science","Obesity","Randomised controlled trials"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-F307C8418E63BA1424089572C3B3FE9F"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/248805586/Prevotella_to_Bacteroides_ratio_predicts_body_weight_and_fat_loss_success_on_24_week_diets_varying_in_macronutrient_composition_and_dietary_fiber_results_from_a_post_hoc_analysis_version_of_record_.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"http://europepmc.org/Copyright","type":"rem","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC6331389/pdf/41366_2018_Article_93.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/153135607/Hjorth_et_al._2018_IJO_MEPEB_.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2019","yearSub":"2019"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Nutrition, Exercise and Sports, Obesity Research"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, National Food Institute"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"cn","key":"bgi","loc":["China","Asia, East"],"name":"BGI","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Mads Fiil","key":"hjorthpau","last":"Hjorth","locid":"00918fa0-7b1e-4128-919e-a0af9497026b","name":"Hjorth, Mads Fiil","orcid":"0000-0001-9440-2737","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Trine","key":"blædelpau","last":"Blædel","locid":"8bb74fb7-dc65-4af0-a29c-580eac2a1908","name":"Blædel, Trine","orcid":"0000-0003-3440-4217","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Line Quist","key":"bendtsenpau","last":"Bendtsen","locid":"d59f6a91-604e-4dbc-9692-3df71bb7f765","name":"Bendtsen, Line Quist","orcid":"0000-0002-9541-7989","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Janne Kunchel","key":"lorenzenpau","last":"Lorenzen","locid":"d8755162-ea5d-46be-b486-15f75a00b42b","name":"Lorenzen, Janne Kunchel","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Jacob Bak","key":"holmpau","last":"Holm","locid":"db5f46b1-98f2-448d-95d3-223362b3b7c2","name":"Holm, Jacob Bak","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Pia","key":"kiilerichpau","last":"Kiilerich","locid":"ccf01d8e-0bf9-40ce-9e2b-039ea6f19e18","name":"Kiilerich, Pia","orcid":"0000-0001-5665-6074","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Henrik Munch","key":"roagerpau","last":"Roager","locid":"432a59ac-f546-4d50-8131-0e0eef9c4ffb","name":"Roager, Henrik Munch","orcid":"0000-0002-2504-8313","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 3","first":"Karsten","key":"kristiansenpau","last":"Kristiansen","locid":"941041b1-1b8f-4be5-9a65-5e98f662c07e","name":"Kristiansen, Karsten","orcid":"0000-0002-6024-0917","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Lesli Hingstrup","key":"larsenpau","last":"Larsen","locid":"cf1fc793-3fd4-4dca-ab8e-ee3ed914ec04","name":"Larsen, Lesli Hingstrup","orcid":"0000-0001-8827-7992","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Arne","key":"astruppau","last":"Astrup","locid":"5d943631-c9fc-4b63-8aaa-263ba4248735","name":"Astrup, Arne","orcid":"0000-0001-8968-8996","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["29777234"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1038/s41366-018-0093-2","issn":["03070565","14765497"],"issn_org":["03070565"],"journal":"International Journal of Obesity","pages":"149-157","publisher":"Springer Nature","status":"p","title":"International Journal of Obesity","volume":"43"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85047111941"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Technical University of Denmark","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-c7c2ba21-777d-4806-98c2-1df48a406c15","ku-b251af79-fa1c-4cbc-be59-5c0472bbcc3b"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Prevotella-to-Bacteroides ratio predicts body weight and fat loss success on 24-week diets varying in macronutrient composition and dietary fiber: results from a post-hoc analysis","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-099.92-037100","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"Objective We aimed to investigate the association of night work during pregnancy with the risk of severe postpartum depression (PPD). Methods We performed a nationwide register-based cohort study of workers in all Danish public hospitals. Daily information on working hours was retrieved from the Danish Working Hour Database from January 2007 to December 2015. Pregnancies, covariates and outcome were identified from national registries for births and hospital contacts. We performed logistic regression of the risk of severe PPD in relation to the number and duration of night shifts, spells of consecutive night shifts, and short shift intervals during the first 32 pregnancy weeks. Analyses were adjusted for age, body mass index, socioeconomic status, parity, sickness absence three months prior to pregnancy, and prior diagnosis of severe depression. Results The study cohort comprised 25 009 singleton pregnancies from 19 382 workers. The majority were nurses or physicians. Overall, we did not observe an increased risk of PPD for any of the dimensions of night work analyzed. We found, however, an increased risk of PPD (adjusted odds ratio 2.08, 95% confidence interval 1.09-4.00) among women who stopped working night shifts after the first pregnancy trimester (N=3094). Conclusion Overall, our results do not support night work during pregnancy as a risk factor for severe PPD among hospital employees. However, we observed a 2-fold increased risk of PPD among women who stopped working night shifts after the first pregnancy trimester. This may reflect the influence of the healthy worker survivor effect and warrants further attention.","author_count":17,"contributors":["Hammer, Paula","Hageman, Ida","Garde, Anne","Begtrup, Luise","Flachs, Esben","Hansen, Johnni","Hansen, Ase","Hougaard, Karin","Kolstad, Henrik A.","Larsen, Ann","Pinborg, Anja","Specht, Ina","Bonde, Jens Peter"],"cuid":["S03-093.15-767352"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":3,"corresponding_author":["Hammer, Paula"],"keyword":["Cohort study","DAD","Depression","Depressive Disorder","Disorders","Health","Healthy Worker Effect","Inference","Night work","Nurse","Nurses","Postpartum depression","Pregnancy","Register based cohort study","Risk","Shift work","Shift worker","Sleep","Symptoms","doctor"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-HO","DI-NP","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"GV":["DI-GV-ALL","DI-GV-WORK"],"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"NP":["DI-NP-ALL","DI-NP-CAN"],"OT":["Department of Occupational Medicine, Aarhus University Hospital, Aarhus, Denmark."],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-GV-WORK","DI-HO-CAP","DI-NP-CAN","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"night work and postpartum depression a national register based cohort study","src":["au","ku","nfa"],"src-code":["DI-GV-WORK","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.5271/sjweh.3831","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S03-093.15-767352","identifier":[{"id":"000492848100005","type":"wos"},{"id":"85074745213","type":"scopus"},{"id":"31125110","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["au-33849777-47c4-4217-be59-c31f175a7473","ku-ccd425e6-2327-4210-8e9a-ef1f44279f5f","nfa-46a0eef1-4fa5-4f85-871c-ffe3d97724d2"],"keyword":["DAD","DISORDERS","HEALTH","INFERENCE","NURSES","PREGNANCY","PSYCHIATRIC EPISODES","RISK","SHIFT WORK","SLEEP","SYMPTOMS","cohort study","depression","depressive disorder","doctor","healthy worker effect","night work","nurse","postpartum depression","register-based cohort study","shift worker"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-6-2E968FD8937B1128938ACF44A2D2168B"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.au.dk/ws/files/210233815/Night_work_a