 5","corresponding":null,"first":"Gunita","key":"deksnepau","last":"Deksne","name":"Deksne, Gunita","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"6","corresponding":null,"first":"Heidi Larsen","key":"enemarkpau","last":"Enemark","name":"Enemark, Heidi Larsen","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"7","corresponding":null,"first":"Lis","key":"albanpau","last":"Alban","name":"Alban, Lis","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Maria Vang","key":"johansenpau","last":"Johansen","locid":"4fd2a7ba-3f5a-47a5-bfec-56f4b92848c2","name":"Johansen, Maria Vang","orcid":"0000-0001-7946-1712","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"8","corresponding":null,"first":"Henrik Vedel","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","name":"Nielsen, Henrik Vedel","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"7","corresponding":null,"first":"Marianne","key":"sandbergpau","last":"Sandberg","name":"Sandberg, Marianne","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"9 10","corresponding":null,"first":"Anna","key":"lundénpau","last":"Lundén","name":"Lundén, Anna","rank":11,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"8","corresponding":null,"first":"Christen Rune","key":"stensvoldpau","last":"Stensvold","name":"Stensvold, Christen Rune","rank":12,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"11","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Sara","key":"monteiropirespau","last":"Monteiro Pires","locid":"eaae5296-a349-424e-aeaa-851a9e4a2510","name":"Pires, Sara Monteiro","name_org":"Monteiro Pires, Sara","orcid":"0000-0002-7751-1509","rank":13,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"12 2 8","corresponding":null,"first":"Pikka","key":"jokelainenpau","last":"Jokelainen","name":"Jokelainen, Pikka","rank":14,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["30906889"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.parepi.2019.e00100","issn":["24056731"],"journal":"Parasite Epidemiology and Control","paperid":"e00100","publisher":"Elsevier","status":"p","title":"Parasite Epidemiology and Control","volume":"5"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85062647036"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Governmental Institutions","All Miscellaneous","All Private Research","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-GV-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-PR","DI-PR-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Danish Agriculture & Food Council","Governmental Institutions","Miscellaneous","Other Research","Private Research","Technical University of Denmark","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-9affdaaf-0656-44b6-b8cc-d9cf0fe74c1f","ku-a759f832-a5a0-4dfe-aff5-e21d0877ef57"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Seroprevalence of Toxoplasma gondii in domestic pigs, sheep, cattle, wild boars, and moose in the Nordic-Baltic region: A systematic review and meta-analysis","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-093.39-036119","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"Background: The combination of organized cervical cancer screening and childhood HPV vaccination programs has the potential to eliminate cervical cancer in the future. However, only women participating in both programs gain the full protection, and combined non-attenders remain at high risk of developing cervical cancer. Our aim was to analyze the association between non-adherence to HPV vaccination and non-participation in cervical cancer screening for the total population and stratified by native background and parental education. Participants: Women born in 1993 eligible for both childhood HPV vaccination and first cervical cancer screening. Analysis: Logistic regression models were used to estimate the odds ratio (OR) of non-participation in cervical cancer screening with 95% confidence intervals (CI). Stratified and adjusted logistic regression models were used along with the Wald test in order to test for interaction. Results: 24,828 women were included in the study. Among vaccinated women, 61.4% participated in cervical cancer screening; only 39.0% of unvaccinated women participated in cervical cancer screening. Unvaccinated and unscreened women were often non-native and had the lowest socio-economic status, whereas vaccinated and screened women were often native and had the highest socio-economic status. The adjusted OR for non-participation in cervical cancer screening was 2.07 [95% CI: 1.88–2.28] for unvaccinated compared to vaccinated women. After stratifying by country of origin, unvaccinated natives had the highest adjusted OR of not participating in cervical cancer screening compared to non-native women from both western and non-western countries (adjusted ORs of 2.2 [95% CI: 2.0– 2.4], 1.3 [95% CI: 0.6–2.8], and 1.5 [95% CI: 1.1–2.0], respectively) (Wald test p=0.019). Conclusion: Among natives, non-adherence to HPV vaccination and non-participation in screening seem to be signs of generally poor health-preventive behavior, whereas among non-natives from non-western countries, non-attendance in HPV vaccination and cervical cancer screening seem to be influenced by unrelated factors. Therefore, a differentiated and culturally sensitive approach is needed to enhance overall cervical cancer preventive behavior across different nativities.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Koed Badre-Esfahani, Sara","Larsen, Mette Bach","Seibæk, Lene","Petersen, Lone Kjeld","Blaakær, Jan","Støvring, Henrik","Andersen, Berit"],"cuid":["S02-097.95-015220"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"corresponding_author":["Badre-Esfahani, Sara"],"keyword":["Human papilloma virus","Nationality","Non participation","Screening","Socio economic status","Vaccination"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CDR"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CDR","DI-U-AU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"non adherence to childhood hpv vaccination is associated with non participation in cervical cancer screening a nationwide danish register based cohort study","src":["au","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.2147/CLEP.S203023","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["According to Danish legislation and the Central Denmark Region Committees on Biomedical Research Ethics, the study did not require ethical approval because it was based on register data. The same institutions waived patient consent for use of register data. In accordance with Danish law and the EU’s General Data Protection Regulation, the project was listed at the Central Denmark Region internal list of research projects (J. No.: 1-16-02-400-16). Andersen B has received HPV test kits and HPV self-sampling devices from Roche and Axlab for other studies. Andersen B reports grants from Helsefonden, grants from Familien Hede Nielsens fond, during the conduct of the study; non-financial support from Roche, non-financial support from Axlab, outside the submitted work. Pedersen LK has received speaker’s fee from Merck and Sanofi Pasteur in relation to lectures on HPV vaccines. The authors report no other conflicts of interest in this work."],"id":"S02-097.95-015220","identifier":[{"id":"31814770","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC6853196","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"85075185674","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-23ff640a-3ec1-47a1-aad2-256f14832f39","sdu-206071a8-2e5f-4473-8eae-f7f9debeb520"],"keyword":["Human papilloma virus","Nationality","Non-participation","Screening","Socio-economic status","Vaccination"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-93588975FD6E36876CD556AA17287170"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.2147/CLEP.S203023","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/157063549/Non_Adherence_To_Childhood_HPV_Vaccination_Is_Associated_With_Non_Participation_In_Cervical_Cancer_Screening.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2019","yearSub":"2019"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - Department of Obstetrics and Gynaecology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"central denmark region","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CDR","l3nl":"Central Denmark Region","l3nn":"Central Denmark Region","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Central Denmark Region","namevar":["Aarhus University Hospital"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CDR","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Clinical Research, KI, OUH, Research unit of Gynaecology and Obstetrics"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Sara","key":"koedbadreesfahanipau","last":"Koed Badre-Esfahani","locid":"3712eb12-c4e8-4af5-972e-4ead8c43b9c3","name":"Koed Badre-Esfahani, Sara","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Mette Bach","key":"larsenpau","last":"Larsen","locid":"40b0a541-1654-4488-a108-499a4097e34a","name":"Larsen, Mette Bach","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Lene","key":"seibækpau","last":"Seibæk","locid":"2bf95667-24b9-444b-b3e1-a62965aac67a","name":"Seibæk, Lene","orcid":"0000-0002-2396-8669","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Lone Kjeld","key":"petersenpau","last":"Petersen","locid":"000a791a-6685-4ef0-b286-f342dd3ad555","name":"Petersen, Lone Kjeld","orcid":"0000-0002-1424-6170","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Jan","key":"blaakærpau","last":"Blaakær","locid":"1a4149d9-2f14-46a8-882f-702c626338f5","name":"Blaakær, Jan","orcid":"0000-0003-3726-0589","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Henrik","key":"støvringpau","last":"Støvring","locid":"c19fb430-dcd1-4a13-995f-4247f8ebc11d","name":"Støvring, Henrik","orcid":"0000-0002-5821-2351","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Berit","key":"andersenpau","last":"Andersen","locid":"4f64dc11-e5ca-4037-bbad-8ca533d841e4","name":"Andersen, Berit","orcid":"0000-0003-4074-6504","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["31814770"],"pub":{"doi":"10.2147/clep.s203023","issn":["11791349"],"journal":"Clinical Epidemiology","pages":"969-980","publisher":"Dove Medical Press Ltd","status":"p","title":"Clinical Epidemiology","volume":"11"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85075185674"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","Central Denmark Region","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-23ff640a-3ec1-47a1-aad2-256f14832f39","sdu-206071a8-2e5f-4473-8eae-f7f9debeb520"],"src":["AU Aarhus University","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Non-Adherence To Childhood HPV Vaccination Is Associated With Non-Participation In Cervical Cancer Screening – A Nationwide Danish Register-Based Cohort Study","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-097.95-015220","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"AIMS/HYPOTHESIS: Dietary recommendations for treating type 2 diabetes are unclear but a trend towards recommending a diet reduced in carbohydrate content is acknowledged. We compared a carbohydrate-reduced high-protein (CRHP) diet with an iso-energetic conventional diabetes (CD) diet to elucidate the effects on glycaemic control and selected cardiovascular risk markers during 6 weeks of full food provision of each diet. METHODS: The primary outcome of the study was change in HbA1c. Secondary outcomes reported in the present paper include glycaemic variables, ectopic fat content and 24 h blood pressure. Eligibility criteria were: men and women with type 2 diabetes, HbA1c 48-97 mmol/mol (6.5-11%), age >18 years, haemoglobin >6/>7 mmol/l (women/men) and eGFR >30 ml min-1 (1.73 m)-2. Participants were randomised by drawing blinded ballots to 6 + 6 weeks of an iso-energetic CRHP vs CD diet in an open label, crossover design aiming at body weight stability. The CRHP/CD diets contained carbohydrate 30/50 energy per cent (E%), protein 30/17E% and fat 40/33E%, respectively. Participants underwent a meal test at the end of each diet period and glycaemic variables, lipid profiles, 24 h blood pressure and ectopic fat including liver and pancreatic fat content were assessed at baseline and at the end of each diet period. Data were collected at Copenhagen University Hospital, Bispebjerg and Copenhagen University Hospital, Herlev. RESULTS: Twenty-eight participants completed the study. Fourteen participants carried out 6 weeks of the CRHP intervention followed by 6 weeks of the CD intervention, and 14 participants received the dietary interventions in the reverse order. Compared with a CD diet, a CRHP diet reduced the primary outcome of HbA1c (mean ± SEM: -6.2 ± 0.8 mmol/mol (-0.6 ± 0.1%) vs -0.75 ± 1.0 mmol/mol (-0.1 ± 0.1%); p < 0.001). Nine (out of 37) pre-specified secondary outcomes are reported in the present paper, of which five were significantly different between the diets, (p < 0.05); compared with a CD diet, a CRHP diet reduced the secondary outcomes (mean ± SEM or medians [interquartile range]) of fasting plasma glucose (-0.71 ± 0.20 mmol/l vs 0.03 ± 0.23 mmol/l; p < 0.05), postprandial plasma glucose AUC (9.58 ± 0.29 mmol/l × 240 min vs 11.89 ± 0.43 mmol/l × 240 min; p < 0.001) and net AUC (1.25 ± 0.20 mmol/l × 240 min vs 3.10 ± 0.25 mmol/l × 240 min; p < 0.001), hepatic fat content (-2.4% [-7.8% to -1.0%] vs 0.2% [-2.3% to 0.9%]; p < 0.01) and pancreatic fat content (-1.7% [-3.5% to 0.6%] vs 0.5% [-1.0% to 2.0%]; p < 0.05). Changes in other secondary outcomes, i.e. 24 h blood pressure and muscle-, visceral- or subcutaneous adipose tissue, did not differ between diets. CONCLUSIONS/INTERPRETATION: A moderate macronutrient shift by substituting carbohydrates with protein and fat for 6 weeks reduced HbA1c and hepatic fat content in weight stable individuals with type 2 diabetes. TRIAL REGISTRATION: ClinicalTrials.gov NCT02764021. FUNDING: The study was funded by grants from Arla Food for Health; the Novo Nordisk Foundation Center for Basic Metabolic Research, University of Copenhagen; the Department of Clinical Medicine, Aarhus University; the Department of Nutrition, Exercise and Sports, University of Copenhagen; and Copenhagen University Hospital, Bispebjerg.","author_count":15,"contributors":["Skytte, Mads J","Samkani, Amirsalar","Petersen, Amy D","Thomsen, Mads N","Astrup, Arne","Chabanova, Elizaveta","Frystyk, Jan","Holst, Jens J","Thomsen, Henrik S","Madsbad, Sten","Larsen, Thomas M","Haugaard, Steen B","Krarup, Thure"],"cuid":["S02-099.16-020561"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"keyword":["Carbohydrate restriction","Cardiovascular disease","Dietary intervention","Ectopic fat deposition","Faculty of Science","Glycaemic control","Metabolism","Non alcoholic fatty liver disease","Nutritional therapy","Type 2 diabetes mellitu