{"abstract":"Significant increase of the signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) is possible by cooling receive coils to cryogenic temperatures, if they are not highly sample noise dominated. Conventionally, the noise of the preamplifier is excluded leading to an overestimation of the achievable SNR gain. In this work, we show that for the case of a small-animal birdcage coil for 13C at 3T cooled with liquid nitrogen to 77K, the SNR is overestimated by approximately 40% if the effect of the room temperature preamplifier is excluded. Hence, the preamplifier should either be included in the SNR gain estimation or cooled with the coil.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Johansen, Daniel Højrup","Sanchez, Juan Diego","Zhurbenko, Vitaliy","Ardenkjær-Larsen, Jan Henrik"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-318588"],"event":{"end":"2019-05-16","place":"Montréal, ca","start":"2019-05-11","title":"ISMRM 27th Annual Meeting &amp; Exhibition","year":"2019"},"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"on the snr of cryogenic receive coils when using room temperature preamplifiers","src":["dtu"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/181826972/ISMRM2019_1508.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-318588","ids":["dtu-2f39dc2d-3b99-4006-a634-e47ab31b9681"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-2FC1A8FD7A44D06CE896E6AFF5E4AC7E"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/181826972/ISMRM2019_1508.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, Centers, Center for Hyperpolarization in Magnetic Resonance","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Electrical Engineering","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Electrical Engineering, Electromagnetic Systems"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Daniel Højrup","key":"johansenpau","last":"Johansen","locid":"114285a3-128f-493e-b93a-e30c40bb6e96","name":"Johansen, Daniel Højrup","orcid":"0000-0002-8302-7182","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Juan Diego","key":"sanchezpau","last":"Sanchez","locid":"2d24121b-877e-4bfe-a434-9c7c3e7a34e0","name":"Sanchez, Juan Diego","orcid":"0000-0002-8201-4136","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Vitaliy","key":"zhurbenkopau","last":"Zhurbenko","locid":"8449f428-fe8f-4ade-ad94-2e052869c1f4","name":"Zhurbenko, Vitaliy","orcid":"0000-0002-1515-733X","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jan Henrik","key":"ardenkjærlarsenpau","last":"Ardenkjær-Larsen","locid":"ae9519c9-1b64-4426-b89f-4c36d08c1836","name":"Ardenkjær-Larsen, Jan Henrik","orcid":"0000-0001-6167-6926","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Technical University of Denmark","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-2f39dc2d-3b99-4006-a634-e47ab31b9681"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark"],"title":"On the SNR of Cryogenic Receive Coils when using Room Temperature Preamplifiers","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-318588","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"Spring migrating sea trout juveniles can be classified as parr, pre-smolt or smolt based on body morphology and osmoregulatory capacity. In this respect, parr are assumed to be less prepared for a marine life and to have lower survival at sea than pre-smolts and smolts. However, the behaviour and survival of these trout phenotypes upon entering the sea is not well known. Using passive integrated transponder telemetry, this study found that the return rate from the sea to the natal river was higher for parr compared to pre-smolts and smolts. Additionally, trout classified as parr generally migrated earlier to the sea and a larger proportion returned to the river after less than one year at sea. The daily mortality rate at sea was comparable among the different phenotypes of trout, suggesting that the higher proportion of returning parr to the river was linked to their shorter duration at sea. These results provide evidence of different life-history strategies for seaward-migrating juvenile sea trout, ultimately affecting their return rate to the natal river. Investigations failing to consider downstream migrating parr and pre-smolts risks neglecting a large part of the anadromous population and may result in inaccurate assessments of sea trout stocks in rivers.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Del Villar-Guerra, Diego","Larsen, Martin H","Baktoft, Henrik","Koed, Anders","Aarestrup, Kim"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-327487"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Baktoft, Henrik"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Loughs Agency"],"keyword":["Animal Migration","Behavioural ecology"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-UND"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-UND"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the influence of initial developmental status on the life history of sea trout salmo trutta","src":["dtu"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-019-49175-0","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-327487","identifier":[{"id":"31530837","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC6748944","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"85072289489","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["dtu-4cff9702-0ed6-456e-b4e7-e3e8cd3edfbf"],"keyword":["Animal migration","Behavioural ecology"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-E7BFD4296404F68F7D0E43ED887A5E0C"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/193281276/s41598_019_49175_0.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-019-49175-0","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2019","yearSub":"2019"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"gb","key":"loughs agency","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"Loughs Agency","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"unclear universities","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-UND","l3nl":"Undetermined","l3nn":"Unclear Universities","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Unclear Universities","namevar":["Danish Center for Wild Salmon"],"nora_code":"DI-U-UND","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, National Institute of Aquatic Resources","Technical University of Denmark, National Institute of Aquatic Resources, Section for Freshwater Fisheries Ecology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Diego","key":"delvillarguerrapau","last":"Del Villar-Guerra","name":"Del Villar-Guerra, Diego","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Martin H","key":"larsenpau","last":"Larsen","name":"Larsen, Martin H","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Henrik","key":"baktoftpau","last":"Baktoft","locid":"642a7cea-4ecc-4866-97f3-0d86f3e47ec0","name":"Baktoft, Henrik","orcid":"0000-0002-3644-4960","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Anders","key":"koedpau","last":"Koed","locid":"70851536-6d9b-439b-b100-58a92830a28f","name":"Koed, Anders","orcid":"0000-0002-2163-0955","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Kim","key":"aarestruppau","last":"Aarestrup","locid":"60987b6f-7027-4bd5-91bc-d2f10390aa63","name":"Aarestrup, Kim","orcid":"0000-0001-8521-6270","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["31530837"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1038/s41598-019-49175-0","issn":["20452322"],"journal":"Scientific Reports","paperid":"13468","publisher":"Nature Publishing Group","status":"p","title":"Scientific Reports","volume":"9"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85072289489"],"sdg":["14. Life Below Water","All UN SDG Classification"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Technical University of Denmark","Undetermined","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-4cff9702-0ed6-456e-b4e7-e3e8cd3edfbf"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark"],"title":"The influence of initial developmental status on the life-history of sea trout (Salmo trutta)","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-327487","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"Metabolic reprogramming is one ofthe defning features of cancer and abnormal metabolism is associated with many other pathologies. Molecular imaging techniques capable of detecting such changes have become essential for cancer diagnosis,treatment planning, and surveillance. In particular, 18F-FDG (fuorodeoxyglucose) PET has emerged as an essential imaging modality for cancer because of its unique ability to detect a disturbed molecular pathway through measurements of glucose uptake. However, FDG-PET has limitations that restrict its usefulness in certain situations and the information gained is limited to glucose uptake only.13C magnetic resonance spectroscopy theoretically has certain advantages over FDG-PET, but its inherent low sensitivity has restricted its use mostly to single voxel measurements unless dissolution dynamic nuclear polarization (dDNP) is used to increase the signal, which brings additional complications for clinical use.We show here a new method of imaging glucose metabolism in vivo by MRI chemical shiftimaging (CSI) experiments thatrelies on a simple, butrobust and efcient, post-processing procedure by the higher dimensional analog of singular value decomposition,tensor decomposition. Using this procedure, we achieve an order of magnitude increase in signalto noise in both dDNP and non-hyperpolarized non-localized experiments without sacrifcing accuracy. In CSI experiments an approximately 30-fold increase was observed, enough thatthe glucose to lactate conversion indicative oftheWarburg efect can be imaged without hyper-polarization with a time resolution of 12s and an overall spatial resolution that compares favorably to 18F-FDG PET.","author_count":15,"contributors":["Brender, Jeffrey R.","Kishimoto, Shun","Merkle, Hellmut","Reed, Galen","Hurd, Ralph E.","Chen, Albert P.","Ardenkjær-Larsen, Jan Henrik","Munasinghe, Jeeva","Saito, Keita","Seki, Tomohiro","Oshima, Nobu","Yamamoto, Kazutoshi","Choyke, Peter L.","Mitchell, James","Krishna, Murali C."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-355331"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Krishna, Murali C."],"country":["United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","GE Healthcare","National Institutes of Health"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"dynamic imaging of glucose and lactate metabolism by c 000000000013 mrs without hyperpolarization","src":["dtu"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-019-38981-1","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["Mouse?Models.? The animal experiments were conducted according to a protocol approved by the Animal Research Advisory Committee of the NIH (RBB-159-2SA) in accordance with the National Institutes of Health Guidelines for Animal Research. Female athymic nude mice weighing approximately 26 g were supplied by the Frederick Cancer Research Center, Animal Production (Frederick, MD) and housed with ad libitum access to NIH Rodent Diet #31 Open Formula (Envigo) and water on a 12-hour light/dark cycle. Xenografts were generated by the subcutaneous injection of 3 × 106 MiaPaCa-2 (America Type Cell Collection (ATCC), Manassas, VA, USA) or Hs766t (Threshold Pharmaceuticals, Redwood City, CA, USA) pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma cells. Both cell lines were tested in May 2013 and authenticated by IDEXX RADIL (Columbia, MO) using a panel of microsatellite markers"],"id":"S01-000.00-355331","identifier":[{"id":"PMC6399318","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"85062420938","type":"scopus"},{"id":"30833588","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["dtu-a9e56a55-5ee3-4ead-9df3-21d1cf28493e"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-AF3B710A3ABA4877275DB60CFB345B21"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/171223942/s41598_019_38981_1.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-019-38981-1","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2019","yearSub":"2019"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"us","key":"national institutes of health","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"National Institutes of Health","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"us","key":"ge healthcare","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"GE Healthcare","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, Centers, Center for Hyperpolarization in Magnetic Resonance","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Electrical Engineering","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Electrical Engineering, Center for Magnetic Resonance"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jeffrey R.","key":"brenderpau","last":"Brender","name":"Brender, Jeffrey R.","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Shun","key":"kishimotopau","last":"Kishimoto","name":"Kishimoto, Shun","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Hellmut","key":"merklepau","last":"Merkle","name":"Merkle, Hellmut","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Galen","key":"reedpau","last":"Reed","name":"Reed, Galen","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Ralph E.","key":"hurdpau","last":"Hurd","name":"Hurd, Ralph E.","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Albert P.","key":"chenpau","last":"Chen","name":"Chen, Albert P.","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Jan Henrik","key":"ardenkjærlarsenpau","last":"Ardenkjær-Larsen","locid":"ae9519c9-1b64-4426-b89f-4c36d08c1836","name":"Ardenkjær-Larsen, Jan Henrik","orcid":"0000-0001-6167-6926","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jeeva","key":"munasinghepau","last":"Munasinghe","name":"Munasinghe, Jeeva","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Keita","key":"saitopau","last":"Saito","name":"Saito, Keita","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Tomohiro","key":"sekipau","last":"Seki","name":"Seki, Tomohiro","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Nobu","key":"oshimapau","last":"Oshima","name":"Oshima, Nobu","rank":11,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Kazutoshi","key":"yamamotopau","last":"Yamamoto","name":"Yamamoto, Kazutoshi","rank":12,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Peter L.","key":"choykepau","last":"Choyke","name":"Choyke, Peter L.","rank":13,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"James","key":"mitchellpau","last":"Mitchell","name":"Mitchell, James","rank":14,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Murali C.","key":"krishnapau","last":"Krishna","name":"Krishna, Murali C.","rank":15,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["30833588"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1038/s41598-019-38981-1","issn":["20452322"],"issue":"1","journal":"Scientific Reports","paperid":"3410","publisher":"Nature Publishing Group","status":"p","title":"Scientific Reports","volume":"9"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85062420938"],"sdg":["3. Good Health and Well-being","All UN SDG Classification"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Technical University of Denmark","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-a9e56a55-5ee3-4ead-9df3-21d1cf28493e"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark"],"title":"Dynamic Imaging of Glucose and Lactate Metabolism by C-13-MRS without Hyperpolarization","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-355331","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"author_count":4,"contributors":["Jacobsen, Brian Arly"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-422434"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Det Humanistiske Fakultet","Kommunalpolitik","Religionspolitik"],"level":"sci","mra":["hum"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"den usynlige religionspolitik","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dba","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://unipress.dk/udgivelser/r/religion-i-det-offentlige-rum/","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-422434","ids":["ku-30a76076-44bc-4637-8b53-1742a239edc9"],"keyword":["Det Humanistiske Fakultet","kommunalpolitik","religionspolitik"],"lang":"Danish","level":"Scientific","mra":["Humanities"],"noi":["Local-1-1-8848BA48368EE5CB999A68B57A55CE0C"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Humanities, Department of Cross-Cultural and Regional Studies"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Brian Arly","key":"jacobsenpau","last":"Jacobsen","locid":"ad035c25-6285-4df3-aa55-2bca49b9e924","name":"Jacobsen, Brian Arly","orcid":"0000-0001-8449-8202","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Henrik Reintoft","key":"christensenpedh","last":"Christensen","name":"Christensen, Henrik Reintoft","rank":2,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Brian Arly","key":"jacobsenpedh","last":"Jacobsen","name":"Jacobsen, Brian Arly","rank":3,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Signe Alma Engelbreth","key":"larsenpedh","last":"Larsen","name":"Larsen, Signe Alma Engelbreth","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"isbn":["9788771840834"],"issn":["19047762"],"issue":"6","pages":"211-237","place":"Aarhus","publisher":"Aarhus Universitetsforlag","series":"Sociologiske studier","status":"p","title":"Religion i det offentlige rum – et dansk perspektiv"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-30a76076-44bc-4637-8b53-1742a239edc9"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Den usynlige religionspolitik","type":"Book Chapter","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-422434","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"Protein O-mannosylation (O-Man), originally discovered in yeast five decades ago, is an important post-translational modification (PTM) conserved from bacteria to humans, but not found in plants or nematodes. Until recently, the homologous family of ER-located protein O-mannosyl transferases (PMT1-7 in yeast; POMT1/POMT2 in humans), were the only known enzymes involved in directing O-Man biosynthesis in eukaryotes. However, recent studies demonstrate the existence of multiple distinct O-Man glycosylation pathways indicating that the genetic and biosynthetic regulation of O-Man in eukaryotes is more complex than previously envisioned. Introduction of sensitive glycoproteomics strategies provided an expansion of O-Man glycoproteomes in eukaryotes (yeast and mammalian cell lines) leading to the discovery of O-Man glycosylation on important mammalian cell adhesion (cadherin superfamily) and signaling (plexin family) macromolecules, and to the discovery of unique nucleocytoplasmic O-Man glycosylation in yeast. It is now evident that eukaryotes have multiple distinct O-Man glycosylation pathways including: i) the classical PMT1-7 and POMT1/POMT2 pathway conserved in all eukaryotes apart from plants; ii) a yet uncharacterized nucleocytoplasmic pathway only found in yeast; iii) an ER-located pathway directed by the TMTC1-4 genes found in metazoans and protists and primarily dedicated to the cadherin superfamily; and iv) a yet uncharacterized pathway found in metazoans primarily dedicated to plexins. O-Man glycosylation is thus emerging as a much more widespread and evolutionary diverse PTM with complex genetic and biosynthetic regulation. While deficiencies in the POMT1/POMT2 O-Man pathway underlie muscular dystrophies, the TMTC1-4 pathway appear to be involved in distinct congenital disorders with neurodevelopmental phenotypes. Here, we review and discuss the recent discoveries of the new non-classical O-Man glycosylation pathways, their substrates, functions and roles in disease.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Larsen, Ida Signe Bohse","Narimatsu, Yoshiki","Clausen, Henrik","Joshi, Hiren J.","Halim, Adnan"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-464756"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"multiple distinct o mannosylation pathways in eukaryotes","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"djr","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/235405036/Accepted_manuscript_Larsen_et_al.pdf","version":"Pre-print"},"id":"S01-000.00-464756","identifier":[{"id":"30999272","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85064198693","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-7731f7b1-242b-42ae-bef6-4c4746eadc9b"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-A094C3B63EE95BA7CB5745EE7F6BE659"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/235405036/Accepted_manuscript_Larsen_et_al.pdf","version":"Pre-print"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2019","yearSub":"2019"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Cellular and Molecular Medicine, Glycomics Program"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Ida Signe Bohse","key":"larsenpau","last":"Larsen","locid":"f2869bf8-54a4-49ae-9db2-434d2714b362","name":"Larsen, Ida Signe Bohse","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Yoshiki","key":"narimatsupau","last":"Narimatsu","locid":"d9d8a2cb-0c61-43eb-9b4c-4b4248e71f06","name":"Narimatsu, Yoshiki","orcid":"0000-0003-1428-5695","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Henrik","key":"clausenpau","last":"Clausen","locid":"a7882c09-1275-4857-8b5b-7e3efc1d088d","name":"Clausen, Henrik","orcid":"0000-0002-0915-5055","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Hiren J","key":"joshipau","last":"Joshi","locid":"59e7c6d1-b66a-49df-a6f5-4dd110dd7e03","name":"Joshi, Hiren J.","name_org":"Joshi, Hiren J","orcid":"0000-0002-8192-2829","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Adnan","key":"halimpau","last":"Halim","locid":"65f08640-6203-4f49-ad7e-f95cb9c070b8","name":"Halim, Adnan","orcid":"0000-0003-1629-187X","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["30999272"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.sbi.2019.03.003","issn":["0959440X","1879033X"],"issn_org":["0959440X"],"journal":"Current Opinion in Structural Biology","pages":"171-178","status":"p","title":"Current Opinion in Structural Biology","volume":"56"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85064198693"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-7731f7b1-242b-42ae-bef6-4c4746eadc9b"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Multiple distinct O-Mannosylation pathways in eukaryotes","type":"Journal Review","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-464756","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"Aberrant display of the truncated core1 O-glycan T-antigen is a common feature of human cancer cells that correlates with metastasis. Here we show that T-antigen in Drosophila melanogaster macrophages is involved in their developmentally programmed tissue invasion. Higher macrophage T-antigen levels require an atypical major facilitator superfamily (MFS) member that we named Minerva which enables macrophage dissemination and invasion. We characterize for the first time the T and Tn glycoform O-glycoproteome of the Drosophila melanogaster embryo, and determine that Minerva increases the presence of T-antigen on proteins in pathways previously linked to cancer, most strongly on the sulfhydryl oxidase Qsox1 which we show is required for macrophage tissue entry. Minerva's vertebrate ortholog, MFSD1, rescues the minerva mutant's migration and T-antigen glycosylation defects. We thus identify a key conserved regulator that orchestrates O-glycosylation on a protein subset to activate a program governing migration steps important for both development and cancer metastasis.","author_count":13,"contributors":["Valoskova, Katarina","Biebl, Julia","Roblek, Marko","Emtenani, Shamsi","Gyoergy, Attila","Misova, Michaela","Ratheesh, Aparna","Reis-Rodrigues, Patricia","Shkarina, Kateryna","Larsen, Ida Signe Bohse","Vakhrushev, Sergey Y.","Clausen, Henrik","Siekhaus, Daria E."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-512173"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["Austria","United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Institute of Science and Technology Austria","University of Warwick"],"keyword":["BMP","Cancer biology","D melanogaster","Developmental biology","Dissemination","ECM","Extracellular matrix","Invasion","Macrophage","Metastasis","NOTCH","O glycosylation","Protein Folding","T antigen","Tn antigen","Transporter","major facilitator superfamily"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"a conserved major facilitator superfamily member orchestrates a subset of o glycosylation to aid macrophage tissue invasion","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.7554/eLife.41801","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-512173","identifier":[{"id":"PMC6435326","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"85063713056","type":"scopus"},{"id":"30910009","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-c6f0b9b3-8a7f-4159-8814-d2cc75b8bf52"],"keyword":["BMP","D. melanogaster","ECM","Extracellular Matrix","MFSD1","Notch","O-glycosylation","Qsox1","T antigen","Tn antigen","cancer biology","developmental biology","dissemination","invasion","macrophage","major facilitator superfamily","metastasis","protein folding","transporter"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-01556DDD1104867B6CD6729CF58B0F6C"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.7554/eLife.41801","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/216865845/elife_41801_v1.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2019","yearSub":"2019"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"at","key":"institute of science and technology austria","loc":["Austria","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Institute of Science and Technology Austria","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gb","key":"university of warwick","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Warwick","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU Universit