13","publisher":"Nordic Association of Occupational Safety and Health","status":"p","title":"Scandinavian Journal of Work, Environment & Health","title_org":"Scandinavian Journal of Work, Environment and Health","volume":"44"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85049454292"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Governmental Institutions","All Hospitals","All Non-Profit Organisations","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","Central Denmark Region","DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-GV-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-NP","DI-NP-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Governmental Institutions","Hospitals","Non-Profit Organisations","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-bc5c8203-733d-4ecf-a6ae-d9f41655cdd4","ku-2745c63e-9abd-40a0-983c-8ddb4f73ab70","nfa-24aeb80f-096b-4205-86cf-3dec0db096ce"],"src":["AU Aarhus University","KU University of Copenhagen","NFA National Research Centre for the Working Environment"],"title":"Night work and hypertensive disorders of pregnancy : A national register-based cohort study","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S03-099.67-767826","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
{"abstract":"BACKGROUND: The ability of cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) lactate to distinguish between acute bacterial meningitis (ABM) and aseptic meningitis/encephalitis (AME) is debated. We assessed the diagnostic value of CSF lactate to discriminate between ABM and AME. METHODS: We included 176 patients from a prospective adult cohort with neuroinfections. In total, 51 ABM and 125 AME patients with clinically and/or microbiologically diagnosed acute meningitis were examined with CSF-lactate and traditional markers for infection. Receiver operating characteristic (ROC) curves were used to assess diagnostic performance. RESULTS: In CSF, lactate, leukocytes, fraction of neutrophils, protein and glucose ratio, were significantly different between the ABM and AME groups. CSF lactate had the best diagnostic value, with an area under the curve (AUC) of 0.976 (95%CI 0.966-0.997) and using a cut-off of 3.5 mmol/L a sensitivity of 96% and specificity of 85%. Antibiotic treatment before lumbar puncture had no significant effect on the AUC of CSF lactate. CONCLUSIONS: Compared to traditional CSF-markers, CSF lactate is more accurate to distinguish between ABM and AME.","author_count":11,"contributors":["Buch, Kristian","Bodilsen, Jacob","Knudsen, Andreas","Larsen, Lykke","Helweg-Larsen, Jannik","Storgaard, Merete","Brandt, Christian","Wiese, Lothar","Østergaard, Christian","Nielsen, Henrik","Lebech, Anne-Mette"],"cuid":["S04-092.52-005936"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":4,"corpau":["Danish Study Group for Infections in the Brain","for the Danish Study Group for Infections in the Brain"],"corresponding_author":["Buch, Kristian"],"keyword":["Acute Disease","Acute bacterial meningitis","Adult","Aged","Area Under Curve","Aseptic meningitis","Biomarkers cerebrospinal fluid","CSF lactate","Cohort Studies","Community Acquired Infections cerebrospinal fluid","Data Accuracy","Diagnosis Differential","Encephalitis","Female","Humans","Lactic Acid cerebrospinal fluid","Male","Meningitis Aseptic cerebrospinal fluid","Meningitis Bacterial cerebrospinal fluid","Middle Aged","Prospective Studies","ROC Curve","Sensitivity and Specificity","Spinal Puncture"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-NDR","DI-HO-SDR","DI-HO-ZEA"],"OT":["Department of Infectious Diseases, Copenhagen University Hospital/Rigshospitalet & University of Copenhagen, Copenhagen, Denmark."],"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-NDR","DI-HO-SDR","DI-HO-ZEA","DI-U-AAU","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"cerebrospinal fluid lactate as a marker to differentiate between community acquired acute bacterial meningitis and aseptic meningitis encephalitis in adults a danish prospective observational cohort study","src":["aau","au","ku","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85042939270","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S04-092.52-005936","identifier":[{"id":"29490540","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85042939270","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["aau-7a4a0355-6beb-4f4a-a5af-9812895736c3","au-3eab88d2-df2f-4d54-91b2-0b74d9981e52","ku-91630e8e-aa13-467c-9997-ec2f3eeb95a2","sdu-2e576c7d-d46c-434e-bd90-cd926148801a"],"keyword":["Acute Disease","Acute bacterial meningitis","Adult","Aged","Area Under Curve","Aseptic meningitis","Biomarkers/cerebrospinal fluid","CSF lactate","Cohort Studies","Community-Acquired Infections/cerebrospinal fluid","Data Accuracy","Diagnosis, Differential","Encephalitis","Female","Humans","Lactic Acid/cerebrospinal fluid","Male","Meningitis, Aseptic/cerebrospinal fluid","Meningitis, Bacterial/cerebrospinal fluid",