{"abstract":"To investigate dynamic coronary MR angiography using hyperpolarized water as a positive contrast agent. Hyperpolarization can increase the signal by several orders of magnitude, and has recently been translated to human cardiac application. The aim was to achieve large 1 H signal enhancement to allow high-resolution imaging of the coronary arteries. Protons in D2 O were hyperpolarized by dissolution dynamic nuclear polarization. A total of 18 mL of hyperpolarized water was injected into the coronary arteries of healthy pigs (N = 9; 3 injections in 3 animals). The MRI images were acquired with a gradient-echo sequence in an oblique slab covering the main left coronary arteries with 0.55 mm in-plane resolution. The acquisition time was 870 ms per frame. A more than 200-fold signal enhancement compared with thermally polarized water at 3 T was obtained. Coronary angiographic images with a signal-to-noise ratio from the left main stem of 269 ± 169 and coronary sharpness from the proximal left anterior descending coronary artery of 0.31 ± 0.086 mm-1 were obtained. Dynamic images were acquired over a 10 s time window. Hyperpolarized water MR angiography of the coronary arteries in a large animal model with high signal-to-noise ratio and high spatial and temporal resolution was obtained. Magn Reson Med, 2018. © 2018 International Society for Magnetic Resonance in Medicine.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Lipsø, Kasper Wigh","Søvsø Szocska Hansen, Esben","Tougaard, Rasmus Stilling","Laustsen, Christoffer","Ardenkjaer-Larsen, Jan Henrik"],"cuid":["S02-096.05-027706"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"corresponding_author":["Ardenkjær-Larsen, Jan Henrik"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","GE Healthcare"],"keyword":["Coronary Angiography","Dissolution DNP","Hyperpolarization","Journal Article","Magnetic Resonance Angiography"],"level":"sci","mra":["med","sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-SDR"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-DTU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-SDR","DI-U-AU","DI-U-DTU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"dynamic coronary mr angiography in a pig model with hyperpolarized water","src":["au","dtu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-DTU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/142514990/Lips_et_al_2018_Magnetic_Resonance_in_Medicine.pdf","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S02-096.05-027706","identifier":[{"id":"29327374","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85047812501","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-cf355c9a-64fe-4c19-9e6b-e6a16df62b80","dtu-324f3c0f-1b67-4bf2-b036-a7eb3664beb2"],"keyword":["Cardiac perfusion","Coronary angiography","Dissolution-DNP","Hyperpolarization","Journal Article","Magnetic resonance angiography"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science","Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-135F4B69CDF84FB99E76F38D5D9BA806"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/142514990/Lips_et_al_2018_Magnetic_Resonance_in_Medicine.pdf","version":"Other Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2018","yearSub":"2018"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, Department of Electrical Engineering"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - The MR Research Centre"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"dk","key":"region of southern denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-SDR","l3nl":"Region of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"Region of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Region of Southern Denmark","namevar":["Odense University Hospital"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-SDR","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Hans Kasper Wigh","key":"lipsøpau","last":"Lipsø","locid":"68c4d525-3c44-4330-8725-7d6bc61ccc8f","name":"Lipsø, Hans Kasper Wigh","orcid":"0000-0002-8791-9997","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 3","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Esben Søvsø Szocska","key":"hansenpau","last":"Hansen","locid":"2ea5821e-bbda-4f7e-a18a-0b9eb3b993f0","name":"Søvsø Szocska Hansen, Esben","name_org":"Hansen, Esben Søvsø Szocska","orcid":"0000-0001-5512-9870","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Rasmus Stilling","key":"tougaardpau","last":"Tougaard","locid":"ed4f8644-8a58-424c-9f0c-dac467df4a24","name":"Tougaard, Rasmus Stilling","orcid":"0000-0003-2199-0405","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Christoffer","key":"laustsenpau","last":"Laustsen","locid":"ba5f7084-cbbb-4c1a-9fb6-85cce410d52a","name":"Laustsen, Christoffer","orcid":"0000-0002-0317-2911","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jan Henrik","key":"ardenkjærlarsenpau","last":"Ardenkjær-Larsen","locid":"ae9519c9-1b64-4426-b89f-4c36d08c1836","name":"Ardenkjær-Larsen, Jan Henrik","orcid":"0000-0001-6167-6926","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["29327374"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1002/mrm.27088","issn":["07403194","15222594"],"issn_org":["07403194"],"issue":"3","journal":"Magnetic Resonance in Medicine","pages":"1165-1169","publisher":"John Wiley and Sons Inc.","status":"p","title":"Magnetic Resonance in Medicine","volume":"80"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85047812501"],"sdg":["3. Good Health and Well-being","All UN SDG Classification"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Region of Southern Denmark","Technical University of Denmark","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-cf355c9a-64fe-4c19-9e6b-e6a16df62b80","dtu-324f3c0f-1b67-4bf2-b036-a7eb3664beb2"],"src":["AU Aarhus University","DTU Technical University of Denmark"],"title":"Dynamic coronary MR angiography in a pig model with hyperpolarized water","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-096.05-027706","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
{"abstract":"The role of host genetic variation in the development of complicated Staphylococcus aureus bacteremia (SAB) is poorly understood. We used whole exome sequencing (WES) to examine the cumulative effect of coding variants in each gene on risk of complicated SAB in a discovery sample of 168 SAB cases (84 complicated and 84 uncomplicated, frequency matched by age, sex, and bacterial clonal complex [CC]), and then evaluated the most significantly associated genes in a replication sample of 240 SAB cases (122 complicated and 118 uncomplicated, frequency matched for age, sex, and CC) using targeted sequence capture. In the discovery sample, gene-based analysis using the SKAT-O program identified 334 genes associated with complicated SAB at p<3.5 x 10 −3. These, along with eight biologically relevant candidate genes were examined in the replication sample. Gene-based analysis of the 342 genes in the replication sample using SKAT-O identified one gene, GLS2, significantly associated with complicated SAB (p = 1.2 x 10 −4) after Bonferroni correction. In Firth-bias corrected logistic regression analysis of individual variants, the strongest association across all 10,931 variants in the replication sample was with rs2657878 in GLS2 (p = 5 x 10 −4). This variant is strongly correlated with a missense variant (rs2657879, p = 4.4 x 10 −3) in which the minor allele (associated here with complicated SAB) has been previously associated with lower plasma concentration of glutamine. In a microarray-based gene-expression analysis, individuals with SAB exhibited significantly lower expression levels of GLS2 than healthy controls. Similarly, Gls2 expression is lower in response to S. aureus exposure in mouse RAW 264.7 macrophage cells. Compared to wild-type cells, RAW 264.7 cells with Gls2 silenced by CRISPR-Cas9 genome editing have decreased IL1-β transcription and increased nitric oxide production after S. aureus exposure. GLS2 is an interesting candidate gene for complicated SAB due to its role in regulating glutamine metabolism, a key factor in leukocyte activation.","author_count":29,"contributors":["Scott, William K","Medie, Felix Mba","Ruffin, Felicia","Sharma-Kuinkel, Batu K","Cyr, Derek D","Guo, Shengru","Dykxhoorn, Derek M","Skov, Robert L","Bruun, Niels Eske","Dahl, Anders","Lerche, Christian J","Petersen, Andreas","Larsen, Anders Rhod","Lauridsen, Trine Kiilerich","Johansen, Helle Krogh","Ullum, Henrik","Sørensen, Erik","Hassager, Christian","Bundgaard, Henning","Schønheyder, Henrik Carl","Torp-Pedersen, Christian","Oestergaard, Louise Bruun","Arpi, Magnus","Rosenvinge, Flemming","Erikstrup, Lise T","Chehri, Mahtab","Søgaard, Peter","Andersen, Paal S","Fowler, Vance G"],"cuid":["S04-096.54-003121"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":4,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"country":["United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Duke Clinical Research Institute","Duke University","Duke University Medical Center","University of Miami"],"keyword":["Adolescent","Adult","Aged","Aged 80 and over","Alleles","Animals","Bacteremia","Female","Gene Frequency genetics","Genetic Variation genetics","Humans","Male","Mice","Middle Aged","RAW 264 7 Cells","Risk Factors","Staphylococcus aureus pathogenicity","Transcriptome genetics","Whole Exome Sequencing methods"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-HO","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"GV":["DI-GV-ALL","DI-GV-SSI"],"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-NDR","DI-HO-SDR"],"OT":["Dr. John T. Macdonald Foundation Department of Human Genetics, Miller School of Medicine, University of Miami, Miami, FL, United States of America."],"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-GV-SSI","DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-HO-NDR","DI-HO-SDR","DI-U-AAU","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","