{"abstract":"Summary: Deficiency of apolipoprotein E (APOE) causes familial dysbetalipoproteinemia in humans resulting in a higher risk of atherosclerotic disease. In mice, APOE deficiency results in a severe atherosclerosis phenotype, but it is unknown to what extent this is unique to mice. In this study, APOE was targeted in Yucatan minipigs. APOE−/− minipigs displayed increased plasma cholesterol and accumulation of apolipoprotein B-48–containing chylomicron remnants on low-fat diet, which was significantly accentuated upon feeding a high-fat, high-cholesterol diet. APOE−/− minipigs displayed accelerated progressive atherosclerosis but not xanthoma formation. This indicates that remnant lipoproteinemia does not induce early lesions but is atherogenic in pre-existing atherosclerosis.","author_count":14,"contributors":["Shim, Jeong Tangkjær","Poulsen, Christian Bo","Hagensen, Mette K","Larsen, Torben","Heegaard, Peter M H","Christoffersen, Christina","Bolund, Lars","Schmidt, Mette","Liu, Ying","Li, Juan","Li, Rong","Callesen, Henrik","Bentzon, Jacob Fog","Sørensen, Charlotte Brandt"],"cuid":["S03-088.14-666129"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":3,"continent":["Asia, East"],"country":["China"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Nanjing Agricultural University"],"keyword":["Apolipoprotein E","Atherosclerosis","Pig","Remnant cholesterol dysbetalipoproteinemia"],"level":"sci","mra":["med","sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-AU","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"apolipoprotein e deficiency increases remnant lipoproteins and accelerates progressive atherosclerosis but not xanthoma formation in gene modified minipigs","src":["au","dtu","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-DTU"