{"abstract":"Renal ischemia/reperfusion injury (IRI) is a leading cause of acute kidney injury (AKI), and at present, there is a lack of reliable biomarkers that can diagnose AKI and measure early progression because the commonly used methods cannot evaluate single-kidney IRI. Hyperpolarized [1,4-(13)C2]fumarate conversion to [1,4-(13)C2]malate by fumarase has been proposed as a measure of necrosis in rat tumor models and in chemically induced AKI rats. Here we show that the degradation of cell membranes in connection with necrosis leads to elevated fumarase activity in plasma and urine and secondly that hyperpolarized [1,4-(13)C2]malate production 24 h after reperfusion correlates with renal necrosis in a 40-min unilateral ischemic rat model. Fumarase activity screening on bio-fluids can detect injury severity, in bilateral as well as unilateral AKI models, differentiating moderate and severe AKI as well as short- and long-term AKI. Furthermore after verification of renal injury by bio-fluid analysis the precise injury location can be monitored by in vivo measurements of the fumarase activity non-invasively by hyperpolarized [1,4-(13)C]fumarate MR imaging. The combined in vitro and in vivo biomarker of AKI responds to the essential requirements for a new reliable biomarker of AKI.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Nielsen, Per Mose","Eldirdiri, Abubakr","Bertelsen, Lotte Bonde","Stødkilde-Jørgensen, Hans","Ardenkjaer-Larsen, Jan Henrik","Laustsen, Christoffer"],"cuid":["S02-084.59-021736"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"keyword":["Enzyme activity","Enzymes General and comparative studies coenzymes","Genetics Animal","MRI","Multidisciplinary","Necrosis","Reperfusion Injury"],"level":"sci","mra":["med","sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-DTU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-DTU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"fumarase activity an in vivo and in vitro biomarker for acute kidney injury an in vivo and in vitro biomarker for acute kidney injury","src":["au","dtu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-DTU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/132541468/srep40812.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-084.59-021736","identifier":[{"id":"85009887785","type":"scopus"},{"id":"28094329","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC5240145","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["au-7c6aabf1-5363-4a7d-81d6-3527b6b9974d","dtu-aa030d43-a990-41c3-a74b-52b6b962d81b"],"keyword":["Animals, Chordates, Mammals, Nonhuman Vertebrates, Nonhuman Mammals, Rodents, Vertebrates","Biochemistry studies - Nucleic acids, purines and pyrimidines","Blood - Blood and lymph studies","Blood - Blood cell studies","Cardiovascular system - Blood vessel pathology","Enzymes - General and comparative studies: coenzymes","Genetics - Animal","MRI","Multidisciplinary","Reperfusion Injury","Urinary system - Pathology","Urinary system - Physiology and biochemistry","enzyme activity","necrosis"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science","Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-1E890B246DA7D3054019C1881E4BFD2D"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/132541468/srep40812.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2017","yearSub":"2017"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - The MR Research Centre"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, Centers, Center for Hyperpolarization in Magnetic Resonance","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Electrical Engineering","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Electrical Engineering, Center for Magnetic Resonance"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Per Mose","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"e1a60076-a622-4795-8777-6c13958aba7d","name":"Nielsen, Per Mose","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Abubakr","key":"eldirdiripau","last":"Eldirdiri","locid":"03e69644-72e2-4e46-87f9-948cfd8929d3","name":"Eldirdiri, Abubakr","orcid":"0000-0002-3879-3881","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Lotte Bonde","key":"bertelsenpau","last":"Bertelsen","locid":"17c3813d-f5fd-4a91-9ca6-79a0a1de421d","name":"Bertelsen, Lotte Bonde","orcid":"0000-0002-2491-3184","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Hans Stødkilde","key":"jørgensenpau","last":"Jørgensen","locid":"5d4cd2e1-37e5-468a-9048-d492c0a466bf","name":"Stødkilde-Jørgensen, Hans","name_org":"Jørgensen, Hans Stødkilde","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Jan Henrik","key":"ardenkjærlarsenpau","last":"Ardenkjær-Larsen","locid":"ae9519c9-1b64-4426-b89f-4c36d08c1836","name":"Ardenkjær-Larsen, Jan Henrik","orcid":"0000-0001-6167-6926","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Christoffer","key":"laustsenpau","last":"Laustsen","locid":"ba5f7084-cbbb-4c1a-9fb6-85cce410d52a","name":"Laustsen, Christoffer","orcid":"0000-0002-0317-2911","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["28094329"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1038/srep40812","issn":["20452322"],"journal":"Scientific Reports","pages":"40812","paperid":"40812","publisher":"Nature Publishing Group","status":"p","title":"Scientific Reports","volume":"7"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85009887785"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Technical University of Denmark","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-7c6aabf1-5363-4a7d-81d6-3527b6b9974d","dtu-aa030d43-a990-41c3-a74b-52b6b962d81b"],"src":["AU Aarhus University","DTU Technical University of Denmark"],"title":"Fumarase activity: an in vivo and in vitro biomarker for acute kidney injury : an in vivo and in vitro biomarker for acute kidney injury","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-084.59-021736","year":"2017","yearsub":"2017"}
{"abstract":"Background and purpose - The use of uncemented fixation in total hip arthroplasty (THA) is increasing. Registry studies have indicated an increased risk of revision of uncemented implants due to early periprosthetic femoral fracture. In this paper, we describe the incidence and predisposing factors for intraoperative and early postoperative (≤ 90 days) periprosthetic femoral fractures after cemented and uncemented THA. Patients and methods - This was a prospective observational study in 8 Danish high-volume centers from February 2010 to November 2013. We used the 90-day follow-up from the Danish National Patient Registry and patient records. We obtained intraoperative information from the Danish Hip Arthroplasty Registry and from surgical notes. Results - Of 7,169 primary consecutive THAs, 5,482 (77%) were performed using uncemented femoral components. The total incidence of periprosthetic femoral fractures ≤ 90 days postoperatively was 2.1% (n = 150). 70 fractures were detected intraoperatively (46 required osteosynthesis). 51 postoperatively detected fractures occurred without trauma (42 of which were reoperated) and 29 were postoperative fall-related fractures (27 of which were reoperated). 134 fractures (2.4%) were found in uncemented femoral components and 16 (0.9%) were found in cemented femoral components (p < 0.001). Uncemented femoral stem (relative risk (RR) = 4.1, 95% CI: 2.3-7.2), medically treated osteoporosis (RR =2.8, CI: 1.6-4.8), female sex (RR =1.6, CI: 1.1-2.2), and age (RR =1.4 per 10 years, CI: 1.2-1.6) were associated with increased risk of periprosthetic femoral fracture when analyzed using multivariable regression analysis. Interpretation - Uncemented femoral components were associated with an increased risk of early periprosthetic femoral fractures, especially in elderly, female, and osteoporotic patients.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Lindberg-Larsen, Martin","Jørgensen, Christoffer C.","Solgaard, Søren","Kjersgaard, Anne G","Kehlet, Henrik","Laursen, Mogens Berg"],"cuid":["S02-098.01-002150"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"corpau":["Lundbeck Foundation Centre for Fast-Track Hip and Knee Replacement Collaborative Group"],"keyword":["Adolescent","Adult","Age Factors","Aged","Aged 80 and over","Arthroplasty Replacement Hip","Bone Cements","Female","Femoral Fractures","Hip Prosthesis","Humans","Intraoperative Period","Journal Article","Male","Middle Aged","Observational Study","Postoperative Complications","Prospective Studies","Registries","Risk Factors","Young Adult"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"increased risk of intraoperative and early postoperative periprosthetic femoral fracture with uncemented stems","src":["aau","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1080/17453674.2017.1302908","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-098.01-002150","identifier":[{"id":"28290738","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC5499329","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"85015250788","type":"scopus"},{"id":"000404588400005","type":"wos"}],"ids":["aau-2ac5cade-2ea4-4881-ba19-2d8dcabfefc8","ku-6a450c9b-a408-459d-9ec2-91977d612e1c"],"keyword":["Adolescent","Adult","Age Factors","Aged","Aged, 80 and over","Arthroplasty, Replacement, Hip","Bone Cements","Female","Femoral Fractures","Hip Prosthesis","Humans","Intraoperative Period","Journal Article","Male","Middle Aged","Observational Study","Postoperative Complications","Prospective Studies","Registries","Risk Factors","Young Adult"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-72654DF05FFFE38B9AEC70B0E22758C8"],"oa_link":[{"ac