{"abstract":"This work provides an innovative look at the use of open data for extracting information to detect and prevent crime, and also explores the link between terrorism and organized crime. In counter-terrorism and other forms of crime prevention, foresight about potential threats is vitally important and this information is increasingly available via electronic data sources such as social media communications. However, the amount and quality of these sources is varied, and researchers and law enforcement need guidance about when and how to extract useful information from them. The emergence of these crime threats, such as communication between organized crime networks and radicalization towards terrorism, is driven by a combination of political, economic, social, technological, legal and environmental factors. The contributions to this volume represent a major step by researchers to systematically collect, filter, interpret, and use the information available. For the purposes of this book, the only data sources used are publicly available sources which can be accessed legally and ethically. This work will be of interest to researchers in criminology and criminal justice, particularly in police science, organized crime, counter-terrorism and crime science. It will also be of interest to those in related fields such as applications of computer science and data mining, public policy, and business intelligence.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Larsen, Henrik Legind","Blanco, José María","Pastor Pastor, Raquel","Yager, Ronald R."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-056948"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Spain"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Centre of Analysis and Foresight, Guardia Civil","ISDEFE, Defence Ministry"],"keyword":["Early warning","Environmental scanning","Foresight","Indicators","Methodology","Organised crime","Predictive policing","Strategic analysis","Threats"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"OT":["Machine Intelligence InstituteIona College Machine Intelligence Institute"],"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"using open data to detect organized crime threats factors driving future crime","src":["aau"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU"],"type":"db","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-056948","identifier":[{"id":"85054775239","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["aau-177b1bba-64ee-46f8-9b3d-c8bda04666dd"],"keyword":["crime facilitating factors","early warning","environmental scanning","foresight","future crime","indicators","methodology","organised crime","predictive policing","strategic analysis","threats"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-90546ED784B0B3416F2EF8E09F9751D6"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design","Aalborg University, The Technical Faculty of IT and Design, Department of Electronic Systems"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"es","key":"centre of analysis and foresight guardia civil","loc":["Spain","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Centre of Analysis and Foresight, Guardia Civil","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"es","key":"isdefe defence ministry","loc":["Spain","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"ISDEFE, Defence Ministry","used":1},{"affno":"4","key":"miscellaneous","l2":"DI-OT","l2n":"Miscellaneous","l3":"DI-OT","l3nl":"Other Research","l3nn":"Miscellaneous","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Miscellaneous","namevar":["Machine Intelligence InstituteIona College Machine Intelligence Institute"],"nora_code":"DI-OT","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Henrik Legind","key":"larsenped","last":"Larsen","locid":"b686118d-6daf-4022-840f-846762685da7","name":"Larsen, Henrik Legind","orcid":"0000-0002-4276-6245","rank":1,"role":"Editor","rolecode":"ped"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"José María","key":"blancoped","last":"Blanco","name":"Blanco, José María","rank":2,"role":"Editor","rolecode":"ped"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Raquel","key":"pastorpastorped","last":"Pastor Pastor","name":"Pastor Pastor, Raquel","rank":3,"role":"Editor","rolecode":"ped"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Ronald R.","key":"yagerped","last":"Yager","name":"Yager, Ronald R.","rank":4,"role":"Editor","rolecode":"ped"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-319-52703-1","eisbn":["9783319527031"],"isbn":["9783319527024"],"pages":"282","publisher":"Springer","status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85024903891","2-s2.0-85054775239"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Miscellaneous","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Miscellaneous","Other Research","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-177b1bba-64ee-46f8-9b3d-c8bda04666dd"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University"],"title":"Using Open Data to Detect Organized Crime Threats : Factors Driving Future Crime","type":"Book","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-056948","year":"2017","yearsub":"2017"}
{"abstract":"AIMS: The aim of this study was to investigate whether there is a similar mortality and thrombo-embolic risk, after an atrial ablation procedure, compared with an atrial fibrillation (AF) procedure. METHODS AND RESULTS: Using data from nationwide Danish health registries, we identified patients aged 18-75 years undergoing a first-time atrial flutter or an AF ablation procedure in the period 2000-13. Cox proportional hazards regression was used to calculate hazard ratios (HRs) after 5 years of follow-up, adjusting for concomitant risk factors. A total of 1096 and 2266 patients underwent an ablation for atrial flutter or AF, respectively. Age distribution was similar in the two, but atrial flutter patients had more co-morbidities. During 5 years of follow-up, we observed 38 and 36 deaths in the atrial flutter and AF groups, corresponding to an almost two-fold higher mortality rate among atrial flutter patients [crude HR 1.92, 95% confidence interval (CI) 1.22-3.03]. The higher mortality rate persisted after adjustment for age, sex, diabetes mellitus, and hypertension (adjusted HR 1.68, 95% CI 1.05-2.69). The rate of thrombo-embolic events was similar in the two groups (crude HR 1.34, 95% CI 0.71-2.56; adjusted HR 1.22, 95% CI 0.62-2.41). CONCLUSION: In this observational study, patients with atrial flutter had a significantly higher all-cause mortality rate compared with those with AF after an ablation procedure, but similar thrombo-embolic event rates. Future studies should elucidate the reason for this difference in mortality.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Vadmann, Henrik","Gorst-Rasmussen, Anders","Hjortshøj, Søren Pihlkjær","Riahi, Sam","Lip, Gregory Y H","Larsen, Torben Bjerregaard"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-095916"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Birmingham"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"death and thrombo embolic risk after ablation of atrial flutter compared with atrial fibrillation a nationwide cohort study","src":["aau"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-095916","identifier":[{"id":"000404444200031","type":"wos"},{"id":"27738075","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85027418366","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["aau-9e2e0864-8e7a-4aca-9b5e-294287264d39"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-C0373FE97996818DF001B09E6908D115"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2017","yearSub":"2017"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Faculty of Medicine","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Medicine, Aalborg University Hospital","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Medicine, Aalborg University Hospital, Aalborg AF Study Group","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Medicine, Aalborg University Hospital, Hjertemedicin (Kardiologi) [Klinik Hjerte-Lunge]","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Medicine, Aalborg University Hospital, Klinik Hjerte-Lunge","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine, Aalborg Thrombosis Research Unit"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gb","key":"university of birmingham","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Birmingham","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Henrik","key":"vadmannpau","last":"Vadmann","locid":"e4e70ebf-6db8-4fca-8b41-6b7e3657bfb1","name":"Vadmann, Henrik","orcid":"0000-0001-8551-9271","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Anders","key":"gorstrasmussenpau","last":"Gorst-Rasmussen","locid":"2aa85786-c0b1-42ec-93d4-eb7a75696409","name":"Gorst-Rasmussen, Anders","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Søren Pihlkjær","key":"hjortshøjpau","last":"Hjortshøj","locid":"d24227f4-62ce-4a90-86ec-482cf4e88a34","name":"Hjortshøj, Søren Pihlkjær","orcid":"0000-0002-4231-9963","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Sam","key":"riahipau","last":"Riahi","locid":"9e5489d8-981a-469c-bd84-20b2e24832de","name":"Riahi, Sam","orcid":"0000-0003-1849-9463","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Gregory Y H","key":"lippau","last":"Lip","locid":"8fa30e84-7381-41b4-8037-afc032238a3d","name":"Lip, Gregory Y H","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Torben Bjerregaard","key":"larsenpau","last":"Larsen","locid":"a3c6747f-b9db-488b-8eb9-887d38742880","name":"Larsen, Torben Bjerregaard","orcid":"0000-0002-8485-8674","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["27738075"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1093/europace/euw107","issn":["10995129","10996044"],"issn_org":["10995129"],"issue":"5","journal":"Europace","pages":"838-842","publisher":"Oxford University Press","status":"p","title":"Europace","volume":"19"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85027418366"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-9e2e0864-8e7a-4aca-9b5e-294287264d39"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University"],"title":"Death and thrombo-embolic risk after ablation of atrial flutter compared with atrial fibrillation : a nationwide cohort study","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-095916","year":"2017","yearsub":"2017"}
{"abstract":"Correct staging of pancreatic cancer is paramount, as treatment is stage specific. However, minimally invasive tools to facilitate staging are lacking. DNA promoter hypermethylation is a hallmark of cancer. The aim of this study is to evaluate promoter hypermethylation in cell-free DNA as a prognostic marker for stage classification of pancreatic adenocarcinoma. Consecutive patients with pancreatic adenocarcinoma were prospectively included. Plasma samples were obtained before diagnostic work-up and treatment. Patients were staged according to the TNM classification. Methylation-specific PCR of 28 genes was performed. Prognostic prediction models for staging of pancreatic adenocarcinoma were developed by multivariable logistic regression analysis using stepwise backwards elimination. Ninety-five patients with pancreatic adenocarcinoma were included. The mean number of hypermethylated genes was identical for stage I, II and III disease (7.09 (95% CI; 5.51-8.66), 7.00 (95% CI; 5.93-8.07) and 6.77 (95% CI; 5.08-8.46)), respectively, and highly significantly different from stage IV disease (10.24 (95% CI; 8.88-11.60)). The prediction model (SEPT9v2, SST, ALX4, CDKN2B, HIC1, MLH1, NEUROG1, and BNC1) enabled the differentiation of stage IV from stage I-III disease (AUC of 0.87 (cut point 0.55; sensitivity 74%, specificity 87%)). Model (MLH1, SEPT9v2, BNC1, ALX4, CDKN2B, NEUROG1, WNT5A, and TFPI2) enabled the differentiation of stage I-II from stage III-IV disease (AUC of 0.82 (cut point 0.66; sensitivity 73%, specificity 80%)). Cell-free DNA promoter hypermethylation has the potential to be blood-based prognostic markers for pancreatic adenocarcinoma, as panels of hypermethylated genes enables the differentiation according to cancer stage. However, further validation is required.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Henriksen, Stine Dam","Madsen, Poul Henning","Larsen, Anders Christian","Johansen, Martin Berg","Pedersen, Inge Søkilde","Krarup, Henrik","Thorlaci