{"abstract":"Migraine with aura (MA) is characterized by cortical dysfunction. Frequent aura attacks may alter cerebral cortical structure in patients, or structural grey matter abnormalities may predispose MA patients to aura attacks. In the present study we aimed to investigate cerebral grey matter structure in a large group of MA patients with and without sensory aura (i.e. gradually developing, transient unilateral sensory disturbances). We included 60 patients suffering from migraine with typical visual aura and 60 individually age and sex-matched controls. Twenty-nine of the patients additionally experienced sensory aura regularly. We analysed high-resolution structural MR images using two complimentary approaches and compared patients with and without sensory aura. Patients were also compared to controls. We found no differences of grey matter density or cortical thickness between patients with and without sensory aura and no differences for the cortical visual areas between patients and controls. The somatosensory cortex was thinner in patients (1.92 mm vs. 1.96 mm, P = 0.043) and the anterior cingulate cortex of patients had a decreased grey matter density (P = 0.039) compared to controls. These differences were not correlated to the clinical characteristics. Our results suggest that sensory migraine aura is not associated with altered grey matter structure and that patients with visual aura have normal cortical structure of areas involved in visual processing. The observed decreased grey matter volume of the cingulate gyrus in patients compared to controls have previously been reported in migraine with and without aura, but also in a wide range of other neurologic and psychiatric disorders. Most likely, this finding reflects general bias between patients and healthy controls.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Hougaard, Anders","Amin, Faisal Mohammad","Arngrim, Nanna","Vlachou, Maria","Larsen, Vibeke Andrée","Larsson, Henrik B W","Ashina, Messoud"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-502505"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"sensory migraine aura is not associated with structural grey matter abnormalities","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.nicl.2016.02.007","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-502505","identifier":[{"id":"27298761","type":"pmid"},{"id":"84960436542","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC4893014","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["ku-b6a2f017-95b2-4d82-ba59-f2be930c1e7d"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-2DE6F640927962808E6FC64B40A7EC78"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.nicl.2016.02.007","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2016","yearSub":"2016"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","first":"Anders","key":"hougaardpau","last":"Hougaard","name":"Hougaard, Anders","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Faisal Mohammad","key":"aminpau","last":"Amin","name":"Amin, Faisal Mohammad","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Nanna","key":"arngrimpau","last":"Arngrim","name":"Arngrim, Nanna","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Maria","key":"vlachoupau","last":"Vlachou","name":"Vlachou, Maria","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Vibeke Andrée","key":"larsenpau","last":"Larsen","name":"Larsen, Vibeke Andrée","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Henrik B W","key":"larssonpau","last":"Larsson","locid":"3c09e175-dfd1-4df5-9132-536a42c19373","name":"Larsson, Henrik B W","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Messoud","key":"ashinapau","last":"Ashina","locid":"0e941844-26c5-4777-a66a-64a163c19cd2","name":"Ashina, Messoud","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["27298761"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.nicl.2016.02.007","issn":["22131582"],"journal":"NeuroImage: Clinical","pages":"322-327","status":"p","title":"NeuroImage: Clinical","volume":"11"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84960436542"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-b6a2f017-95b2-4d82-ba59-f2be930c1e7d"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Sensory migraine aura is not associated with structural grey matter abnormalities","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-502505","year":"2016","yearsub":"2016"}
{"abstract":"Purpose To prospectively investigate the diagnostic accuracy of [ 181F]fluorodeoxyglucose (FDG)-positron emission tomography (PET)/computed tomography (CT) with dual-time-point imaging, contrastenhanced CT (ceCT), and bone scintigraphy (BS) in patients with suspected breast cancer recurrence. Patients and Methods One hundred women with suspected recurrence of breast cancer underwent 1-hour and 3-hour FDG-PET/CT, ceCT, and BS within approximately 10 days. The study was powered to estimate the precision of the individual imaging tests. Images were visually interpreted using a four-point assessment scale, and readers were blinded to other test results. The reference standard was biopsy along with treatment decisions and clinical follow-up (median, 17 months). Results FDG-PET/CT resulted in no false negatives and fewer false positives than the other imaging techniques. Accuracy of results were similar for 1-hour and 3-hour FDG-PET/CT. For distant recurrence, the area under the receiver operating curve was 0.99 (95% CI, 0.97 to 1) for FDG-PET/CT, 0.84 (95% CI, 0.73 to 0.94) for ceCT, and 0.86 (95% CI, 0.77 to 0.94) for the combined ceCT+BS. Of 100 patients, 22 (22%) were verified with distant recurrence, and 18 of these had bone involvement. Nineteen patients (19%) had local recurrence only. In exploratory analyses, diagnostic accuracy of FDG-PET/CT was better than ceCT alone or ceCT combined with BS in diagnosing distant, bone, and local recurrence, shown by a greater area under the receiver operating curve and higher sensitivity, specificity, and superior likelihood ratios. Conclusion FDG-PET/CT was accurate in diagnosing recurrence in breast cancer patients. It allowed for distant recurrence to be correctly ruled out and resulted in only a small number of false-positive cases. Exploratory findings suggest that FDG-PET/CT has greater accuracy than conventional imaging technologies in this patient group. [ampi]]copy; 2016 by American Society of Clinical Oncology.","author_count":16,"contributors":["Hildebrandt, Malene Grubbe","Gerke, Oke","Baun, Christina","Falch Braas, Kirsten","Ansholm Hansen, Jeanette","Farahani, Ziba","Petersen, Henrik","Larsen, Lisbet Brønsro","Duvnjak, Sandra","Buskevica, I","Bektas, Selma","Søe, Katrine Lydolph","Jylling, Anne Marie Bak","Ewertz, Marianne","Alavi, Abass","Høilund-Carlsen, Poul Flemming"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-613787"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"country":["United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Pennsylvania"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"sup 000000000018 sup 000000000001f fluorodeoxyglucose fdg positron emission tomography pet computed tomography ct in suspected recurrent breast cancer a prospective comparative study of dual time point fdg pet ct contrast enhanced ct and bone scintigraphy","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1200/JCO.2015.63.5185","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-613787","identifier":[{"id":"27001573","type":"pmid"},{"id":"84974555718","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["sdu-8d92c82a-5c13-418d-abd5-83c9553e7fe7"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-F094B59D7F2AF372607E2B0EDF7EB7E6"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"Ikke-specificeret","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1200/JCO.2015.63.5185","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Blocked","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2016","yearSub":"2016"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southe