{"abstract":"Signal enhancement by hyperpolarization is a way of overcoming the low sensitivity in magnetic resonance; MRI in particular. One of the most well-known methods, dissolution Dynamic Nuclear Polarization, has been used clinically in cancer patients. One way of ensuring a low bioburden of the hyperpolarized product is by use of a closed fluid path that constitutes a barrier to contamination. The fluid path can be filled with the pharmaceuticals, i.e. imaging agent and solvents, in a clean room, and then stored or immediately used at the polarizer. In this study, we present a method of filling the fluid path that allows it to be reused. The filling method has been investigated in terms of reproducibility at two extrema, high dose for patient use and low dose for rodent studies, using [1-13C]pyruvate as example. We demonstrate that the filling method allows high reproducibility of six quality control parameters with standard deviations 3–10 times smaller than the acceptance criteria intervals in clinical studies.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Malinowski, Ronja Maja","Lipsø, Hans Kasper Wigh","Lerche, Mathilde Hauge","Ardenkjær-Larsen, Jan Henrik"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-304938"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Acceptance criteria","Biochemistry","Biophysics","Condensed Matter Physics","Dissolution","Dissolution DNP","Dynamic nuclear polarization","Filling","High reproducibility","Hyperpolarization","Magnetic resonance","Nuclear and High Energy Physics","Optical Instruments","Polarization","Polarizer","Spin polarization","Standard deviation"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"dissolution dynamic nuclear polarization capability study with fluid path","src":["dtu"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jmr.2016.09.015","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-304938","identifier":[{"id":"27693965","type":"pmid"},{"id":"84989180783","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["dtu-01652c50-a233-4796-a4cb-5c841110383e"],"keyword":["Acceptance criteria","Biochemistry","Biophysics","Condensed Matter Physics","Dissolution","Dissolution dynamics","Dissolution-DNP","Dynamic Nuclear Polarization","Filling","High reproducibility","Hyperpolarization","Magnetic resonance","Nuclear and High Energy Physics","Optical instruments","Polarization","Polarizer","Spin polarization","Standard deviation"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-D76FD7CB34FC762E8BED4C6C24A693CB"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jmr.2016.09.015","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/130629948/Dissolution_Dynamic_Nuclear_Polarization_capability_study_with_fluid_path_Post_print.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Blocked","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2016","yearSub":"2016"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, Department of Chemistry","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Electrical Engineering"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Ronja Maja","key":"malinowskipau","last":"Malinowski","locid":"30b6c771-8ec0-4485-a831-5fb2b3b2f500","name":"Malinowski, Ronja Maja","orcid":"0000-0002-2735-164X","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Hans Kasper Wigh","key":"lipsøpau","last":"Lipsø","locid":"68c4d525-3c44-4330-8725-7d6bc61ccc8f","name":"Lipsø, Hans Kasper Wigh","orcid":"0000-0002-8791-9997","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Mathilde Hauge","key":"lerchepau","last":"Lerche","locid":"bf55c4cb-8ebc-4c00-a107-f90c312e6e76","name":"Lerche, Mathilde Hauge","orcid":"0000-0002-5899-2781","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jan Henrik","key":"ardenkjærlarsenpau","last":"Ardenkjær-Larsen","locid":"ae9519c9-1b64-4426-b89f-4c36d08c1836","name":"Ardenkjær-Larsen, Jan Henrik","orcid":"0000-0001-6167-6926","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["27693965"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.jmr.2016.09.015","issn":["10907807","10960856"],"issn_org":["10907807"],"journal":"Journal of Magnetic Resonance","pages":"141-146","publisher":"Elsevier","status":"p","title":"Journal of Magnetic Resonance","volume":"272"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84989180783"],"sdg":["3. Good Health and Well-being","All UN SDG Classification"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Technical University of Denmark","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-01652c50-a233-4796-a4cb-5c841110383e"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark"],"title":"Dissolution Dynamic Nuclear Polarization capability study with fluid path","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-304938","year":"2016","yearsub":"2016"}
{"abstract":"Dissolution dynamic nuclear polarization (DNP) enables the acquisition of (13) C magnetic resonance data with a high sensitivity. Recently, metabolically inactive hyperpolarized (13) C-labeled compounds have shown to be potentially useful for perfusion imaging. The purpose of this study was to validate hyperpolarized perfusion imaging methods by comparing with conventional gadolinium (Gd)-based perfusion MRI techniques and pathology. Dynamic (13) C data using metabolically inactive hyperpolarized bis-1,1-(hydroxymethyl)-[1-(13) C]cyclopropane-d8 (HMCP) were obtained from an orthotopic human glioblastoma (GBM) model for the characterization of tumor perfusion and compared with standard Gd-based dynamic susceptibility contrast (DSC) MRI data and immunohistochemical analysis from resected brains. Distinct HMCP perfusion characteristics were observed within the GBM tumors compared with contralateral normal brain tissue. The perfusion parameters obtained from the hyperpolarized HMCP data in tumor were strongly correlated with normalized peak height measured from the DSC images. The results from immunohistochemical analysis supported these findings by showing a high level of vascular staining for tumor that exhibited high levels of hyperpolarized HMCP signal. The results from this study have demonstrated that hyperpolarized HMCP data can be used as an indicator of tumor perfusion in an orthotopic xenograft model for GBM. Magn Reson Med, 2016. © 2016 Wiley Periodicals, Inc.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Park, Ilwoo","von Morze, Cornelius","Lupo, Janine M","Ardenkjær-Larsen, Jan Henrik","Kadambi, Achuta","Vigneron, Daniel B","Nelson, Sarah J"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-320693"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"country":["United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Massachusetts Institute of Technology","University of California at San Diego"],"keyword":["Brain tumor","Dynamic nuclear polarization"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"investigating tumor perfusion by hyperpolarized 000000000013 c mri with comparison to conventional gadolinium contrast enhanced mri and pathology in orthotopic human gbm xenografts correlation of000000000013c perfusion imaging and gd enhanced contrast mri","src":["dtu"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/84975744557","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-320693","identifier":[{"id":"PMC4991955","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"84975744557","type":"scopus"},{"id":"26892398","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["dtu-3637ce9e-a4f0-4367-8f7b-617af7aa181b"],"keyword":["brain tumor","dynamic nuclear polarization","gadolinium dynamic susceptibility contrast imaging","hyperpolarized 13C perfusion MRI"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-B04A755FD43A46F9CE427ED56A8FEC02"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2016","yearSub":"2016"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"us","key":"university of california at san diego","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"University of California at San Diego","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, Centers, Center for Hyperpolarization in Magnetic Resonance","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Electrical Engineering","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Electrical Engineering, Center for Magnetic Resonance"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"us","key":"massachusetts institute of technology","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Massachusetts Institute of Technology","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Ilwoo","key":"parkpau","last":"Park","name":"Park, Ilwoo","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Cornelius","key":"vonmorzepau","last":"von Morze","name":"von Morze, Cornelius","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Janine M","key":"lupopau","last":"Lupo","name":"Lupo, Janine M","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Jan Henrik","key":"ardenkjærlarsenpau","last":"Ardenkjær-Larsen","locid":"ae9519c9-1b64-4426-b89f-4c36d08c1836","name":"Ardenkjær-Larsen, Jan Henrik","orcid":"0000-0001-6167-6926","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Achuta","key":"kadambipau","last":"Kadambi","name":"Kadambi, Achuta","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Daniel B","key":"vigneronpau","last":"Vigneron","name":"Vigneron, Daniel B","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Sarah J","key":"nelsonpau","last":"Nelson","name":"Nelson, Sarah J","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["26892398"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1002/mrm.26155","issn":["07403194","15222594"],"issn_org":["07403194"],"journal":"Magnetic Resonance in Medicine","publisher":"John Wiley and Sons Inc.","status":"p","title":"Magnetic Resonance in Medicine"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84975744557"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Technical University of Denmark","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-3637ce9e-a4f0-4367-8f7b-617af7aa181b"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark"],"title":"Investigating tumor perfusion by hyperpolarized (13) C MRI with comparison to conventional gadolinium contrast-enhanced MRI and pathology in orthotopic human GBM xenografts : Correlation of13C Perfusion Imaging and Gd-Enhanced Contrast MRI","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-320693","year":"2016","yearsub":"2016"}
{"abstract":"Incomplete knowledge of the longitudinal relaxationtime constant (T1) leads to incorrect assumptions in quantita-tive kinetic models of cellular systems, studied by hyper-polarized real-time NMR. Using an assay that measures theintracellular signal of small carboxylic acids in living cells, theintracellular T1of the carboxylic acid moiety of acetate, keto-isocaproate, pyruvate, and butyrate was determined. Theintracellular T1is shown to be up to four-fold shorter thanthe extracellular T1. Such a large difference in T1valuesbetween the inside and the outside of the cell has significantinfluence on the quantification of intracellular metabolicactivity. It is expected that the significantly shorter T1valueof the carboxylic moieties inside cells is a result of macro-molecular crowding. An artificial cytosol has been preparedand applied to predict the T1of other carboxylic acids. Wedemonstrate the value of this prediction tool.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Karlsson, Magnus","Jensen, Pernille Rose","Ardenkjær-Larsen, Jan Henrik","Lerche, Mathilde Hauge"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-357937"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Hyperpolarisation","NMR Spektroskopie"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"difference between extra and intracellular t000000000001 values of carboxylic acids affects the quantitative analysis of cellular kinetics by hyperpolarized nmr","src":["dtu"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/84991243025","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-357937","identifier":[{"id":"84991243025","type":"scopus"},{"id":"27666128","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["dtu-b29ed3df-5cf1-4a87-b42c-3ac5f9c49955"],"keyword":["Carbonsäuren","Hyperpolarisation","Longitudinale Relaxation","Lösungs-DNP","NMR-Spektroskopie"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-3-6-2171CCC1C85E7150BDE955BE237AC031"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2016","yearSub":"2016"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, Department of Electrical Engineering","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Electrical Engineering, Center for Magnetic Resonance"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Magnus","key":"karlssonpau","last":"Karlsson","locid":"b36d1ae3-33f2-4b98-9112-fbe3e155be82","name":"Karlsson, Magnus","orcid":"0000-0001-7738-1700","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Pernille Rose","key":"jensenpau","last":"Jensen","locid":"945cf0b9-5a16-4e82-a927-c9c42c2467b2","name":"Jensen, Pernille Rose","orcid":"0000-0003-4359-848X","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jan Henrik","key":"ardenkjærlarsenpau","last":"Ardenkjær-Larsen","locid":"ae9519c9-1b64-4426-b89f-4c36d08c1836","name":"Ardenkjær-Larsen, Jan Henrik","orcid":"0000-0001-6167-6926","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Mathilde Hauge","key":"lerchepau","last":"Lerche","locid":"bf55c4cb-8ebc-4c00-a107-f90c312e6e76","name":"Lerche, Mathilde Hauge","orcid":"0000-0002-5899-2781","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["27666128"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1002/anie.201607535","issn":["00448249","15213757"],"issn_org":["00448249"],"issue":"43","journal":"Angewandte Chemie","pages":"13765-13768","publisher":"John Wiley and Sons Ltd","status":"p","title":"Angewandte Chemie","volume":"128"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84991243025"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Technical University of Denmark","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-b29ed3df-5cf1-4a87-b42c-3ac5f9c49955"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark"],"title":"Difference between Extra- and Intracellular T1 Values of Carboxylic Acids Affects the Quantitative Analysis of Cellular Kinetics by Hyperpolarized NMR","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-357937","year":"2016","yearsub":"2016"}
