{"abstract":"Purpose: To use dynamic magnetic resonance spectroscopy (MRS) of hyperpolarized 13C-pyruvate to follow the progress over time in vivo of breast cancer metabolism in the MMTV-PymT model, and to follow the response to the anti-estrogen drug tamoxifen. Methods: Tumor growth was monitored by anatomical MRI by measuring tumor volumes. Dynamic MRS of hyperpolarized 13C was used to measure an \"apparent\" pyruvate-to-lactate rate constant (kp) of lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) in vivo. Further, ex vivo pathology and in vitro LDH initial reaction velocity were evaluated. Results: Tamoxifen significantly halted the tumor growth measured as tumor volume by MRI. In the untreated animals, kp correlated with tumor growth. The kP was somewhat but not significantly lower in the treated group. Studies in vitro confirmed the effects of tamoxifen on tumor growth, and here the LDH reaction velocity was reduced significantly in the treated group. Conclusion: These hyperpolarized 13C MRS findings indicate that tumor metabolic changes affects kP. The measured kp did not relate to treatment response to the same extent as did tumor growth, histological evaluation, and in vitro determination of LDH activity. © 2014 Wiley Periodicals, Inc.","author_count":11,"contributors":["Asghar Butt, Sadia","Søgaard, Lise V.","Ardenkjær-Larsen, Jan Henrik","Lauritzen, Mette H.","Engelholm, Lars H.","Paulson, Olaf B.","Mirza, Osman","Holck, Susanne","Magnusson, Peter","Åkeson, Per"],"cuid":["S02-086.07-035050"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"keyword":["Cancer metabolism","Tamoxifen"],"level":"sci","mra":["med","sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"OT":["Danish Research Centre for Magnetic Resonance, Centre for Functional and Diagnostic Imaging and Research"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"monitoring mammary tumor progression and effect of tamoxifen treatment in mmtv pymt using mri and magnetic resonance spectroscopy with hyperpolarized 000000000001 sup 000000000013 sup c pyruvate","src":["dtu","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1002/mrm.25095","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S02-086.07-035050","identifier":[{"id":"24435823","type":"pmid"},{"id":"84919876261","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["dtu-6b174121-acbe-4764-a58b-b2bd288ee389","ku-55870b03-a65b-4797-ba0a-e08b482f9c11"],"keyword":["Cancer metabolism","Hyperpolarized 13C-MRS","MMTV-PymT","Tamoxifen"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science","Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-70D76A2C3ED3DD43C392173C373B476C"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1002/mrm.25095","version":"Other Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Medicine (MED)","Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2015","yearSub":"2014"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"capital region of denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CAP","l3nl":"Capital Region of Denmark","l3nn":"Capital Region of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Capital Region of Denmark","namevar":["Copenhagen University Hospital Herlev and Gentofte"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CAP","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"miscellaneous","l2":"DI-OT","l2n":"Miscellaneous","l3":"DI-OT","l3nl":"Other Research","l3nn":"Miscellaneous","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Miscellaneous","namevar":["Danish Research Centre for Magnetic Resonance, Centre for Functional and Diagnostic Imaging and Research"],"nora_code":"DI-OT","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, Department of Electrical Engineering","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Electrical Engineering, Biomedical Engineering"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1},{"affno":"4","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Sadia","key":"asgharbuttpau","last":"Asghar Butt","name":"Asghar Butt, Sadia","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Lise V.","key":"søgaardpau","last":"Søgaard","name":"Søgaard, Lise V.","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jan Henrik","key":"ardenkjærlarsenpau","last":"Ardenkjær-Larsen","locid":"ae9519c9-1b64-4426-b89f-4c36d08c1836","name":"Ardenkjær-Larsen, Jan Henrik","orcid":"0000-0001-6167-6926","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Mette H.","key":"lauritzenpau","last":"Lauritzen","name":"Lauritzen, Mette H.","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Lars H.","key":"engelholmpau","last":"Engelholm","name":"Engelholm, Lars H.","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4 1","first":"Olaf B.","key":"paulsonpau","last":"Paulson","locid":"d33dae0d-50f2-4819-8768-8e57163ed23c","name":"Paulson, Olaf B.","orcid":"0000-0001-7712-8596","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4 1","first":"Osman Asghar","key":"mirzapau","last":"Mirza","locid":"a6c704a6-62dc-442e-a05b-1b8132166081","name":"Mirza, Osman","name_org":"Mirza, Osman Asghar","orcid":"0000-0001-9435-0690","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4 1","first":"Susanne","key":"holckpau","last":"Holck","locid":"bd4aad62-7596-4684-8c7e-55dcc62b59a1","name":"Holck, Susanne","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Peter","key":"magnussonpau","last":"Magnusson","name":"Magnusson, Peter","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Per","key":"åkesonpau","last":"Åkeson","name":"Åkeson, Per","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Per","key":"akesonpau","last":"Akeson","name":"Akeson, Per","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["24435823"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1002/mrm.25095","issn":["07403194","15222594"],"issn_org":["07403194"],"issue":"1","journal":"Magnetic Resonance in Medicine","pages":"51-58","publisher":"John Wiley and Sons Inc.","status":"p","title":"Magnetic Resonance in Medicine","volume":"73"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84919876261"],"sdg":["3. Good Health and Well-being","All UN SDG Classification"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Miscellaneous","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Miscellaneous","Other Research","Technical University of Denmark","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-6b174121-acbe-4764-a58b-b2bd288ee389","ku-55870b03-a65b-4797-ba0a-e08b482f9c11"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Monitoring mammary tumor progression and effect of tamoxifen treatment in MMTV-PymT using MRI and magnetic resonance spectroscopy with hyperpolarized [1-<sup>13</sup>C]pyruvate","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-086.07-035050","year":"2015","yearsub":2015}
{"abstract":"Metastasis is the main cause of cancer-related deaths and remains the most significant challenge to management of the disease. Metastases are established through a complex multistep process involving intracellular signaling pathways. To gain insight to proteins central to specific steps in metastasis formation, we used a metastasis cell line model that allows investigation of extravasation and colonization of circulating cancer cells to lungs in mice. Using stable isotopic labeling by amino acids in cell culture and subcellular fractionation, the nuclear, cytosol, and mitochondria proteomes were analyzed by LC-MS/MS, identifying a number of proteins that exhibited altered expression in isogenic metastatic versus nonmetastatic cancer cell lines, including NADH-cytochrome b5 reductase 3 (CYB5R3), l-lactate dehydrogenase A (LDHA), Niemann-pick c1 protein (NPC1), and nucleolar RNA helicase 2 (NRH2). The altered expression levels were validated at the protein and transcriptional levels, and analysis of breast cancer biopsies from two cohorts of patients demonstrated a significant correlation between high CYB5R3 expression and poor disease-free and overall survival in patients with estrogen receptor-negative tumors (DFS: p = .02, OS: p = .04). CYB5R3 gene knock-down using siRNA in metastasizing cells led to significantly decreased tumor burden in lungs when injected intravenously in immunodeficient mice. The cellular effects of CYB5R3 knock-down showed signaling alterations associated with extravasation, TGFβ and HIFα pathways, and apoptosis. The decreased apoptosis of CYB5R3 knock-down metastatic cancer cell lines was confirmed in functional assays. Our study reveals a central role of CYB5R3 in extravasation/colonization of cancer cells and demonstrates the ability of our quantitative, comparative proteomic approach to identify key proteins of specific important biological processes that may also prove useful as potential biomarkers of clinical relevance. MS data are available via ProteomeXchange with identifier PXD001391.","author_count":8,"contributors":["Lund, Rikke R","Leth-Larsen, Rikke","Caterino, Tina Di","Terp, Mikkel Green","Nissen, Jeanette","Lænkholm, Anne-Vibeke","Jensen, Ole N.","Ditzel, Henrik J"],"cuid":["S02-086.46-041307"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"keyword":["Animals","Biomarkers Tumor","Breast Neoplasms","Carrier Proteins","Cell Line Tumor","Female","Gene Expression Regulation Neoplastic","Humans","Hypoxia Inducible Factor 1 alpha Subunit","Isoenzymes","Journal Article","L Lactate Dehydrogenase","Membrane Glycoproteins","Mice","Mice SCID","Neoplasm Metastasis","Neoplasm Transplantation","RNA Small Interfering","Receptor erbB 2","Receptors Estrogen","Receptors Nerve Growth Factor","Research Support Non U S Gov t","Signal Transduction","Survival Analysis","Transforming Growth Factor beta"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"nadh cytochrome b000000000005 reductase 000000000003 promotes colonization and metastasis formation and is a prognostic marker of disease free and overall survival in estrogen receptor negative breast cancer","src":["ku","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/129083544/NADH_Cytochrome_b5_Reductase_3_Promotes_Colonization_and_Metastasis_Formation_and_Is_a_Prognostic_Marker_of_Disease_Free_and_Overall_Survival_in_Estrogen_Receptor_Negative_Breast_Cancer.pdf","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-086.46-041307","identifier":[{"id":"PMC4638041","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"84946080481","type":"scopus"},{"id":"26351264","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-31e6fff5-bced-44ba-ad57-68718b8e6e58","sdu-447dda7a-f7d2-4de9-a679-39f8f08647e6"],"keyword":["Animals","Biomarkers, Tumor","Breast Neoplasms","Carrier Proteins","Cell Line, Tumor","Cytochrome-B(5) Reductase","Female","Gene Expression Regulation, Neoplastic","Humans","Hypoxia-Inducible Factor 1, alpha Subunit","Isoenzymes","Journal Article","L-Lactate Dehydrogenase","Membrane Glycoproteins","Mice","Mice, SCID","Neoplasm Metastasis","Neoplasm Transplantation","RNA, Small Interfering","Receptor, ErbB-2","Receptors, Estrogen","Receptors, Nerve Growth Factor","Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","Signal Transduction","Survival Analysis","Transforming Growth Factor beta"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-AC138C230980AC71DF506C185F313522"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1074/mcp.M115.050385","version":"Other Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/129083544/NADH_Cytochrome_b5_Reductase_3_Promotes_Colonization_and_Metastasis_Formation_and_Is_a_Prognostic_Marker_of_Disease_Free_and_Overall_Survival_in_Estrogen_Receptor_Negative_Breast_Cancer.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2015","yearSub":"2015"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Molecular Medicine, Ditzel group"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Rikke R","key":"lundpau","last":"Lund","locid":"d2d19374-e4bd-45f3-87c1-dfbaae8fa898","name":"Lund, Rikke Raaen","name_org":"Lund, Rikke R","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Rikke","key":"lethlarsenpau","last":"Leth-Larsen","locid":"66fd89b9-b81d-4e74-bf02-9a0ea313ee3e","name":"Leth-Larsen, Rikke","orcid":"0000-0002-1612-4089","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Tina Di","key":"caterinopau","last":"Caterino","locid":"7e502925-4608-4db2-8496-d7994371a00a","name":"Caterino, Tina Di","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Mikkel G","key":"terppau","last":"Terp","locid":"31863af3-7d3a-48d5-ae0a-e76e938a23fb","name":"Terp, Mikkel Green","name_org":"Terp, Mikkel G","orcid":"0000-0002-4468-6900","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jeanette","key":"nissenpau","last":"Nissen","locid":"bb297927-6c44-4a2a-b091-69c610251692","name":"Nissen, Jeanette","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 1","first":"Anne-Vibeke","key":"lænkholmpau","last":"Lænkholm","locid":"13a69661-098a-4035-8245-0b75d9011883","name":"Lænkholm, Anne-Vibeke","orcid":"0000-0003-2166-8686","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Ole Nørregaard","key":"jensenpau","last":"Jensen","locid":"d918f237-468a-4785-ab50-d716d3ecb613","name":"Jensen, Ole Nørregaard","orcid":"0000-0003-1862-8528","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Henrik J","key":"ditzelpau","last":"Ditzel","locid":"f10637d7-ca3b-4956-a007-100f66737838","name":"Ditzel, Henrik","name_org":"Ditzel, Henrik J","orcid":"0000-0003-3927-5135","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["26351264"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1074/mcp.m115.050385","issn":["15359476","15359484"],"issn_org":["15359476"],"issue":"11","journal":"Molecular and Cellular Proteomics","pages":"2988-2999","publisher":"American Society for Biochemistry and Molecular Biology Inc.","status":"p","title":"Molecular and Cellular Proteomics","volume":"14"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84946080481"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-31e6fff5-bced-44ba-ad57-68718b8e6e58","sdu-447dda7a-f7d2-4de9-a679-39f8f08647e6"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"NADH-Cytochrome b5 Reductase 3 Promotes Colonization and Metastasis Formation and Is a Prognostic Marker of Disease-Free and Overall Survival in Estrogen Receptor-Negative Breast Cancer","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-086.46-041307","year":"2015","yearsub":2016}
{"abstract":"Immunomodulatory host defense peptides (HDPs) are considered to be lead compounds for novel anti-sepsis and anti-inflammatory agents. However, development of drugs based on HDPs has been hampered by problems with toxicity and low bioavailability due to in vivo proteolysis. Here, a subclass of proteolytically stable HDP mimics consisting of lipidated α-peptide/β-peptoid oligomers was investigated for their effect on neutrophil function. The most promising compound, Pam-(Lys-βNSpe)6-NH2, was shown to inhibit formyl peptide receptor 2 (FPR2) agonist-induced neutrophil granule mobilization and release of reactive oxygen species. The potency of Pam-(Lys-βNSpe)6-NH2 was comparable to that of PBP10, the most potent FPR2-selective inhibitor known. The immunomodulatory effects of structural analogues of Pam-(Lys-βNSpe)6-NH2 emphasized the importance of both the lipid and peptidomimetic parts. By using imaging flow cytometry in primary neutrophils and FPR-transfected cell lines we found that a fluorescently labelled analogue of Pam-(Lys-βNSpe)6-NH2 interacted selectively with FPR2. Furthermore the interaction between Pam-(Lys-βNSpe)6-NH2 and FPR2 was found to prevent binding of the FPR2-specific activating peptide agonist Cy5-WKYMWM, while the binding of a FPR1-selective agonist was not inhibited. To our knowledge, Pam-(Lys-βNSpe)6-NH2 is the first HDP mimic found to inhibit activation of human neutrophils via direct interaction with FPR2. Hence, we consider Pam-(Lys-βNSpe)6-NH2 to be a convenient tool in the further dissection of the role of FPR2 in inflammation and homeostasis as well as for investigation of the importance of neutrophil stimulation in anti-infective therapy involving HDPs.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Skovbakke, Sarah Line","Heegaard, Peter M. H.","Larsen, Camilla J.","Franzyk, Henrik","Forsman, Huamei","Dahlgren, Claes"],"cuid":["S02-098.50-037157"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Sweden"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Gothenburg"],"keyword":["Anti inflammatory","Formyl peptide receptors","G protein coupled receptors","Host defense peptides","Neutrophils"],"level":"sci","mra":["med","sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the proteolytically stable peptidomimetic pam lys ßnspe sub 000000000006 sub nh sub 000000000002 sub selectively inhibits human neutrophil activation via formyl peptide receptor 000000000002","src":["dtu","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bcp.2014.11.004","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S02-098.50-037157","identifier":[{"id":"25462815","type":"pmid"},{"id":"84920718913","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["dtu-ca72f537-82b5-4f42-b8c9-f8eb9989d299","ku-25742f42-83e0-408e-a12e-b0d935b51d7e"],"keyword":["Anti-inflammatory","Formyl peptide receptors","G-protein-coupled receptors","Host defense peptides","Neutrophils"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science","Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-69427DBC07B4B676B723642C60FC98B8"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bcp.2014.11.004","version":"Other Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://backend.orbit.dtu.dk/ws/files/102674783/1_s2.0_S0006295214006613_main.pdf","version":"Other Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2015","yearSub":2014},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Drug Design and Pharmacology, Natural Products and Peptides"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, National Veterinary Institute","Technical University of Denmark, National Veterinary Institute, Section for Immunology and Vaccinology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"se","key":"university of gothenburg","loc":["Sweden","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"University of Gothenburg","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Sarah Line","key":"skovbakkepau","last":"Skovbakke","locid":"6c43699f-79b1-42e1-bc75-5783c8024f41","name":"Skovbakke, Sarah Line","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Peter M. H.","key":"heegaardpau","last":"Heegaard","locid":"f15c1dfb-4275-4369-9b2b-2af936d2e0e6","name":"Heegaard, Peter M. H.","orcid":"0000-0003-4506-4932","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Camilla J.","key":"larsenpau","last":"Larsen","name":"Larsen, Camilla J.","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Henrik","key":"franzykpau","last":"Franzyk","locid":"5120ce68-6886-4ac8-8178-310a47d19261","name":"Franzyk, Henrik","orcid":"0000-0002-2822-1927","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Huamei","key":"forsmanpau","last":"Forsman","name":"Forsman, Huamei","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Claes","key":"dahlgrenpau","last":"Dahlgren","name":"Dahlgren, Claes","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["25462815"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.bcp.2014.11.004","issn":["00062952","18732968"],"issn_org":["00062952"],"issue":"2","journal":"Biochemical Pharmacology","pages":"182-195","publisher":"Elsevier","status":"p","title":"Biochemical Pharmacology","volume":"93"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84920718913"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Technical University of Denmark","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-ca72f537-82b5-4f42-b8c9-f8eb9989d299","ku-25742f42-83e0-408e-a12e-b0d935b51d7e"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"The Proteolytically Stable Peptidomimetic Pam-(Lys-ßNSpe)<sub>6</sub>-NH<sub>2</sub> Selectively Inhibits Human Neutrophil Activation via Formyl Peptide Receptor 2","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-098.50-037157","year":"2015","yearsub":2015}
{"abstract":"OBJECTIVES: An association between infection and arterial thromboembolic events (ATE) has been suggested. Here we examined the risk of myocardial infarction (MI), stroke and other ATE after Staphylococcus aureus bacteremia (SAB). METHODS: Danish register-based nation-wide observational cohort study between 1995 and 2008 with matched control subjects from the general population. RESULTS: Within a year, 278 of 15669 SAB patients and 2570 of 156690 controls developed MI, stroke or another ATE. The incidence rates among SAB patients were highest within the first 30 days and decreased over a year. The adjusted relative risk of MI, stroke and other ATE during the first 30 days after SAB in patients compared to controls were 2.2 (95% CI: 1.6-3.1), 5.5 (95% CI: 3.8-8.3) and 15.5 (95% CI: 6.9-35), respectively. Compared to controls, the increased adjusted relative risk persisted for 30 days for MI, 180 days for stroke and one year for other ATE. Increasing age, hypertension, atrial flutter/fibrillation, prior ATE and endocarditis in SAB patients were associated with an increased risk of ATE. CONCLUSIONS: SAB was associated with a short-term increased risk of ATE that persisted longer dependent on type of event. Studies are warranted to investigate treatment strategies to diminish ATE after SAB.","author_count":10,"contributors":["Mejer, N","Gotland, N","Uhre, M L","Westh, H","Schønheyder, Henrik Carl","Petersen, A","Jensen, A G","Larsen, A R","Skov, R","Benfield, T"],"cuid":["S02-098.68-004751"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"corpau":["Danish Staphylococcal Bacteremia Study Group"],"keyword":["Adolescent","Adult","Aged","Aged 80 and over","Bacteremia","Cohort Studies","Denmark","Female","Humans","Incidence","Male","Middle Aged","Myocardial Infarction","Risk Assessment","Staphylococcal Infections","Stroke","Thromboembolism","Time Factors","Young Adult"],"level":"sci","mra