last":"Nord-Larsen","locid":"d5b3bda9-5a06-45e4-8c9a-07e768bef87a","name":"Nord-Larsen, Thomas","orcid":"0000-0002-5341-6435","rank":13,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"isbn":["9788771560756"],"issn":["2244999X"],"pages":"35","place":"Aarhus","publisher":"Aarhus University, DCE - Danish Centre for Environment and Energy","publisher_org":"Aarhus University, DCE – Danish Centre for Environment and Energy","repno":"Technical Report from DCE – Danish Centre for Environment and Energy","series":"Technical Report from DCE - Danish Centre for Environment and Energy","status":"p"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-7de37feb-c7db-42e5-9d5f-d4c5c73f942b","ku-ba2ebed2-a4f1-445f-8afc-22cf8f8158a1"],"src":["AU Aarhus University","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Estimating land use / land cover changes in Denmark from 1990 - 2012 : Technical documentation for the assessment of land use / land cover changes for estimation of carbon dioxide fixation in soil","type":"Report","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-095.90-021854","year":"2014","yearsub":"2014"}
{"abstract":"PURPOSE: Whiplash trauma in motor vehicle accidents (MVA) may involve various painful soft tissue damages, but weeks/months later a minority of victims still suffers from various long-lasting and disabling symptoms, whiplash-associated disorders (WAD). The etiology is currently unknown, but X-ray-occult fractures may be one cause in some cases. The purpose of this prospective study was to examine the association between occult fractures, as seen on bone single photon emission computed tomography (SPECT), with neck-, head- and arm pain. METHODS: An inception cohort of 107 patients presenting with acute whiplash symptoms following an MVA was invited to have a cervical SPECT shortly post injury and again 6 months later. Associations between occult fractures and pain levels at baseline, 6 and 12 months of follow-up were analyzed. RESULTS: Eighty-eight patients had baseline SPECT performed at median 15 days (range 3-28) post injury, but only 49 patients accepted to have the follow-up SPECT at 6 months. Abnormal SPECT, defined as minimum one area of focal uptake, was seen in 32 patients at baseline, reflecting an occult fracture. Occult fractures were not associated with pain levels, neither at baseline nor at follow-up. CONCLUSION: Occult fractures do not seem to play a role for development of chronic pain after whiplash.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Hertzum-Larsen, Rasmus","Petersen, Henrik","Kasch, Helge","Bendix, Tom"],"cuid":["S03-100.00-016598"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":3,"keyword":["Adult","Bone SPECT","Cohort","Cohort Studies","Female","Follow Up Studies","Headache","Humans","Male","Neck pain","Occult fractures","Prospective Studies","Radiography","Whiplash","Whiplash Injuries complications","Young Adult"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-CDR","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"do x ray occult fractures play a role in chronic pain following a whiplash injury","src":["au","ku","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s00586-014-3362-3","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S03-100.00-016598","identifier":[{"id":"24866255","type":"pmid"},{"id":"84905900089","type":"scopus"},{"id":"000339889300011","type":"wos"}],"ids":["au-3648a03c-0e3a-4c60-92fd-48e87588fe5f","ku-ee9360f0-7497-44aa-aaed-422975ffab3f","sdu-80c339d2-ef51-4161-ac8e-9258023fae6e"],"keyword":["Accidents, Traffic/trends","Adult","Bone SPECT","Chronic Pain/diagnostic imaging","Cohort","Cohort Studies","Female","Follow-Up Studies","Fractures, Closed/complications","Headache","Humans","Male","Neck pain","Occult fractures","Pain Measurement/trends","Prospective Studies","Radiography","Whiplash","Whiplash Injuries/complications","Young Adult"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-6-923AD93D2449FD3C66D36ED282E25DD0"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s00586-014-3362-3","version":"Other Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2014","yearSub":"2014"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"capital region of denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CAP","l3nl":"Capital Region of Denmark","l3nn":"Capital Region of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Capital Region of Denmark","namevar":["National Hospital of Denmark"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CAP","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Public Health, Section of General Practice"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Clinical Research, KI, OUH, Research unit of Clinical Physiology and Nuclear Medicine (Odense)"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1},{"affno":"4","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - The Department of Neurology","Aarhus University, Health, Department of Dentistry and Oral Health, Department of Dentistry and Oral Health - Dentistry"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"5","country":"dk","key":"central denmark region","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CDR","l3nl":"Central Denmark Region","l3nn":"Central Denmark Region","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Central Denmark Region","namevar":["Aarhus University Hospital"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CDR","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Rasmus","key":"hertzumlarsenpau","last":"Hertzum-Larsen","name":"Hertzum-Larsen, Rasmus","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 3","first":"Henrik","key":"petersenpau","last":"Petersen","locid":"d77d330b-9cb2-465a-96ae-d99cb085a7c6","name":"Petersen, Henrik","orcid":"0000-0002-1118-1500","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4 5","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Helge","key":"kaschpau","last":"Kasch","locid":"f78dc707-c440-4d0c-8afb-a43de72ff13c","name":"Kasch, Helge","orcid":"0000-0002-9302-4946","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3 1","corresponding":null,"first":"Tom","key":"bendixpau","last":"Bendix","locid":"c5ab51e5-ff76-46c3-bedb-47b7a4048fe0","name":"Bendix, Tom","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["24866255"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/s00586-014-3362-3","issn":["09406719","14320932"],"issn_org":["09406719"],"issue":"8","journal":"European Spine Journal","pages":"1673-1679","publisher":"Springer","status":"p","title":"European Spine Journal","volume":"23"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84905900089"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","Central Denmark Region","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Universities","University of Copenhagen","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-3648a03c-0e3a-4c60-92fd-48e87588fe5f","ku-ee9360f0-7497-44aa-aaed-422975ffab3f","sdu-80c339d2-ef51-4161-ac8e-9258023fae6e"],"src":["AU Aarhus University","KU University of Copenhagen","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Do X-ray-occult fractures play a role in chronic pain following a whiplash injury?","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S03-100.00-016598","year":"2014","yearsub":"2014"}
{"abstract":"Background: Studies indicate an effect of dietary calcium on change in body weight (BW) and waist circumference (WC), but the results are inconsistent. Furthermore, a relation could depend on genetic predisposition to obesity. Objective: The objective was to examine whether genetic predisposition to higher body mass index (BMI), WC, or waist-hip ratio (WHR) interacts with dietary calcium in relation to subsequent annual change in BW (ΔBW) and WC (ΔWC). Design: The study was based on 7569 individuals from the MONItoring trends and determinants of CArdiovascular disease Study, a sample from the Danish Diet, Cancer and Health Study and the INTER99 study, with information on diet; 54 single-nucleotide polymorphisms (SNPs) associated with BMI, WC, or WHR adjusted for BMI; and potential confounders. The SNPs were combined in 4 scores as indicators of genetic predisposition; all SNPs in a general score and a score for each of 3 phenotypes: BMI, WC, and WHR. Linear regression was used to examine the association between calcium intake and ΔBW or ΔWC adjusted for concurrent ΔBW. SNP score x calcium interactions were examined by adding product terms to the models. Results: We found a significant ΔBW of -0.076 kg (P = 0.021; 95% CI: -0.140, -0.012) per 1000 mg Ca. No significant association was observed between dietary calcium and ΔWC. In the analyses with ΔBW as outcome, we found no significant interactions between the developed predisposition scores and calcium. However, we found a significant interaction between a score of 6 WC-associated SNPs and calcium in relation to ΔWC. Each risk allele was associated with a ΔWC of -0.043 cm (P = 0.038; 95% CI: -0.083, -0.002) per 1000 mg Ca. Conclusions: Our study suggests that dietary calcium relates weakly to BW loss. We found no evidence of a general association between calcium and ΔWC, but calcium may reduce WC among people genetically predisposed to a high WC. However, further replication of this finding is needed.","