{"abstract":"INTRODUCTION: The aim of this study was to examine the use of quantitative fluorescein angiographic analysis as a means of estimating rates of perfusion of the retina in eyes with a circulatory deficit secondary to carotid artery stenosis. MATERIAL AND METHODS: The study included 21 eyes with ocular ischaemic syndrome (OIS) and 20 control eyes from subjects with carotid artery stenosis but without signs of ocular ischaemia. Analyses of video fluorescein angiograms extracting time intervals for the time delay between specific phases of the angiogram were performed. Time delay was compared between groups and in relation to degree of carotid artery stenosis and ocular systolic blood pressure. RESULTS: Among the three flow indices of retinal perfusion (arteriovenous passage time 1 (AVP1), arteriovenous passage time 2 (AVP2) and venous filling time (VP)), those including the venous filling phase were significantly prolonged in the OIS group. Furthermore, AVP2 was delayed by 3 sec. in OIS eyes (16.6 sec. versus 13.6 sec. in controls). VP was 2.4 sec. longer in OIS eyes (11.5 sec. versus 9.1 sec.). We found a significant correlation between AVP2 and ocular perfusion pressure, but no correlation between the degree of carotid artery stenosis and any of the flow indices. CONCLUSION: In a patient population spanning a wide ocular systolic blood pressure range, angiography-based quantitative flowmetry demonstrated a difference between carotid artery stenosis patients with and without OIS and a correlation between flow and ocular perfusion pressure. While angiographic flowmetry proved effective in discriminating between groups of individuals, it can only be used to support the diagnosis of the ocular ischaemic syndrome in patients with extreme flow reduction.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Hansen, Grith Lssrkholm","Kofoed, Peter Kristian","Munch, Inger Christine","Sillesen, Henrik","Jensen, Leif Panduro","Iversen, Helle Klingenberg","Larsen, Michael"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-412529"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Aged","Carotid Stenosis","Eye","Eye diseases","Female","Fluorescein Angiography","Humans","Ischemia","Male","Middle Aged","Regional Blood Flow","Retina","Rheology","Syndrome"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"retinal angiographic blood flowmetry is reduced in the ocular ischaemic syndrome","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-412529","identifier":[{"id":"84885083342","type":"scopus"},{"id":"24083530","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-200a58d7-8a32-4287-8274-80fa0f155308"],"keyword":["Aged","Carotid Stenosis","Eye","Eye Diseases","Female","Fluorescein Angiography","Humans","Ischemia","Male","Middle Aged","Regional Blood Flow","Retina","Rheology","Syndrome"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Local-1-1-52E58020FCDEB2AF995E02B43E9E6CFB"],"oai":{"class":"Blocked","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2013","yearSub":"2013"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"capital region of denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CAP","l3nl":"Capital Region of Denmark","l3nn":"Capital Region of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Capital Region of Denmark","namevar":["Copenhagen University Hospital - Rigshospitalet"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CAP","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Grith Lssrkholm","key":"hansenpau","last":"Hansen","name":"Hansen, Grith Lssrkholm","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Peter Kristian","key":"kofoedpau","last":"Kofoed","locid":"928b5241-67c9-4c79-814b-4f4a26485825","name":"Kofoed, Peter Kristian","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Inger Christine","key":"munchpau","last":"Munch","locid":"a1af1ee5-c4b4-44d8-a7af-b7c1ce0882ac","name":"Munch, Inger Christine","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Henrik","key":"sillesenpau","last":"Sillesen","locid":"de688896-c103-431d-8fe1-d44ed6e3a595","name":"Sillesen, Henrik","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Leif Panduro","key":"jensenpau","last":"Jensen","locid":"8c3d8eec-663c-407b-93ec-eb473d665afa","name":"Jensen, Leif Panduro","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Helle K","key":"iversenpau","last":"Iversen","locid":"02552302-5333-48e0-acde-b9a821eab783","name":"Iversen, Helle Klingenberg","name_org":"Iversen, Helle K","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Michael","key":"larsenpau","last":"Larsen","locid":"1d460214-c0db-4a15-a3a5-360c73746ed4","name":"Larsen, Michael","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["24083530"],"pub":{"issn":["09078916","22451919"],"issn_org":["16039629"],"issue":"10","journal":"Danish Medical Journal","pages":"1-5","paperid":"A4716","status":"p","title":"Danish Medical Journal","title_org":"Danish Medical Bulletin (Online)","volume":"60"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-200a58d7-8a32-4287-8274-80fa0f155308"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Retinal angiographic blood flowmetry is reduced in the ocular ischaemic syndrome","t