{"abstract":"Coil efficiency, defined as the B1 magnetic field induced at a given point on the square root of supplied power P, is an important parameter that characterizes both the transmit and receive performance of the radiofrequency (RF) coil. Maximizing coil efficiency will maximize also the signal-to-noise ratio. In this work, we propose a novel method for RF coil efficiency estimation based on the use of a perturbing loop. The proposed method consists of loading the coil with a known resistor by inductive coupling and measuring the quality factor with and without the load. We tested the method by measuring the efficiency of a 13C birdcage coil tuned at 32.13 MHz and verified its accuracy by comparing the results with the nuclear magnetic resonance nutation experiment. The method allows coil performance characterization in a short time and with great accuracy, and it can be used both on the bench and inside the scanner. (c) 2012 Wiley Periodicals, Inc. Concepts Magn Reson Part B (Magn Reson Engineering) 41B: 139143, 2012","author_count":10,"contributors":["Giovannetti, Giulio","Frijia, Francesca","Hartwig, Valentina","Menichetti, Luca","De Marchi, Daniele","Positano, Vincenzo","Landini, Luigi","Lombardi, Massimo","Santarelli, Maria Filomena","Ardenkjær-Larsen, Jan Henrik"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-313557"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Italy"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","National Research Council of Italy","University of Pisa"],"keyword":["Chemistry","Coil efficiency","Instruments","Physics","Radiofrequency coils","Spectroscopy"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-DTU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"a novel method for coil efficiency estimation validation with a 000000000013c birdcage","src":["dtu"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1002/cmr.b.21223","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-313557","identifier":[{"id":"84867910487","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["dtu-1e0d2ba6-3e4d-4faa-8fcc-54243595c69d"],"keyword":["B1 calibration","CHEMISTRY","INSTRUMENTS","PHYSICS","SPECTROSCOPY","coil efficiency","dual-loop probe","radiofrequency coils"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-31839AC39276D801E9CBAE448A42358A"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1002/cmr.b.21223","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"it","key":"national research council of italy","loc":["Italy","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"National Research Council of Italy","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"it","key":"university of pisa","loc":["Italy","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Pisa","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, Department of Electrical Engineering","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Electrical Engineering, Biomedical Engineering"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Giulio","key":"giovannettipau","last":"Giovannetti","name":"Giovannetti, Giulio","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Francesca","key":"frijiapau","last":"Frijia","name":"Frijia, Francesca","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Valentina","key":"hartwigpau","last":"Hartwig","name":"Hartwig, Valentina","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Luca","key":"menichettipau","last":"Menichetti","name":"Menichetti, Luca","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Daniele","key":"demarchipau","last":"De Marchi","name":"De Marchi, Daniele","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Vincenzo","key":"positanopau","last":"Positano","name":"Positano, Vincenzo","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Luigi","key":"landinipau","last":"Landini","name":"Landini, Luigi","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Massimo","key":"lombardipau","last":"Lombardi","name":"Lombardi, Massimo","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Maria Filomena","key":"santarellipau","last":"Santarelli","name":"Santarelli, Maria Filomena","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jan Henrik","key":"ardenkjærlarsenpau","last":"Ardenkjær-Larsen","locid":"ae9519c9-1b64-4426-b89f-4c36d08c1836","name":"Ardenkjær-Larsen, Jan Henrik","orcid":"0000-0001-6167-6926","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1002/cmr.b.21223","issn":["15525031","1552504X"],"issn_org":["1552504X"],"issue":"4","journal":"Concepts in Magnetic Resonance. Part B: Magnetic Resonance Engineering","pages":"139-143","publisher":"John Wiley & Sons Inc.","publisher_org":"JohnWiley & Sons, Inc.","status":"p","title":"Concepts in Magnetic Resonance. Part B: Magnetic Resonance Engineering","title_org":"Concepts in Magnetic Resonance. Part B: Magnetic Resonance Engineering (Online)","volume":"41B"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84867910487"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Technical University of Denmark","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["dtu-1e0d2ba6-3e4d-4faa-8fcc-54243595c69d"],"src":["DTU Technical University of Denmark"],"title":"A novel method for coil efficiency estimation: Validation with a 13C birdcage","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-313557","year":"2012","yearsub":"2012"}
{"abstract":"Since its discovery in the early 2000s, methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus (MRSA) clonal complex 398 (CC398) has become a rapidly emerging cause of human infections, most often associated with livestock exposure. We applied whole-genome sequence typing to characterize a diverse collection of CC398 isolates (n = 89), including MRSA and methicillin-susceptible S. aureus (MSSA) from animals and humans spanning 19 countries and four continents. We identified 4,238 single nucleotide polymorphisms (SNPs) among the 89 core genomes. Minimal homoplasy (consistency index = 0.9591) was detected among parsimony-informative SNPs, allowing for the generation of a highly accurate phylogenetic reconstruction of the CC398 clonal lineage. Phylogenetic analyses revealed that MSSA from humans formed the most ancestral clades. The most derived lineages were composed predominantly of livestock-associated MRSA possessing three different staphylococcal cassette chromosome mec element (SCCmec) types (IV, V, and VII-like) including nine subtypes. The human-associated isolates from the basal clades carried phages encoding human innate immune modulators that were largely missing among the livestock-associated isolates. Our results strongly suggest that livestock-associated MRSA CC398 originated in humans as MSSA. The lineage appears to have undergone a rapid radiation in conjunction with the jump from humans to livestock, where it subsequently acquired tetracycline and methicillin resistance. Further analyses are required to estimate the number of independent genetic events leading to the methicillin-resistant sublineages, but the diversity of SCCmec subtypes is suggestive of strong and diverse antimicrobial selection associated with food animal production. IMPORTANCE Modern food animal production is characterized by densely concentrated animals and routine antibiotic use, which may facilitate the emergence of novel antibiotic-resistant zoonotic pathogens. Our findings strongly support the idea that livestock-associated MRSA CC398 originated as MSSA in humans. The jump of CC398 from humans to livestock was accompanied by the loss of phage-carried human virulence genes, which likely attenuated its zoonotic potential, but it was also accompanied by the acquisition of tetracycline and methicillin resistance. Our findings exemplify a bidirectional zoonotic exchange and underscore the potential public health risks of widespread antibiotic use in food animal production. IMPORTANCE: Modern food animal production is characterized by densely concentrated animals and routine antibiotic use, which may facilitate the emergence of novel antibiotic-resistant zoonotic pathogens. Our findings strongly support the idea that livestock-associated MRSA CC398 originated as MSSA in humans. The jump of CC398 from humans to livestock was accompanied by the loss of phage-carried human virulence genes, which likely attenuated its zoonotic potential, but it was also accompanied by the acquisition of tetracycline and methicillin resistance. Our findings exemplify a bidirectional zoonotic exchange and underscore the potential public health risks of widespread antibiotic use in food animal production.","author_count":33,"contributors":["Price, Lance B.","Stegger, Marc","Hasman, Henrik","Aziz, Maliha","Larsen, Jesper","Andersen, Paal Skytt","Pearson, Talima","Waters, Andrew E.","Foster, Jeffrey T.","Schupp, James","Gillece, John","Driebe, Elizabeth","Liu, Cindy M.","Springer, Burkhard","Zdovc, Irena","Battisti, Antonio","Franco, Alessia","Zmudzki, Jacek","Schwarz, Stefan","Butaye, Patrick","Jouy, Eric","Pomba, Constanca","Porrero, M. Concepción","Ruimy, Raymond","Smith, Tara C.","Robinson, D. Ashley","Weese, J. Scott","Arriola, Carmen Sofia","Yu, Fangyou","Laurent, Frederic","Keim, Paul","Skov, Robert","Aarestrup, Frank Møller"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-326248"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","America, South","Asia, East","Europe, EU","OECD"],"country":["Austria","Belgium","Canada","China","France","Germany","Italy","Peru","Poland","Portugal","Slovenia","Spain","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Austrian Agency for Health and Food Safety GmbH","Complutense University","First Affiliated Hospital of Wenzhou Medical College","Friedrich-Loeffler-Institute","Ghent University","Interdisciplinary Centre of Research in Animal Health","Istituto Zooprofilattico Sperimentale delle Regioni Lazio e Toscana","National Reference Center for Staphylococci","National Veterinary Research Institute","Northern Arizona University","Translational Genomics Research Institute","Universidad Nacional Mayor de San Marcos","University of Guelph","University of Iowa","University of Ljubljana","University of Mississippi","Université Paris Cité"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-U"],"level3":{"GV":["DI-GV-ALL","DI-GV-SSI"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU"]},"level3all":["DI-GV-SSI","DI-U-DTU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"staphylococcus aureus cc000000000398 host adaptation and emergence of methicillin resistance in livestock","src":["dtu"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1128/mBio.00305-11","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-326248","identifier":[{"id":"84863242652","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["dtu-48e824d7-466e-4e89-bd42-dc57b33236e3"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-4-7-1C2A7D28AA99CD600BB076A2E84CF813"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1128/mBio.00305-11","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"us","key":"translational genomics research institute","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Translational Genomics Research Institute","us