34-4a24-9f98-4955b70c628f"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Semiotik","type":"Book Preface, Encycl. Entry","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-254414","year":"2011","yearsub":"2011"}
{"abstract":"This chapter argues that orthodox career thinking–which focuses on vertical progression to higher-level managerial positions—is suffering from three shortcomings. First, it is insufficient to explain career dynamics in modern knowledge organizations. Second, it does not see strategic organizational change as a catalyst for career and disregards the importance of experiential learning on the job. Third, it does not incorporate how career is embedded in the organizational and cultural context, including a wide range of national, institutional features. Based on this, the chapter suggests that we move the focus from narrow career thinking to the more broad-banded concept of talent. The talent concept signifies any kind of outstanding competence of an individual (whether it is managerial or any kind of significant specialist field) which is strategically important to the organization, difficult to achieve, difficult to replace by other types of resources, and difficult to replicate by competitors. Also, a broader definition of how talent can be developed is needed, as it should encompass informal and experiential methods as well as formal education. The broader concept of talent is discussed in relation to the Scandinavian context, which consists of many highly educated knowledge workers.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Holt Larsen, Henrik","Schramm-Nielsen, Jette","Stensaker, Inger"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-276303"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Norway"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Norwegian School of Economics"],"keyword":["Career development","Experiential learning","Lifelong learning","Scandinavia","Talent development"],"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-CBS"]},"level3all":["DI-U-CBS"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"talent development as an alternative to orthodox career thinking the scandinavian case","src":["cbs"],"src-code":["DI-U-CBS"],"type":"dba","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-276303","ids":["cbs-09f1e20e-4fda-4dd1-814a-ab9ed65c430b"],"keyword":["Career development","Experiential learning","Lifelong learning","Scandinavia","Talent development"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-B4D7F64CB0E7456246C2DFE46FF3CE94"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"cbs copenhagen business school","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-CBS","l3nl":"Copenhagen Business School","l3nn":"CBS Copenhagen Business School","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"CBS Copenhagen Business School","namevar":["Copenhagen Business School, Department of Organization"],"nora_code":"DI-U-CBS","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"no","key":"norwegian school of economics","loc":["Norway","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Norwegian School of Economics","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Henrik","key":"holtlarsenpau","last":"Holt Larsen","locid":"8ae11131-8aa8-43f3-a839-8e8910f1cf07","name":"Holt Larsen, Henrik","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Jette","key":"schrammnielsenpau","last":"Schramm-Nielsen","locid":"b2deec26-952b-4521-aca4-1983118f7160","name":"Schramm-Nielsen, Jette","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Inger","key":"stensakerpau","last":"Stensaker","name":"Stensaker, Inger","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Manuel","key":"londonpedh","last":"London","name":"London, Manuel","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"isbn":["9780195390483"],"pages":"329-344","place":"Oxford","publisher":"Oxford University Press","series":"Oxford Library of Psychology","status":"p","title":"The Oxford Handbook of Lifelong Learning"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID