{"abstract":"TO THE EDITOR: Enterococcus faecalis, which exists commensally in the gut in warm-blooded animals and humans, is an opportunistic pathogen that causes a variety of community-acquired and health care-associated infections, such as urinary tract and intraabdominal infections, bacteremia, and endocarditis (1). Only a few studies have assessed the relationships between clinical E. faecalis strains; strains endemic to the health care setting; and community strains residing in humans, animals, or animal-origin food (2).","author_count":13,"contributors":["Larsen, Jesper","Schønheyder, Henrik Carl","Singh, Kavindra V","Lester, Camilla H","Olsen, Stefan","Porsbo, Lone Jannok","Garcia-Migura, Lourdes","Jensen, Lars Birk","Bisgaard, Magne","Murray, Barbara E","Hammerum, Anette Marie"],"cuid":["S02-095.51-020863"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"country":["United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Texas at Dallas"],"level":"sci","mra":["med","sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"GV":["DI-GV-ALL","DI-GV-SSI"],"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CDR"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-GV-SSI","DI-HO-CDR","DI-U-AU","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"porcine and human community reservoirs of enterococcus faecalis denmark","src":["au","dtu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-DTU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3201/eid1712.101584","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S02-095.51-020863","identifier":[{"id":"84930475470","type":"scopus"},{"id":"22172303","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC3311169","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["au-6ffa62c6-84fa-491a-8e36-4e12af69e616","dtu-620047a4-014e-4e62-80d8-42917c0882cb"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","m